window.SCIENCE_CURRICULUM_10 = [{"id":"chemreactions10","index":0,"name":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","subtitle":"Science Chapter 1","emoji":"🧪","palette":{"from":"#f87171","to":"#7f1d1d"},"lessons":[{"id":"cr1","title":"First Spark: Word Equations","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Alchemist's Tower, a dazzling white flame erupts from a strip of magnesium ribbon. To pass through the sealed door, you must learn to write down exactly what vanished and what appeared — the ancient language of word equations.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Chemical Reaction?","body":"Whenever a chemical change occurs, a chemical reaction has taken place. You can tell one happened from clues like a change in state, a change in colour, evolution of a gas, or a change in temperature.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Reactants and Products","body":"The substances that undergo change in a reaction are called reactants, and the new substances formed are called products. When magnesium burns in air, magnesium and oxygen are the reactants and magnesium oxide is the product.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Writing a Word Equation","body":"A word-equation shows reactants changing into products through an arrow, with reactants on the left and products on the right, each separated by a plus sign. For burning magnesium: Magnesium + Oxygen → Magnesium oxide.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Chemical Formula Equations","body":"A chemical equation uses formulae instead of words to represent a reaction more concisely, for example Mg + O2 → MgO. If an equation has not yet been balanced, it is called a skeletal chemical equation.","visual":"🔤"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a clue listed in the text that a chemical reaction has taken place?","options":["Evolution of a gas","The object gets heavier for no reason","The object becomes see-through","Nothing changes at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text lists change of state, change of colour, evolution of a gas, and change of temperature as clues to a chemical reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 'Magnesium + Oxygen → Magnesium oxide', what is magnesium oxide?","options":["The product","A reactant","The catalyst","The physical state"],"answer":0,"explain":"Magnesium oxide is the new substance formed, so it is the product, written on the right of the arrow."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the skeletal chemical equation for burning magnesium in air?","options":["Mg + O2 → MgO","MgO → Mg + O2","Mg2 + O → MgO2","Mg + O2 → Mg2O"],"answer":0,"explain":"The skeletal (unbalanced) equation using formulae is Mg + O2 → MgO."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is Mg + O2 → MgO called a 'skeletal' equation?","options":["It is not yet balanced","It has no reactants","It has three products","It uses only words"],"answer":0,"explain":"A skeletal equation is one where the atoms have not yet been counted and balanced on both sides."}]},{"id":"cr2","title":"The Law of Conservation Puzzle","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"The Guardian of the Iron Gate demands proof that mass is never created nor destroyed. Only a properly balanced equation — atom for atom — will make the rusted chains fall away.","teach":[{"title":"Law of Conservation of Mass","body":"Mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction, so the total mass of elements in the products must equal the total mass of elements in the reactants. This means the number of atoms of each element must stay the same before and after the reaction.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Balanced vs Unbalanced Equations","body":"If the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides of the arrow, the equation is balanced, as in Zn + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2. If not, mass is not equal on both sides and the equation must be balanced.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"The Hit-and-Trial Method","body":"To balance an equation, list the atoms of each element on both sides and add whole-number coefficients in front of formulas — without ever changing the formulas themselves — until every element matches. This trial method turns Fe + H2O → Fe3O4 + H2 into 3Fe + 4H2O → Fe3O4 + 4H2.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Start With the Biggest Molecule","body":"It is convenient to begin balancing with the compound that has the maximum number of atoms, choosing the element with the most atoms in it first, then working through the remaining unbalanced elements one at a time.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the balanced equation 3Fe + 4H2O → Fe3O4 + 4H2, what number goes in front of Fe?","answer":3,"explain":"Three iron atoms on the left balance the three iron atoms found in Fe3O4 on the right."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the same balanced equation, what coefficient is needed in front of H2O?","answer":4,"explain":"Four water molecules supply the four oxygen atoms needed for Fe3O4 and the hydrogen needed for 4H2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"And what coefficient is needed in front of H2 on the product side?","answer":4,"explain":"Four H2O molecules give 8 hydrogen atoms, which need 4 H2 molecules on the product side to balance."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't we fix an unbalanced equation by changing H2O to H2O4?","options":["It would wrongly change the formula of a real substance","It uses too much ink","Oxygen would disappear","It is only allowed for metals"],"answer":0,"explain":"While balancing, we cannot alter the formula of any compound or element — only add coefficients in front of it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the trial method of adding coefficients to balance equations called?","options":["Hit-and-trial method","Guess-and-forget method","Skeleton method","Precipitation method"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text names this step-by-step balancing approach the hit-and-trial method."}]},{"id":"cr3","title":"Marks of State: Reading the Full Equation","icon":"💧","kind":"lesson","story":"The map to the Chlorophyll Grove is written in an old notation — tiny letters (s), (l), (g), (aq) tucked beside each formula, and strange words like 'Sunlight' hovering over an arrow. Decode them to continue.","teach":[{"title":"State Symbols","body":"To make an equation more informative, the physical states of reactants and products are written next to their formulae: (s) for solid, (l) for liquid, (g) for gas, and (aq) for aqueous, meaning dissolved in water.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Steam Counts as Gas","body":"In 3Fe(s) + 4H2O(g) → Fe3O4(s) + 4H2(g), the (g) next to H2O shows that water is used in the form of steam in this particular reaction, not liquid water.","visual":"♨️"},{"title":"Conditions Above the Arrow","body":"Reaction conditions such as temperature, pressure, or a catalyst are sometimes written above or below the arrow, for example CO(g) + 2H2(g) forming CH3OH(l) under a pressure of 340 atm.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Photosynthesis as a Chemical Equation","body":"Even photosynthesis can be written this way: 6CO2(aq) + 12H2O(l) forms C6H12O6(aq) + 6O2(aq) + 6H2O(l), with sunlight and chlorophyll written above the arrow as the conditions needed for the reaction.","visual":"🌿"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which state symbol is used for a substance dissolved in water?","options":["(aq)","(s)","(g)","(l)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The word aqueous (aq) is written when the reactant or product is present as a solution in water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 3Fe(s) + 4H2O(g) → Fe3O4(s) + 4H2(g), why is H2O marked (g) instead of (l)?","options":["Because the water is present as steam in this reaction","Because iron turns water into gas","Because (g) is a typing mistake","Because oxygen is a gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text notes that the symbol (g) is used with H2O here to show water is used in the form of steam."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the equation for making methanol, what is written above the arrow?","options":["The pressure condition, 340 atm","The colour of methanol","The mass of carbon monoxide","The name of the container"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reaction conditions like pressure are written above the arrow, and here it is 340 atm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What two conditions are written above the arrow for photosynthesis?","options":["Sunlight and chlorophyll","Heat and electricity","Pressure and a catalyst","Acid and water"],"answer":0,"explain":"The photosynthesis equation shows Sunlight and Chlorophyll as the conditions above the arrow."}]},{"id":"cr4","title":"Fusion Chamber: Combination Reactions","icon":"🌋","kind":"lesson","story":"In the Fusion Chamber, two or more substances must merge into a single new substance to unlock the next passage — and the chamber grows warm as heat pours out, just as it did when quicklime met water.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Combination Reaction?","body":"When two or more substances, whether elements or compounds, combine to form a single product, the reaction is called a combination reaction. Calcium oxide reacting with water to form calcium hydroxide is one example.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Quicklime Meets Water","body":"Calcium oxide (quick lime) reacts vigorously with water to produce calcium hydroxide (slaked lime), releasing a large amount of heat: CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(aq) + Heat.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"More Combination Reactions","body":"Burning of coal, C(s) + O2(g) → CO2(g), and the formation of water, 2H2(g) + O2(g) → 2H2O(l), are further examples of combination reactions.","visual":"⚫"},{"title":"Exothermic Reactions","body":"Reactions in which heat is released along with the formation of products are called exothermic reactions. Burning of natural gas and respiration, where glucose combines with oxygen in our cells to release energy, are both exothermic.","visual":"🌋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a combination reaction?","options":["Two or more substances combine to form a single product","A single substance breaks into two or more products","One element replaces another in a compound","Ions are exchanged between two compounds"],"answer":0,"explain":"A combination reaction is one where two or more reactants join to form a single product."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(aq) + Heat shows which two things happening at once?","options":["A combination reaction that is also exothermic","A decomposition reaction that is also endothermic","A displacement reaction only","A double displacement reaction only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two substances combine into one product (combination) while releasing heat (exothermic)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is respiration considered an exothermic reaction?","options":["Because glucose combines with oxygen in cells and releases energy","Because it absorbs heat from the surroundings","Because it produces a precipitate","Because it needs sunlight to occur"],"answer":0,"explain":"In respiration, glucose combines with oxygen in body cells and provides energy, releasing it as an exothermic process."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the balanced equation for burning coal, C + O2 → CO2, what coefficient goes in front of O2?","answer":1,"explain":"C(s) + O2(g) → CO2(g) is already balanced with one molecule of O2."}]},{"id":"cr5","title":"The Shattering Chamber: Decomposition Reactions","icon":"💥","kind":"lesson","story":"A single glowing crystal must be shattered into its simpler parts to reveal the hidden key — but shattering it demands energy, whether from roaring heat, blinding light, or crackling electricity.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Decomposition Reaction?","body":"In a decomposition reaction, a single reactant breaks down to give two or more simpler products. Decomposition reactions are the opposite of combination reactions.","visual":"💥"},{"title":"Thermal Decomposition","body":"When a decomposition reaction is carried out by heating, it is called thermal decomposition. Heating ferrous sulphate crystals gives ferric oxide, sulphur dioxide and sulphur trioxide (2FeSO4(s) → Fe2O3(s) + SO2(g) + SO3(g)), and heating limestone gives quick lime and carbon dioxide (CaCO3(s) → CaO(s) + CO2(g)).","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Decomposition by Light and Electricity","body":"Silver chloride decomposes into silver and chlorine in sunlight, 2AgCl(s) → 2Ag(s) + Cl2(g), which is used in black and white photography. Passing electricity through water (electrolysis) decomposes it into hydrogen and oxygen gas.","visual":"☀️"},{"title":"Endothermic Reactions","body":"Decomposition reactions require energy in the form of heat, light, or electricity to break down the reactants. Reactions in which energy is absorbed are called endothermic reactions.","visual":"❄️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a decomposition reaction?","options":["A single reactant breaks down into two or more simpler products","Two substances combine into one product","One metal displaces another from a solution","Two compounds swap ions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Decomposition reactions are the opposite of combination reactions: one reactant breaks into simpler products."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is thermal decomposition?","options":["A decomposition reaction carried out by heating","A decomposition reaction carried out with sunlight","A combination reaction using a catalyst","A displacement reaction in water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thermal decomposition specifically refers to decomposition brought about by heating."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"2AgCl(s) → 2Ag(s) + Cl2(g) happening in sunlight is the basis of which technology mentioned in the source?","options":["Black and white photography","Whitewashing walls","Making cement","Flushing chip packets with nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states that the light-driven decomposition of silver salts like AgCl and AgBr is used in black and white photography."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are decomposition reactions generally described as needing energy input?","options":["They need to absorb energy such as heat, light or electricity to break bonds","They always release a gas that glows","They always produce a precipitate","They never involve oxygen"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text explains decomposition reactions require energy (heat, light, or electricity) to break down the reactants, making them endothermic."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In 2Pb(NO3)2 → 2PbO + 4NO2 + O2, what coefficient is needed in front of NO2?","answer":4,"explain":"Heating lead nitrate gives 2PbO + 4NO2 + O2 as the balanced products."}]},{"id":"cr6","title":"Trial of Exchange: Displacement Duels","icon":"🗡️","kind":"lesson","story":"Two rival elements face off in the Copper Sulphate Arena — one must be forced out of its compound and replaced. Then, in the double displacement chamber, whole partnerships of ions must swap places to form a solid precipitate.","teach":[{"title":"Displacement Reaction","body":"When a more reactive element displaces a less reactive element from its compound, it is called a displacement reaction. Iron displaces copper from copper sulphate solution: Fe(s) + CuSO4(aq) → FeSO4(aq) + Cu(s), turning the nail brownish and fading the blue colour.","visual":"🗡️"},{"title":"More Reactive Metals Win","body":"Zinc and lead are more reactive elements than copper, so they too displace copper from its compounds: Zn(s) + CuSO4(aq) → ZnSO4(aq) + Cu(s), and Pb(s) + CuCl2(aq) → PbCl2(aq) + Cu(s).","visual":"⚙️"},{"title":"Double Displacement Reaction","body":"In a double displacement reaction, there is an exchange of ions between the reactants. Mixing sodium sulphate and barium chloride solutions gives Na2SO4(aq) + BaCl2(aq) → BaSO4(s) + 2NaCl(aq).","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Precipitation Reactions","body":"Any reaction that produces an insoluble solid, called a precipitate, can be called a precipitation reaction. The white, insoluble barium sulphate formed above is a precipitate.","visual":"❕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Fe(s) + CuSO4(aq) → FeSO4(aq) + Cu(s) is an example of which type of reaction?","options":["Displacement reaction","Combination reaction","Decomposition reaction","Double displacement reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Iron displaces copper from copper sulphate solution, making this a displacement reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do zinc and lead displace copper from its compounds?","options":["Because zinc and lead are more reactive than copper","Because copper is more reactive than zinc and lead","Because copper sulphate is a solid","Because zinc and lead are gases"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states zinc and lead are more reactive elements than copper, so they displace it from its compounds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens when sodium sulphate solution is mixed with barium chloride solution?","options":["A white insoluble precipitate of barium sulphate forms","A brown gas is released","The solution turns green","Nothing happens"],"answer":0,"explain":"The SO4(2-) and Ba(2+) ions combine to form the insoluble white precipitate barium sulphate, while sodium chloride stays dissolved."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What do we call a reaction in which an insoluble solid forms from two solutions?","options":["A precipitation reaction","A combustion reaction","A thermal decomposition reaction","An electrolytic reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Any reaction that produces a precipitate can be called a precipitation reaction."}]},{"id":"cr7","title":"Redox Rift: Gain and Loss","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"At the Redox Rift, every substance must declare what it gained and what it lost — oxygen, hydrogen, or both — before the bridge will reform beneath your feet.","teach":[{"title":"Oxidation and Reduction, Defined","body":"If a substance gains oxygen during a reaction, it is oxidised; if it loses oxygen, it is reduced. More fully, gaining oxygen or losing hydrogen means oxidation, while losing oxygen or gaining hydrogen means reduction.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Copper Turns Black, Then Brown Again","body":"Heating copper powder forms black copper(II) oxide as copper is oxidised: 2Cu + O2 → 2CuO. Passing hydrogen gas over the heated CuO reverses this, turning it brown again as copper forms: CuO + H2 → Cu + H2O, where CuO is reduced and hydrogen is oxidised.","visual":"🟫"},{"title":"Redox Reactions","body":"When one reactant in a reaction is oxidised while the other is reduced at the same time, the reaction is called an oxidation-reduction reaction, or redox reaction for short.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"More Redox Examples","body":"In ZnO + C → Zn + CO, carbon is oxidised to CO while ZnO is reduced to zinc. In MnO2 + 4HCl → MnCl2 + 2H2O + Cl2, hydrochloric acid is oxidised to chlorine while manganese dioxide is reduced to manganese chloride.","visual":"🧫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 2Cu + O2 → 2CuO, what happens to copper?","options":["Copper is oxidised because it gains oxygen","Copper is reduced because it loses oxygen","Copper is neither oxidised nor reduced","Copper displaces oxygen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copper gains oxygen to form copper(II) oxide, so it is oxidised."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In CuO + H2 → Cu + H2O, what happens to CuO?","options":["CuO is reduced because it loses oxygen","CuO is oxidised because it gains oxygen","CuO is displaced by hydrogen","CuO acts as a precipitate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copper(II) oxide loses its oxygen to hydrogen and is reduced to copper."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a redox reaction?","options":["A reaction in which one reactant is oxidised while another is reduced","A reaction that only ever produces gases","A reaction between two acids","A reaction that never involves oxygen"],"answer":0,"explain":"A redox (oxidation-reduction) reaction is one where oxidation and reduction happen together in the same reaction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the balanced equation MnO2 + 4HCl → MnCl2 + 2H2O + Cl2, what coefficient is needed in front of HCl?","answer":4,"explain":"Four HCl molecules are needed to balance the chlorine and hydrogen atoms in this redox reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In ZnO + C → Zn + CO, which substance is oxidised?","options":["Carbon, because it gains oxygen to form CO","Zinc oxide, because it gains oxygen","Zinc, because it loses oxygen","Carbon monoxide, because it loses oxygen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carbon gains oxygen to become carbon monoxide, so carbon is oxidised while ZnO is reduced to zinc."}]},{"id":"cr8","title":"Slow Decay: Corrosion and Rancidity","icon":"🦠","kind":"lesson","story":"Time itself is the final trap here — a shiny iron gate has turned reddish-brown, and a sealed jar of oil smells wrong. Understand how oxidation eats away at metals and food over time, and you will know how to stop it.","teach":[{"title":"Corrosion","body":"When a metal is attacked by substances around it such as moisture or acids, it is said to corrode, and this process is called corrosion. Rusting of iron, the black coating on silver, and the green coating on copper are all examples of corrosion.","visual":"🦠"},{"title":"Why Corrosion Matters","body":"Corrosion damages car bodies, bridges, iron railings, ships, and other metal objects, especially those made of iron, and huge amounts of money are spent every year to replace damaged iron.","visual":"🚢"},{"title":"Rancidity","body":"When fats and oils are oxidised, they become rancid, and their smell and taste change. This is why oil and fat containing foods can start to smell or taste unpleasant after being left for a long time.","visual":"🥫"},{"title":"Preventing Rancidity","body":"Adding antioxidants (substances that prevent oxidation) to foods containing fats and oils, storing food in air-tight containers, and flushing chip packets with nitrogen gas all help slow down oxidation and keep food from turning rancid.","visual":"🛡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is corrosion?","options":["A metal being attacked and worn away by moisture, acids, or other substances around it","A metal combining with another metal to form an alloy","A metal being cooled rapidly","A metal reacting with a base to form a precipitate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Corrosion happens when a metal is attacked by substances around it, such as moisture or acids."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to fats and oils when they become rancid?","options":["They are oxidised, and their smell and taste change","They are reduced and lose all colour","They undergo double displacement","They turn into a precipitate"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text explains that oxidised fats and oils become rancid, changing their smell and taste."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do chip manufacturers flush packets with nitrogen gas?","options":["To prevent the chips from getting oxidised and turning rancid","To make the chips crunchier","To add flavour to the chips","To speed up rusting of the packet"],"answer":0,"explain":"Flushing with nitrogen gas keeps oxygen away from the chips, slowing oxidation and rancidity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is given in the source as an example of corrosion?","options":["Rusting of iron","Ripening of fruit","Boiling of water","Dissolving of sugar in water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rusting of iron is explicitly named in the text as a common example of corrosion."}]},{"id":"cr_boss","title":"The Alchemist's Final Trial","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the tower, Corrodius the Rust Dragon guards the exit, its scales flaking with oxide and its breath thick with the smell of rancid oil. Only a true master of chemical reactions and equations can pass.","boss":{"name":"Corrodius, the Rust Dragon","taunt":"Your equations are as unbalanced as my crumbling scales! Face every reaction you have learned, or be reduced to dust like the metal I have corroded!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything You've Learned","body":"A balanced chemical equation shows reactants becoming products with equal atoms of each element on both sides, and every reaction you've studied — combination, decomposition, displacement, double displacement, and redox — fits this rule.","visual":"📖"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What must always be true of a correctly written chemical equation?","options":["It must be balanced, with equal atoms of each element on both sides","It must contain at least one gas","It must have more products than reactants","It must be written only in words"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equations must always be balanced, because mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a reaction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the balanced equation 3Fe + 4H2O → Fe3O4 + 4H2, what coefficient goes in front of H2O?","answer":4,"explain":"Four water molecules are needed to balance the oxygen and hydrogen atoms in this reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(aq) + Heat is what type of reaction?","options":["A combination reaction","A decomposition reaction","A displacement reaction","A double displacement reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two reactants, calcium oxide and water, combine to form a single product, calcium hydroxide."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"2FeSO4(s) → Fe2O3(s) + SO2(g) + SO3(g) on heating is an example of what?","options":["Thermal decomposition","Combination reaction","Displacement reaction","Redox precipitation"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single reactant, ferrous sulphate, breaks down into several products on heating, making this thermal decomposition."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Fe(s) + CuSO4(aq) → FeSO4(aq) + Cu(s) is what type of reaction?","options":["Displacement reaction","Combination reaction","Decomposition reaction","Double displacement reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Iron displaces copper from copper sulphate solution, so this is a displacement reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Na2SO4(aq) + BaCl2(aq) → BaSO4(s) + 2NaCl(aq) is an example of what?","options":["A double displacement (precipitation) reaction","A combination reaction","A thermal decomposition","A redox reaction only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ions are exchanged between the two reactants and an insoluble precipitate, barium sulphate, forms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 2Cu + O2 → 2CuO, copper is...","options":["Oxidised, because it gains oxygen","Reduced, because it loses oxygen","Displaced by oxygen","Unaffected by the reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copper gains oxygen to form copper(II) oxide, which means copper is oxidised."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What links rusting of iron and rancidity of food?","options":["Both are caused by oxidation","Both are combination reactions only","Both require sunlight only","Both are examples of double displacement"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rusting (corrosion) and rancidity are both effects of oxidation reactions in everyday life."}]}]},{"id":"acidsbasessalts10","index":1,"name":"Acids, Bases and Salts","subtitle":"Science Chapter 2","emoji":"⚗️","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"ab1","title":"The Alchemist's Indicators","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"You've inherited a dusty potion cabinet with no labels, only three test tubes: water, an acid, and a base. The old alchemist's notes say a single strip of red litmus paper is all you need to unmask them.","teach":[{"title":"Acid-base indicators","body":"Acid-base indicators are dyes, or mixtures of dyes, that change colour to reveal whether a substance is acidic or basic. Litmus, a purple dye extracted from lichen, is the most common natural indicator.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Litmus tells the tale","body":"Acids turn blue litmus red, while bases turn red litmus blue. In a neutral solution, litmus stays its natural purple colour.","visual":"🔴"},{"title":"Synthetic indicators","body":"Phenolphthalein stays colourless in acids but turns pink in bases. Methyl orange turns red in acids but yellow in bases.","visual":"🌸"},{"title":"Olfactory indicators","body":"Some substances reveal acids or bases through smell instead of colour. Vanilla essence and clove oil lose their characteristic smell when mixed with a base like NaOH, but keep it with an acid like HCl.","visual":"👃"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which natural dye, extracted from lichen, is the most commonly used acid-base indicator?","options":["Litmus","Turmeric","Phenolphthalein","Methyl orange"],"answer":0,"explain":"Litmus solution is a purple dye from lichen, turning red in acids and blue in bases."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What colour does blue litmus solution turn when a drop of acid is added?","options":["Red","Blue","Purple","Yellow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acids turn blue litmus red; bases turn red litmus blue."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Phenolphthalein turns pink when it is added to...","options":["a base","an acid","distilled water","a neutral salt"],"answer":0,"explain":"Phenolphthalein is colourless in acid and pink in basic solutions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Indicators such as vanilla essence and clove oil, which reveal acids or bases through a change in smell, are called","options":["olfactory indicators","visual indicators","synthetic indicators","universal indicators"],"answer":0,"explain":"Olfactory indicators change odour in acidic or basic media instead of changing colour."}]},{"id":"ab2","title":"Bridge of Molten Metal","icon":"⚙️","kind":"lesson","story":"A bridge of zinc and copper blocks blocks your path across the Acid Gorge. Each block reacts differently — some hiss and bubble hydrogen gas, others dissolve into coloured pools — and only the right reaction will let you cross safely.","teach":[{"title":"Acid + Metal","body":"When an acid reacts with a metal, it displaces hydrogen from the acid as hydrogen gas and forms a salt: Acid + Metal → Salt + Hydrogen gas. For example, zinc granules react with dilute sulphuric acid, releasing bubbles of hydrogen gas.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Testing for hydrogen gas","body":"The gas evolved is passed through soap solution to form bubbles, and a burning candle brought near a gas-filled bubble makes it burn with a pop — confirming the gas is hydrogen.","visual":"🎈"},{"title":"A metal can react with a base too","body":"Zinc also reacts with warm sodium hydroxide solution, releasing hydrogen gas and forming sodium zincate: 2NaOH(aq) + Zn(s) → Na2ZnO2(s) + H2(g). Such reactions do not happen with all metals.","visual":"⚙️"},{"title":"Metal oxides act like bases","body":"Copper oxide dissolves in dilute hydrochloric acid, turning the solution blue-green as copper(II) chloride forms: Metal oxide + Acid → Salt + Water. Because metal oxides react with acids just as bases do, they are called basic oxides.","visual":"🟦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When zinc granules react with dilute sulphuric acid, which gas is evolved?","options":["Hydrogen","Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Chlorine"],"answer":0,"explain":"The metal displaces hydrogen from the acid, and a salt (zinc sulphate) also forms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Zinc reacts with warm sodium hydroxide solution to release hydrogen gas and form","options":["sodium zincate","zinc chloride","zinc oxide","sodium hydroxide again"],"answer":0,"explain":"2NaOH(aq) + Zn(s) → Na2ZnO2 (sodium zincate) + H2(g)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Copper oxide dissolves in dilute hydrochloric acid to give a blue-green solution of","options":["copper(II) chloride","copper sulphate","copper hydroxide","copper carbonate"],"answer":0,"explain":"CuO + HCl forms copper(II) chloride, giving the blue-green colour, plus water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Because metal oxides react with acids to give salt and water, just like a base does, metallic oxides are called","options":["basic oxides","acidic oxides","neutral oxides","amphoteric oxides"],"answer":0,"explain":"Metal oxide + Acid → Salt + Water, the same pattern as a base reacting with an acid."}]},{"id":"ab3","title":"The Lime-Water Oracle","icon":"🌫️","kind":"lesson","story":"An oracle guards a cave behind a lake of raw acid. To earn passage, you must summon a gas that clouds her sacred lime-water bowl, then calm the acid lake itself with the right basic potion.","teach":[{"title":"Carbonates and bicarbonates react with acids","body":"Metal carbonates and metal hydrogencarbonates react with acids to give the corresponding salt, carbon dioxide gas, and water. For example, both sodium carbonate and sodium hydrogencarbonate react with dilute HCl to release CO2.","visual":"🫧"},{"title":"Testing carbon dioxide with lime water","body":"The carbon dioxide gas produced turns lime water (calcium hydroxide solution) milky, forming a white precipitate of calcium carbonate: Ca(OH)2 + CO2 → CaCO3 + H2O. Passing in excess CO2 turns it clear again as soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate forms.","visual":"🌫️"},{"title":"Neutralisation reaction","body":"An acid and a base cancel out each other's effect to give a salt and water: NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H2O(l). In general, Base + Acid → Salt + Water is called a neutralisation reaction.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Non-metallic oxides are acidic","body":"Calcium hydroxide, a base, reacts with carbon dioxide to produce a salt and water — the same pattern as an acid-base reaction. This shows that non-metallic oxides, like carbon dioxide, are acidic in nature.","visual":"☁️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sodium carbonate reacts with dilute HCl to release a gas that turns lime water milky. That gas is","options":["carbon dioxide","hydrogen","oxygen","chlorine"],"answer":0,"explain":"Metal carbonates and hydrogencarbonates release CO2 when they react with acids."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The reaction NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H2O(l) is an example of a","options":["neutralisation reaction","combustion reaction","displacement reaction","decomposition reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"An acid and a base reacting to form a salt and water is called neutralisation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Passing excess carbon dioxide through milky lime water turns it clear again by forming soluble","options":["calcium hydrogencarbonate","calcium carbonate","calcium oxide","calcium chloride"],"answer":0,"explain":"CaCO3(s) + H2O + CO2 → Ca(HCO3)2(aq), which is soluble in water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Since carbon dioxide reacts with a base (lime water) just as an acid would, non-metallic oxides are said to be","options":["acidic in nature","basic in nature","always neutral","amphoteric"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-metallic oxides behave like acids when reacting with bases, unlike metallic (basic) oxides."}]},{"id":"ab4","title":"The Conductivity Chamber","icon":"💡","kind":"lesson","story":"A locked door only opens for liquids that make its bulb glow. Glucose and alcohol leave it dark, but something about acids and bases lights it up — and dry acid gas mysteriously fails the test too.","teach":[{"title":"Ions carry the current","body":"When electrodes connected to a battery and bulb are dipped into dilute HCl or sulphuric acid, the bulb glows, showing that ions in the solution carry electric current. Glucose and alcohol solutions do not conduct electricity, even though they also contain hydrogen.","visual":"💡"},{"title":"Acids release H+(aq) ions","body":"Acids contain H+ as the cation, along with an anion like Cl- or NO3-. Since H+ cannot exist alone, it combines with water to form the hydronium ion: H+ + H2O → H3O+. This H+(aq)/H3O+ ion is responsible for acidic properties.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Water is required for acidity","body":"Dry HCl gas does not turn dry litmus paper red, but moist litmus paper does turn red. This shows that H+ ions from HCl are only produced in the presence of water: HCl + H2O → H3O+ + Cl-.","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"Bases release OH-(aq) ions","body":"Bases like NaOH, KOH, and Mg(OH)2 dissolve in water to release hydroxide ions, for example NaOH(s) → Na+(aq) + OH-(aq). A base that dissolves in water is called an alkali.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Dilution is exothermic","body":"Dissolving a concentrated acid or base in water releases a lot of heat. The acid must always be added slowly to water while stirring — never the reverse — or the heat generated can cause dangerous splashing.","visual":"⚠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does dilute HCl make the bulb in the conductivity test glow, while glucose solution does not, even though both contain hydrogen?","options":["Only HCl ionises in water to release H+ ions that carry current","Glucose contains more hydrogen atoms than HCl","HCl is a solid at room temperature","Glucose glows a different colour"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only compounds that produce ions in solution, like acids, can conduct electric current."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Dry HCl gas does not turn dry litmus paper red because","options":["acidic behaviour needs H+ ions, which form only in the presence of water","dry litmus paper cannot react with any gas","HCl gas is actually a basic gas","litmus only detects metals"],"answer":0,"explain":"HCl + H2O → H3O+ + Cl-, so the acidic H+(aq) ion needs water to form."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"While diluting a concentrated acid, the safest method is to","options":["add the acid slowly to water while stirring","add water slowly to the acid","mix acid and water in equal amounts all at once","heat the acid before adding water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adding water to concentrated acid can cause violent splashing and burns; the acid must go into the water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A base that is soluble in water is specifically called","options":["an alkali","a salt","an oxide","an indicator"],"answer":0,"explain":"Not all bases dissolve in water; those that do are called alkalis."}]},{"id":"ab5","title":"The pH Tower","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"A tower of fourteen floors rises before you, numbered 0 at the base to 14 at the peak. Each floor's door reads its own hydrogen-ion secret, and only a universal indicator can reveal the safe path up.","teach":[{"title":"The pH scale","body":"The pH scale measures the hydrogen ion concentration in a solution, running generally from 0 (very acidic) to 14 (very alkaline). The 'p' in pH stands for the German word 'potenz', meaning power.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Reading the scale","body":"A neutral solution has a pH of exactly 7. Values below 7 are acidic, and values above 7 are basic. The higher the concentration of hydronium ions, the lower the pH value.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Universal indicator","body":"A universal indicator is a mixture of several indicators that shows a different colour at each hydrogen-ion concentration, letting pH paper give an approximate pH reading.","visual":"🌈"},{"title":"Strong vs weak","body":"The strength of an acid or base depends on how many H+ or OH- ions it produces. At the same concentration, hydrochloric acid produces more H+ ions than acetic acid, making HCl a strong acid and acetic acid a weak acid.","visual":"💪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the pH value of a neutral solution such as pure water?","answer":7,"explain":"A pH of exactly 7 marks a neutral solution — neither acidic nor basic."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As the pH value increases from 7 to 14, it represents an increasing concentration of which ion?","options":["Hydroxide (OH-)","Hydrogen (H+)","Chloride (Cl-)","Sodium (Na+)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rising pH above 7 means rising alkali strength, i.e. more OH- ions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the same concentration, hydrochloric acid produces more H+ ions than acetic acid. This makes HCl a","options":["strong acid","weak acid","neutral compound","weak base"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acids that produce more H+ ions are called strong acids; those producing fewer are weak acids."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The 'p' in the term 'pH' comes from the German word 'potenz', meaning","options":["power","purity","proportion","particle"],"answer":0,"explain":"pH literally denotes the 'power' of hydrogen ion concentration."}]},{"id":"ab6","title":"Village of Balanced Bodies","icon":"🫀","kind":"lesson","story":"In a village threatened by acid rain and a grumbling stomach-dragon, healers explain that every living thing — from rivers to teeth to bee-stung skin — survives only within its own narrow pH comfort zone.","teach":[{"title":"Our bodies need balance","body":"The human body works within a narrow pH range of 7.0 to 7.8. Living organisms in general can survive only within a narrow range of pH change.","visual":"🫀"},{"title":"Acid rain","body":"Rain water with a pH below 5.6 is called acid rain. When it flows into rivers, it lowers the river's pH, making survival difficult for aquatic life.","visual":"🌧️"},{"title":"Digestion and antacids","body":"The stomach produces hydrochloric acid to digest food without harm. Overeating causes excess acid, leading to indigestion pain; antacids such as magnesium hydroxide (milk of magnesia) — a mild base — neutralise this excess acid.","visual":"🧫"},{"title":"Tooth decay","body":"Tooth decay starts when the pH inside the mouth falls below 5.5, corroding the calcium hydroxyapatite of the tooth enamel. Basic toothpaste neutralises the acid produced by bacteria and helps prevent decay.","visual":"🦷"},{"title":"Chemical self-defence","body":"A bee sting is acidic and causes pain; rubbing a mild base like baking soda on the area gives relief. A nettle sting injects methanoic acid, traditionally soothed by rubbing with the leaf of the (basic) dock plant.","visual":"🐝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Within what pH range does the human body normally function?","options":["7.0 to 7.8","5.0 to 6.0","8.5 to 9.5","3.0 to 4.0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Our body works within this narrow pH range for its metabolic activities."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Rain water is classified as acid rain once its pH falls below","options":["5.6","7.0","6.5","4.0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Below pH 5.6, rain is acidic enough to be called acid rain."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Tooth decay begins when the pH inside the mouth drops below what value?","answer":5.5,"explain":"Below pH 5.5, tooth enamel starts to corrode."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which mild base, often called milk of magnesia, is commonly used as an antacid?","options":["Magnesium hydroxide","Washing soda","Bleaching powder","Plaster of Paris"],"answer":0,"explain":"Magnesium hydroxide neutralises excess stomach acid to relieve indigestion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which mild base is commonly rubbed on the skin to relieve the pain of a bee sting?","options":["Baking soda","Washing soda","Bleaching powder","Plaster of Paris"],"answer":0,"explain":"Baking soda, being a mild base, neutralises the acid left by a bee sting."}]},{"id":"ab7","title":"Census of the Salt Kingdom","icon":"👪","kind":"lesson","story":"The Salt Kingdom is a sprawling family tree of sodium salts, chloride salts, and sulphate salts. Before you can pass through its gates, you must sort its citizens by family — and by temperament, acidic, basic, or neutral.","teach":[{"title":"Families of salts","body":"Salts that share the same positive or negative ion belong to the same family. For example, NaCl and Na2SO4 both belong to the family of sodium salts, while NaCl and KCl both belong to the family of chloride salts.","visual":"👪"},{"title":"Neutral salts","body":"A salt formed from a strong acid and a strong base is neutral, with a pH of exactly 7. For example, sodium chloride, formed from HCl and NaOH, is a neutral salt.","visual":"⚪"},{"title":"Acidic and basic salts","body":"A salt of a strong acid and a weak base is acidic, with a pH less than 7. A salt of a strong base and a weak acid is basic, with a pH more than 7.","visual":"🔺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"NaCl and KCl belong to the same salt family because they share the same","options":["negative ion (chloride)","positive ion","water of crystallisation","colour"],"answer":0,"explain":"Salts sharing the same negative ion, chloride, belong to the chloride family."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A salt formed from a strong acid and a strong base, like sodium chloride, has a pH of","options":["7 (neutral)","less than 7 (acidic)","more than 7 (basic)","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strong acid + strong base gives a neutral salt with pH exactly 7."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A salt formed from a strong base and a weak acid will be","options":["basic, with pH more than 7","acidic, with pH less than 7","neutral, with pH exactly 7","impossible to form"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strong base + weak acid produces a basic salt."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A salt formed from a strong acid and a weak base will be","options":["acidic, with pH less than 7","basic, with pH more than 7","neutral, with pH exactly 7","always insoluble in water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strong acid + weak base produces an acidic salt."}]},{"id":"ab8","title":"The Chlor-Alkali Factory","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep beneath the Salt Kingdom hums a factory powered by electricity and brine. From one humble raw material — common salt — it forges three prized products, and from those, bleaching powder, baking soda, and washing soda.","teach":[{"title":"The chlor-alkali process","body":"Passing electricity through an aqueous solution of sodium chloride (brine) decomposes it: 2NaCl(aq) + 2H2O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + Cl2(g) + H2(g). Chlorine gas forms at the anode, hydrogen gas at the cathode, and sodium hydroxide near the cathode.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Bleaching powder","body":"Bleaching powder, Ca(ClO)2, is made by the action of chlorine gas on dry slaked lime. It is used for bleaching cotton, linen, and wood pulp, as an oxidising agent in industry, and to disinfect drinking water.","visual":"🧺"},{"title":"Baking soda","body":"Baking soda, sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO3), is a mild, non-corrosive base made using sodium chloride as a raw material. Heating it produces sodium carbonate, water, and carbon dioxide: 2NaHCO3 → Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2. It is used in cooking, in antacids, and in soda-acid fire extinguishers.","visual":"🎂"},{"title":"Washing soda","body":"Sodium carbonate obtained by heating baking soda can be recrystallised to form washing soda, Na2CO3.10H2O. It is used in the glass, soap, and paper industries, as a cleaning agent, and for removing permanent hardness of water.","visual":"🧼"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The chlor-alkali process passes electricity through brine to produce sodium hydroxide, hydrogen gas, and","options":["chlorine gas","carbon dioxide","oxygen gas","ammonia gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"2NaCl + 2H2O → 2NaOH + Cl2 + H2 — 'chlor' for chlorine, 'alkali' for sodium hydroxide."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bleaching powder is produced by the action of chlorine gas on","options":["dry slaked lime","washing soda","baking soda","brine"],"answer":0,"explain":"2Ca(OH)2 + 2Cl2 → Ca(ClO)2 + CaCl2 + 2H2O produces bleaching powder."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The chemical name of baking soda is","options":["sodium hydrogencarbonate","sodium carbonate","sodium chloride","sodium hydroxide"],"answer":0,"explain":"Baking soda is NaHCO3, sodium hydrogencarbonate."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Heating baking soda produces sodium carbonate, water, and","options":["carbon dioxide","hydrogen gas","chlorine gas","oxygen gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"2NaHCO3 → Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2 on heating."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Recrystallising sodium carbonate gives washing soda, whose formula is","options":["Na2CO3.10H2O","Na2CO3.5H2O","NaHCO3.10H2O","CaSO4.2H2O"],"answer":0,"explain":"Washing soda is sodium carbonate with ten water molecules of crystallisation."}]},{"id":"ab9","title":"The Crystal Vault","icon":"💎","kind":"lesson","story":"Blue crystals line the vault's shelves — but heat them and they turn ghostly white, only to bloom blue again with a drop of water. Deeper inside, a healer mixes a chalky white powder with water to cast a hardening splint for an injured companion.","teach":[{"title":"Water of crystallisation","body":"Water of crystallisation is the fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt. Hydrated copper sulphate, CuSO4.5H2O, is blue; heating removes its water of crystallisation and turns it white, and adding water restores the blue colour.","visual":"💎"},{"title":"Gypsum","body":"Gypsum is another salt with water of crystallisation, with the formula CaSO4.2H2O, containing two water molecules per formula unit.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"Plaster of Paris","body":"Heating gypsum at 373 K removes water to form calcium sulphate hemihydrate, CaSO4.½H2O, known as Plaster of Paris. It is a white powder used by doctors for supporting fractured bones and for making toys and decorative surfaces.","visual":"🩹"},{"title":"Plaster of Paris meets water","body":"Mixing Plaster of Paris with water changes it back into gypsum, giving a hard solid mass: CaSO4.½H2O + 1½H2O → CaSO4.2H2O. This is why it must be stored in a moisture-proof container.","visual":"🧱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Water of crystallisation is best described as","options":["a fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt","any water present in a wet salt sample","water used to dissolve a salt","water produced when a salt burns"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is a fixed, defined amount of water chemically bound within the salt's crystal, e.g. CuSO4.5H2O."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Heating blue hydrated copper sulphate crystals (CuSO4.5H2O) turns them","options":["white, as the water of crystallisation is removed","black, due to burning","red, due to oxidation","green, due to forming a new compound"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing the water of crystallisation leaves white anhydrous copper sulphate."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many water molecules of crystallisation are present in one formula unit of gypsum, CaSO4?","answer":2,"explain":"Gypsum's formula is CaSO4.2H2O."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Plaster of Paris is made by heating gypsum at 373 K to remove water, forming","options":["calcium sulphate hemihydrate","calcium sulphate dihydrate","calcium carbonate","calcium oxide"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gypsum loses water on heating to become CaSO4.½H2O, calcium sulphate hemihydrate."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When Plaster of Paris is mixed with water, it hardens by changing back into","options":["gypsum","quicklime","washing soda","bleaching powder"],"answer":0,"explain":"CaSO4.½H2O + 1½H2O → CaSO4.2H2O (gypsum), forming a hard solid mass."}]},{"id":"ab10","title":"The pH Sphinx's Gate","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the citadel gate stands the pH Sphinx, keeper of every acid, base, and salt secret you've collected on this journey. She will not move aside until you prove your mastery of the whole chapter.","boss":{"name":"The pH Sphinx","taunt":"Litmus, indicators, ions, salts — you've seen it all. But can you hold the WHOLE chapter in your head at once, traveller?"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything you've learned","body":"Acids and bases are identified by indicators, react with metals to release hydrogen gas, and neutralise each other to form salts and water. Their strength is measured on the pH scale, and common salt is the raw material behind sodium hydroxide, bleaching powder, baking soda, washing soda, and Plaster of Paris.","visual":"👑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which gas is evolved when zinc reacts with dilute sulphuric acid?","options":["Hydrogen","Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Chlorine"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acid + Metal → Salt + Hydrogen gas."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The reaction Acid + Base → Salt + Water is called a","options":["neutralisation reaction","combustion reaction","oxidation reaction","precipitation reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acids and bases cancel each other's effect in a neutralisation reaction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the pH of a neutral solution?","answer":7,"explain":"pH 7 marks the boundary between acidic (below) and basic (above)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A solution with pH 3 compared to a solution with pH 9 is","options":["more acidic, with a higher H+ ion concentration","more basic, with a higher OH- concentration","exactly the same strength","impossible to compare"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lower pH means higher hydronium ion concentration, i.e. more acidic."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Dry HCl gas fails to turn dry litmus paper red because","options":["it needs water present to release H+ ions","it is not an acid at all","litmus only detects bases","dry gases cannot react with paper"],"answer":0,"explain":"HCl + H2O → H3O+ + Cl-; without water, no H+(aq) ions form."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Antacids relieve indigestion by","options":["neutralising excess acid in the stomach with a mild base","adding more acid to help digestion","killing bacteria in the stomach","coating the stomach with sugar"],"answer":0,"explain":"A mild base like magnesium hydroxide neutralises the excess stomach acid causing pain."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The chlor-alkali process electrolyses brine to produce sodium hydroxide, hydrogen gas, and","options":["chlorine gas","oxygen gas","carbon dioxide","ammonia gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"2NaCl + 2H2O → 2NaOH + Cl2 + H2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Plaster of Paris hardens into a solid mass when mixed with water because it changes back into","options":["gypsum (CaSO4.2H2O)","washing soda","bleaching powder","quicklime"],"answer":0,"explain":"CaSO4.½H2O + water → CaSO4.2H2O, gypsum, a hard solid mass."}]}]},{"id":"metalsnonmetals10","index":2,"name":"Metals and Non-metals","subtitle":"Science Chapter 3","emoji":"⚙️","palette":{"from":"#94a3b8","to":"#1e293b"},"lessons":[{"id":"mn1","title":"The Blacksmith's Trial","icon":"🔨","kind":"lesson","story":"The village blacksmith hands you a box of unlabeled samples and a sheet of sandpaper. \"Rub, bend, hammer, and listen,\" she says. \"A true smith knows a metal before it's named.\"","teach":[{"title":"Metallic Lustre","body":"Metals, in their pure state, have a shining surface — this property is called metallic lustre. Freshly cut or sanded iron, copper, aluminium, and magnesium all reveal this shine.","visual":"✨"},{"title":"Malleability and Ductility","body":"Metals can be beaten into thin sheets, a property called malleability, and drawn into thin wires, called ductility. Gold and silver are the most malleable metals, and gold is the most ductile — a single gram of gold can be drawn into a wire about 2 km long.","visual":"🥇"},{"title":"Hardness, Heat and Sound","body":"Metals are generally hard (though hardness varies), are good conductors of heat with high melting points, and produce a ringing sound when struck — a property called sonority. Silver and copper are the best conductors of heat, while lead and mercury conduct heat poorly.","visual":"🔔"},{"title":"The Exceptions","body":"Not every metal fits the pattern: mercury is a liquid at room temperature, while gallium and caesium have such low melting points they would melt on your palm. Alkali metals like lithium, sodium and potassium are so soft they can be cut with a knife.","visual":"⚠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which metal is a liquid at room temperature?","options":["Mercury","Iron","Copper","Zinc"],"answer":0,"explain":"All metals except mercury exist as solids at room temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The ability of a metal to be drawn into thin wires is called:","options":["Ductility","Malleability","Lustre","Sonority"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ductility is the property of being drawn into wires; gold is the most ductile metal."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"About how many kilometres of wire can be drawn from just one gram of gold?","answer":2,"explain":"A wire of about 2 km length can be drawn from one gram of gold, showing its extreme ductility."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which metals are the best conductors of heat?","options":["Silver and copper","Lead and mercury","Iron and zinc","Gold and aluminium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Silver and copper are the best conductors of heat; lead and mercury are comparatively poor conductors."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Alkali metals like sodium and potassium can be cut with a knife because they are:","options":["Soft, with low densities and low melting points","Extremely hard","Liquids at room temperature","Non-conductors of electricity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alkali metals (lithium, sodium, potassium) are so soft they can be cut with a knife, and have low densities and low melting points."}]},{"id":"mn2","title":"Deep Into the Non-metal Caverns","icon":"💎","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the smithy lies a glittering cavern where sulphur crystals and iodine flakes glint strangely — some of them shine like metal, yet snap like glass. Something here breaks the blacksmith's rules.","teach":[{"title":"Meet the Non-metals","body":"There are far fewer non-metals than metals — examples include carbon, sulphur, iodine, oxygen and hydrogen. Non-metals are mostly solids or gases, except for bromine, which is a liquid.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Opposite Properties","body":"Non-metals generally lack lustre, are not malleable or ductile (many are brittle solids), and are poor conductors of heat and electricity. They also don't produce a ringing sound when struck.","visual":"❌"},{"title":"Iodine Breaks the Rule","body":"Iodine is a non-metal, yet it is lustrous — a clear exception to the general rule that non-metals lack shine.","visual":"🌟"},{"title":"Carbon's Many Faces","body":"Carbon is a non-metal that exists in different forms called allotropes. Diamond, an allotrope of carbon, is the hardest natural substance known with a very high melting and boiling point, while graphite, another allotrope, conducts electricity.","visual":"🔷"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which non-metal is a liquid at room temperature?","options":["Bromine","Iodine","Sulphur","Carbon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-metals are solids or gases except bromine, which is a liquid."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which lustrous non-metal is an exception to the usual dull appearance of non-metals?","options":["Iodine","Sulphur","Carbon","Bromine"],"answer":0,"explain":"Iodine is a non-metal but it is lustrous."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which allotrope of carbon is able to conduct electricity?","options":["Graphite","Diamond","Coal","Coke"],"answer":0,"explain":"Graphite, an allotrope of carbon, is a conductor of electricity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Diamond, an allotrope of carbon, is known as:","options":["The hardest natural substance known","The best conductor of electricity","The most malleable substance","The most ductile substance"],"answer":0,"explain":"Diamond is the hardest natural substance known and has a very high melting and boiling point."}]},{"id":"mn3","title":"Trial by Fire","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"The forge master lights a brazier and hands you tongs. \"Hold each metal to the flame,\" he warns, \"but keep the sodium and potassium capped in oil until it's their turn — they do not wait patiently for fire.\"","teach":[{"title":"Metal + Oxygen → Metal Oxide","body":"Almost all metals combine with oxygen to form metal oxides, which are generally basic in nature. For example, copper heated in air forms black copper(II) oxide: 2Cu + O2 → 2CuO.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Amphoteric Oxides","body":"Some metal oxides, like aluminium oxide and zinc oxide, react with both acids and bases to produce salts and water — these are called amphoteric oxides. For example, Al2O3 + 6HCl → 2AlCl3 + 3H2O, and Al2O3 + 2NaOH → 2NaAlO2 + H2O.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Oxides That Form Alkalis","body":"Most metal oxides are insoluble in water, but sodium oxide and potassium oxide dissolve in water to produce alkalis: Na2O(s) + H2O(l) → 2NaOH(aq), and K2O(s) + H2O(l) → 2KOH(aq).","visual":"💧"},{"title":"A Spectrum of Reactivity","body":"Sodium and potassium react so vigorously with oxygen that they must be kept immersed in kerosene oil to prevent accidental fires. Magnesium, aluminium, zinc and lead form a protective oxide layer at ordinary temperature, while silver and gold don't react with oxygen even at high temperatures.","visual":"🛡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are sodium and potassium kept immersed in kerosene oil?","options":["They react vigorously with air/oxygen and could catch fire","To keep them cold","To give them colour","Because they are heavier than kerosene"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sodium and potassium react so vigorously with oxygen that they catch fire if kept in the open, so they are stored in kerosene."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Aluminium oxide reacts with both acids and bases. This makes it a/an:","options":["Amphoteric oxide","Basic oxide only","Acidic oxide only","Neutral oxide"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oxides that react with both acids and bases to produce salts and water, like Al2O3 and ZnO, are called amphoteric oxides."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Complete the equation: 2Cu + O2 → ?","options":["2CuO","Cu2O","CuO2","2Cu2O"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copper heated in air combines with oxygen to form copper(II) oxide: 2Cu + O2 → 2CuO."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which metals do not react with oxygen even at high temperatures?","options":["Silver and gold","Sodium and potassium","Magnesium and aluminium","Zinc and lead"],"answer":0,"explain":"Silver and gold do not react with oxygen even at high temperatures."}]},{"id":"mn4","title":"The River of Reactions","icon":"💦","kind":"lesson","story":"A river guide lowers metal samples into cold water, then hot water, then a hissing curtain of steam. \"Watch closely,\" she says. \"The river tells you who is brave, who is cautious, and who never gets wet at all.\"","teach":[{"title":"Metal + Water → Metal Oxide + Hydrogen","body":"Metals react with water to produce a metal oxide and hydrogen gas; if the oxide is soluble, it dissolves further to form a metal hydroxide. Not all metals react with water.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Violent Reactions with Cold Water","body":"Potassium and sodium react so violently and exothermically with cold water that the evolved hydrogen catches fire: 2Na(s) + 2H2O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + H2(g) + heat energy.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Calcium Floats","body":"Calcium reacts less violently with water — not enough heat is evolved for the hydrogen to catch fire — and it starts floating because bubbles of hydrogen gas stick to its surface: Ca(s) + 2H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(aq) + H2(g).","visual":"🛟"},{"title":"Hot Water, Steam, or Nothing at All","body":"Magnesium reacts only with hot water, floating for the same bubble reason. Aluminium, iron and zinc don't react with cold or hot water but do react with steam, e.g. 3Fe(s) + 4H2O(g) → Fe3O4(s) + 4H2(g), while lead, copper, silver and gold do not react with water at all.","visual":"♨️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which metals react so violently with cold water that the hydrogen produced catches fire?","options":["Sodium and potassium","Magnesium and aluminium","Lead and copper","Silver and gold"],"answer":0,"explain":"The reaction of sodium and potassium with cold water is so violent and exothermic that the evolved hydrogen immediately catches fire."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does calcium start floating during its reaction with water?","options":["Bubbles of hydrogen gas stick to its surface","It turns into a gas","It is lighter than water to begin with","It reacts with oxygen instead of water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Calcium starts floating because the bubbles of hydrogen gas formed stick to the surface of the metal."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Iron reacts with water only in the form of:","options":["Steam","Cold water","Hot water","Ice"],"answer":0,"explain":"Metals like aluminium, iron and zinc do not react with cold or hot water, but they react with steam."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these metals do NOT react with water at all?","options":["Lead, copper, silver and gold","Sodium and potassium","Magnesium and calcium","Zinc and aluminium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Metals such as lead, copper, silver and gold do not react with water at all."}]},{"id":"mn5","title":"The Acid Vault","icon":"⚗️","kind":"lesson","story":"In a chamber lined with bubbling test tubes, you drop metal samples into dilute hydrochloric acid one by one, thermometer in hand, racing to rank them from most to least reactive before the vault seals.","teach":[{"title":"Metal + Acid → Salt + Hydrogen","body":"Metals react with dilute acids to give a salt and hydrogen gas. From the rate of bubble formation and temperature rise, reactivity with dilute HCl decreases in the order Mg > Al > Zn > Fe, while copper shows no reaction at all — no bubbles and no temperature change.","visual":"🧫"},{"title":"Nitric Acid Is Different","body":"Hydrogen gas is usually not evolved when a metal reacts with nitric acid, because HNO3 is a strong oxidising agent that oxidises the H2 produced to water. The exceptions are magnesium and manganese, which react with very dilute HNO3 to evolve H2 gas.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Aqua Regia Dissolves Gold","body":"Aqua regia is a freshly prepared 3:1 mixture of concentrated hydrochloric and concentrated nitric acid. Neither acid alone can dissolve gold, but this highly corrosive fuming mixture can dissolve both gold and platinum.","visual":"👑"},{"title":"The Activity Series","body":"Displacement experiments build the activity series — metals arranged in decreasing order of reactivity: K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, [H], Cu, Hg, Ag, Au, from most to least reactive.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Based on Activity 3.11, which order correctly shows decreasing reactivity with dilute HCl?","options":["Mg > Al > Zn > Fe","Fe > Zn > Al > Mg","Cu > Fe > Zn > Al","Al > Mg > Fe > Zn"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rate of bubble formation was fastest for magnesium, and reactivity decreased in the order Mg > Al > Zn > Fe."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which metal shows no reaction at all with dilute hydrochloric acid?","options":["Copper","Magnesium","Zinc","Iron"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the case of copper, no bubbles were seen and the temperature remained unchanged, showing it does not react with dilute HCl."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Aqua regia, which can dissolve gold, is a 3:1 mixture of concentrated hydrochloric acid and concentrated:","options":["Nitric acid","Sulphuric acid","Acetic acid","Carbonic acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aqua regia is a freshly prepared mixture of concentrated hydrochloric acid and concentrated nitric acid in the ratio 3:1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the activity series, which metal is placed as the most reactive?","options":["Potassium","Gold","Copper","Iron"],"answer":0,"explain":"The activity series lists potassium (K) as the most reactive metal, with gold (Au) as the least reactive."}]},{"id":"mn6","title":"The Crystal Caverns","icon":"🧲","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep underground, a copper wire dipped into a silvery solution begins to change before your eyes, while nearby, sodium and chlorine atoms trade electrons in a shimmering give-and-take. Something is being built — and taken.","teach":[{"title":"Displacement Reveals Reactivity","body":"In Activity 3.12, an iron nail placed in copper sulphate solution reacts, while a copper wire in iron sulphate solution does not — proving iron is more reactive than copper. In general, Metal A + Salt solution of B → Salt solution of A + Metal B, whenever A is more reactive than B.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Chasing a Full Outer Shell","body":"Elements react with a tendency to attain a completely filled valence shell, like the noble gases. A sodium atom has one electron in its outermost shell; losing it leaves a stable octet and creates a positively charged sodium cation, Na+.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Forming Sodium Chloride","body":"Chlorine has seven outer electrons and needs just one more to complete its octet. The electron sodium loses is taken up by chlorine, forming a chloride anion, Cl−. The oppositely charged Na+ and Cl− ions attract each other by strong electrostatic forces, existing as aggregates of ions rather than molecules — this is an ionic (electrovalent) compound.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"Forming Magnesium Chloride","body":"Magnesium has two outer electrons to lose, forming a doubly charged cation, Mg2+. It combines with two chloride ions (each Cl−) to form magnesium chloride, MgCl2, another ionic compound formed by transfer of electrons from a metal to a non-metal.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Activity 3.12, an iron nail placed in copper sulphate solution reacts, but copper wire in iron sulphate solution does not. This shows that:","options":["Iron is more reactive than copper","Copper is more reactive than iron","Iron and copper never react with salts","Copper displaces iron"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since iron displaces copper from its salt solution, iron must be more reactive than copper."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Compounds formed by the transfer of electrons from a metal to a non-metal are called:","options":["Ionic (electrovalent) compounds","Covalent compounds","Metallic alloys","Allotropes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The compounds formed by the transfer of electrons from a metal to a non-metal are known as ionic compounds or electrovalent compounds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When a sodium atom loses one electron, it becomes:","options":["A sodium cation, Na+","A sodium anion, Na−","A neutral sodium molecule","A chloride ion"],"answer":0,"explain":"After losing an electron, sodium has 11 protons but only 10 electrons, giving a net positive charge — the sodium cation Na+."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the charge on the magnesium ion in MgCl2?","options":["+2","+1","−1","−2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Magnesium loses two electrons to form the doubly-charged cation Mg2+, which combines with two Cl− ions."}]},{"id":"mn7","title":"The Ionic Fortress","icon":"🧊","kind":"lesson","story":"The fortress walls are built from crystal salts — hard, brittle, and utterly silent until melted or dissolved. Only then do they hum with the ability to carry a current.","teach":[{"title":"Hard but Brittle","body":"Ionic compounds are solids, somewhat hard because of the strong force of attraction between positive and negative ions, but generally brittle — they break into pieces when pressure is applied.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"High Melting and Boiling Points","body":"Ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points — for example, NaCl melts at 1074 K — because a considerable amount of energy is required to break the strong inter-ionic attraction.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Solubility","body":"Electrovalent compounds are generally soluble in water but insoluble in solvents such as kerosene and petrol.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Conducting Electricity","body":"Ionic compounds in the solid state cannot conduct electricity because the ions cannot move within their rigid structure. But in the molten state or dissolved in water, the ions move freely and the compound conducts electricity.","visual":"⚡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ionic compounds are generally brittle because:","options":["They break into pieces when pressure disturbs their rigid ion arrangement","They are always gases","They have very low melting points","They contain only covalent bonds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ionic compounds are hard due to strong ionic attraction but break into pieces (are brittle) when pressure is applied."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do ionic compounds have high melting points?","options":["A lot of energy is needed to break the strong electrostatic attraction between ions","They are always metals","They have weak intermolecular forces","They contain only non-metals"],"answer":0,"explain":"Considerable energy is required to overcome the strong inter-ionic attraction, giving ionic compounds high melting and boiling points."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ionic compounds conduct electricity when they are:","options":["Molten or dissolved in water","In the solid state only","Never able to conduct","Frozen solid"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the solid state, ions cannot move due to the rigid structure, but in molten or dissolved form the ions move freely and conduct electricity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ionic compounds are generally soluble in water but insoluble in:","options":["Kerosene and petrol","All liquids equally","Only hot water","Only acids"],"answer":0,"explain":"Electrovalent compounds are generally soluble in water and insoluble in solvents such as kerosene and petrol."}]},{"id":"mn8","title":"The Mining Expedition","icon":"⛏️","kind":"lesson","story":"A mine cart rattles past oxide-stained rock walls. The foreman hands you three tools — a furnace, a crucible of carbon, and an electrolysis rig — and tells you to match the right tool to the right ore, or the whole shipment is ruined.","teach":[{"title":"Minerals, Ores and Gangue","body":"Elements or compounds that occur naturally in the earth's crust are called minerals; where a mineral contains a high enough percentage of a metal to be profitably extracted, it is called an ore. The unwanted impurities like soil and sand mixed with an ore are called gangue.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"Three Bands of Reactivity","body":"Metals low in the activity series (gold, silver, platinum, copper) are often found free in nature. Metals in the middle (zinc, iron, lead) occur mainly as sulphides, oxides or carbonates. Metals at the top (K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al) are so reactive they are never found free.","visual":"⚒️"},{"title":"Roasting and Calcination","body":"Before reduction, sulphide ores are converted to oxides by roasting — heating strongly in excess air, e.g. 2ZnS(s) + 3O2(g) → 2ZnO(s) + 2SO2(g) — while carbonate ores are converted by calcination, heating strongly in limited air, e.g. ZnCO3(s) → ZnO(s) + CO2(g).","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Reduction and Electrolysis","body":"Low-reactivity metal oxides need only heating (HgS heats to HgO then to Hg). Middle-reactivity oxides are reduced with carbon, e.g. ZnO(s) + C(s) → Zn(s) + CO(g), or with a very reactive metal as in the thermit reaction, Fe2O3(s) + 2Al(s) → 2Fe(l) + Al2O3(s) + Heat. Highly reactive metals like sodium are obtained by electrolytic reduction of their molten compounds — Na+ + e− → Na at the cathode.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Refining by Electrolysis","body":"In electrolytic refining, the impure metal is made the anode and a strip of pure metal the cathode, with a metal salt solution as electrolyte. Passing current dissolves pure metal from the anode and deposits an equal amount on the cathode, while insoluble impurities settle as anode mud.","visual":"🏭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Carbonate ores are converted into oxides by heating strongly in limited air — this process is called:","options":["Calcination","Roasting","Refining","Electrolysis"],"answer":0,"explain":"Calcination converts carbonate ores into oxides by heating strongly in limited air, while roasting does the same for sulphide ores in excess air."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The thermit reaction, Fe2O3 + 2Al → 2Fe + Al2O3 + Heat, is used to:","options":["Join railway tracks or cracked machine parts","Extract gold from ore","Purify drinking water","Prevent rusting of iron"],"answer":0,"explain":"The highly exothermic thermit reaction produces molten iron and is used to join railway tracks or cracked machine parts."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sodium, magnesium and calcium — metals at the top of the activity series — are extracted by:","options":["Electrolytic reduction of their molten compounds","Heating alone","Roasting in air","Reaction with dilute acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Highly reactive metals cannot be reduced by carbon and are instead extracted by electrolysis of their molten compounds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the electrolytic refining of copper, the impure copper is made the:","options":["Anode","Cathode","Electrolyte","Gangue"],"answer":0,"explain":"The impure metal is made the anode, a strip of pure metal the cathode, and a metal salt solution the electrolyte."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The insoluble impurities that settle at the bottom of the anode during electrolytic refining are called:","options":["Anode mud","Gangue","Ore","Alloy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Insoluble impurities settle down at the bottom of the anode and are known as anode mud."}]},{"id":"mn9","title":"Showdown with the Rust King","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Rust King","taunt":"Moist air, salty seas, and centuries of neglect — I have flaked apart every iron gate that ever mocked me. Show me you know how to stop me, or watch your armour crumble to orange dust."},"story":"The Rust King rules a graveyard of corroded iron gates, blackened silver, and green-crusted copper pipes. Only a champion who understands corrosion — and how metallurgy defeats it — can seal his throne room forever.","teach":[{"title":"How Metals Corrode","body":"Silver tarnishes black by reacting with sulphur in the air to form silver sulphide. Copper reacts with moist carbon dioxide to lose its shiny brown surface and gain a green coat of basic copper carbonate. Iron exposed to moist air for a long time acquires a coating of a brown flaky substance called rust.","visual":"🦠"},{"title":"The Conditions for Rust","body":"Activity 3.14 shows iron nails rust only when exposed to BOTH air and water together — nails kept in water alone with no dissolved air, or in dry air alone, do not rust.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Preventing Corrosion","body":"Rusting can be prevented by painting, oiling, greasing, galvanising, chrome plating, anodising, or making alloys. Galvanising coats iron or steel with a thin layer of zinc, and protects the article against rusting even if the coating is broken.","visual":"🛡️"},{"title":"The Power of Alloys","body":"An alloy is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal, made by melting the primary metal and dissolving the others into it in definite proportions. Mixing iron with a little carbon makes it hard and strong; adding nickel and chromium gives stainless steel, which resists rust.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Alloys in Everyday Use","body":"Brass (copper and zinc) and bronze (copper and tin) are poor conductors compared to pure copper. Solder, an alloy of lead and tin, has a low melting point and is used for welding electrical wires together. An alloy containing mercury is called an amalgam.","visual":"🔩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Activity 3.14, iron nails rust only when exposed to:","options":["Both air and water together","Water alone with no dissolved air","Dry air alone","Oil alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nails rusted only in the test tube where both air and water were present; they did not rust with only one of the two."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Galvanisation protects iron and steel from rusting by coating them with a thin layer of:","options":["Zinc","Gold","Tin","Copper"],"answer":0,"explain":"Galvanisation is a method of protecting steel and iron from rusting by coating them with a thin layer of zinc."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Brass is an alloy of:","options":["Copper and zinc","Copper and tin","Lead and tin","Iron and carbon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc (Cu and Zn)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bronze is an alloy of:","options":["Copper and tin","Copper and zinc","Lead and tin","Zinc and iron"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin (Cu and Sn)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Solder, an alloy of lead and tin with a low melting point, is mainly used for:","options":["Welding electrical wires together","Making jewellery","Making coins","Cooking utensils"],"answer":0,"explain":"Solder has a low melting point and is used for welding electrical wires together."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Mixing iron with nickel and chromium produces:","options":["Stainless steel, which is hard and does not rust","Brass, which conducts electricity well","Bronze, used for casting bells","Solder, used in welding"],"answer":0,"explain":"When iron is mixed with nickel and chromium, we get stainless steel, which is hard and does not rust."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The green coating that forms on copper vessels exposed to moist air is:","options":["Basic copper carbonate","Copper oxide","Copper sulphide","Rust"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copper reacts with moist carbon dioxide in the air to form a green coat of basic copper carbonate."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is pure 24 carat gold not suitable for making jewellery?","options":["It is too soft","It is too reactive","It is too expensive to shape","It corrodes quickly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pure gold (24 carat) is very soft, so it is alloyed with silver or copper (as 22 carat gold) to make it hard enough for jewellery."}]}]},{"id":"carboncompounds10","index":3,"name":"Carbon and its Compounds","subtitle":"Science Chapter 4","emoji":"⬡","palette":{"from":"#1f2937","to":"#000000"},"lessons":[{"id":"cc1","title":"The Covalent Bond","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"You've shrunk down to the size of an atom and landed inside a swirling electron cloud. To escape this molecular maze you must learn the trick every atom here uses to feel complete: sharing electrons instead of fighting over them. Welcome to the world of the covalent bond.","teach":[{"title":"Why carbon shares instead of transfers","body":"Carbon (atomic number 6) has 4 valence electrons. To reach a noble-gas configuration it would need to gain or lose all 4 - but a C4- ion is hard for the nucleus to hold onto, and forming C4+ needs too much energy. So carbon shares its electrons with other atoms instead.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"A single covalent bond - H2","body":"Hydrogen has 1 electron and needs 1 more to fill its shell. Two hydrogen atoms share their electrons, so each ends up with 2 electrons like helium. This shared pair is a single covalent bond, drawn as a line: H-H.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Double and triple bonds - O2 and N2","body":"An oxygen atom needs 2 more electrons to complete its octet, so two oxygen atoms each share 2 electrons, forming a double bond. A nitrogen atom needs 3 more electrons, so two nitrogen atoms each share 3 electrons, forming a triple bond.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Methane - carbon's tetravalent bond","body":"Carbon is tetravalent because it has 4 valence electrons. In methane (CH4), carbon shares one electron each with 4 hydrogen atoms, forming 4 single covalent bonds so every atom reaches a filled outer shell.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a covalent bond?","options":["A bond formed when two atoms share a pair of electrons","A bond formed when one atom transfers electrons to another","A bond formed by attraction between oppositely charged ions","A bond formed only between metal atoms"],"answer":0,"explain":"In a covalent bond, both atoms contribute and share a pair of electrons so each attains a filled outer shell."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why doesn't carbon easily form a C4+ or C4- ion?","options":["Gaining or losing 4 electrons is too costly for the nucleus or requires too much energy","Carbon has no valence electrons","Carbon is already a noble gas","Carbon only forms ionic bonds"],"answer":0,"explain":"A C4- ion is hard for the 6-proton nucleus to hold, and forming C4+ would need a huge amount of energy - so carbon shares electrons instead."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many covalent bonds does the carbon atom form in one molecule of methane (CH4)?","answer":4,"explain":"Carbon is tetravalent and forms one single bond with each of the 4 hydrogen atoms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What type of bond exists between the two nitrogen atoms in a molecule of N2?","options":["Triple covalent bond","Single covalent bond","Double covalent bond","Ionic bond"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each nitrogen atom contributes 3 electrons, giving 3 shared pairs - a triple bond."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many valence electrons does a carbon atom have?","answer":4,"explain":"Carbon's electronic configuration gives it 4 electrons in its outermost shell, making it tetravalent."}]},{"id":"cc2","title":"Allotropes of Carbon","icon":"💎","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Vault of Forms, pure carbon waits disguised as two very different guardians: an unbreakable jewel and a slippery grey mineral. Figure out how the very same atoms can bond so differently, and the vault will show you a third, football-shaped secret.","teach":[{"title":"Diamond - a rigid cage","body":"In diamond, each carbon atom is bonded to 4 other carbon atoms, forming a rigid three-dimensional structure. This makes diamond the hardest substance known, and, since there are no free electrons, a poor conductor of electricity.","visual":"💎"},{"title":"Graphite - sliding sheets","body":"In graphite, each carbon atom is bonded to only 3 other carbon atoms in the same plane, forming hexagonal layers stacked on one another. This structure makes graphite smooth and slippery, and, unlike most non-metals, a good conductor of electricity.","visual":"⬛"},{"title":"Fullerenes - carbon footballs","body":"Fullerenes are another allotrope of carbon. The first identified, C-60, has its carbon atoms arranged in the shape of a football, resembling the geodesic dome designed by architect Buckminster Fuller - which is how it got its name.","visual":"⚽"},{"title":"Same element, different physical properties","body":"Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon, so their chemical properties are identical - but their very different bonding arrangements give them completely different physical properties, like hardness and conductivity.","visual":"🔬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In diamond, how many other carbon atoms is each carbon atom bonded to?","answer":4,"explain":"Each carbon in diamond bonds to 4 others, forming a rigid three-dimensional structure."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is graphite a good conductor of electricity while diamond is not?","options":["Graphite's layered structure leaves electrons free to move, unlike diamond's rigid bonding","Graphite contains hidden metal impurities","Diamond is not really made of carbon","Graphite has more carbon atoms than diamond"],"answer":0,"explain":"Graphite's flat hexagonal layers (each carbon bonded to only 3 others) allow it to conduct electricity, unlike diamond's rigid 3-D structure."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What shape gave Buckminsterfullerene (C-60) its name?","options":["A football / geodesic dome shape","A diamond crystal shape","A flat hexagonal sheet","A long straight chain"],"answer":0,"explain":"C-60's carbon atoms are arranged like a football, similar to Buckminster Fuller's geodesic dome design."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon, so their chemical properties are:","options":["The same, even though their physical properties differ","Completely opposite","Unrelated to carbon","Radioactive"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since both are forms of the same element, carbon, their chemical properties match - only the bonding arrangement (and hence physical properties) differs."}]},{"id":"cc3","title":"Versatile Carbon","icon":"⛓️","kind":"lesson","story":"The Architect's Guild claims only carbon can build with itself - chain after chain, ring after ring - without the structure falling apart. Prove you understand catenation and tetravalency, and the Guild will hand you the blueprint behind millions of compounds.","teach":[{"title":"Catenation - carbon bonds with carbon","body":"Carbon has the unique ability to form bonds with other carbon atoms, giving rise to large molecules. This property is called catenation, and it can produce long chains, branched chains, or even rings of carbon atoms.","visual":"⛓️"},{"title":"Why carbon beats silicon at this","body":"No other element shows catenation like carbon does. Silicon can form chains with hydrogen up to 7 or 8 atoms long, but these compounds are very reactive and unstable - while the carbon-carbon bond is strong and stable, allowing far longer chains.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Tetravalency - four hands to hold with","body":"Because carbon has a valency of four, it can bond with 4 other carbon atoms, or with atoms of other monovalent elements like hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, sulphur or chlorine - giving rise to compounds with many different properties.","visual":"4️⃣"},{"title":"Small atom, strong bonds","body":"Carbon's small size lets its nucleus hold shared electron pairs very strongly, making its bonds exceptionally stable compared to the weaker bonds formed by elements with bigger atoms.","visual":"🔒"},{"title":"Organic chemistry and the 'vital force' myth","body":"Carbon compounds (other than carbides, oxides of carbon, and carbonate/hydrogencarbonate salts) are studied as organic chemistry. It was once believed such compounds needed a living 'vital force' to form, until Friedrich Wohler disproved this in 1828 by preparing urea from ammonium cyanate.","visual":"🧫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is catenation?","options":["Carbon's ability to bond with other carbon atoms, forming chains, branches or rings","Carbon's ability to conduct electricity","The process of burning a carbon compound","Carbon's ability to form ionic bonds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Catenation is carbon's unique ability to link with itself to build long chains, branches and rings."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two properties of carbon together explain the huge number of carbon compounds that exist?","options":["Catenation and tetravalency","Radioactivity and catenation","Tetravalency and low density","Catenation and high melting point"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carbon's ability to chain with itself (catenation) combined with its four bonding positions (tetravalency) produces millions of compounds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who disproved the 'vital force' theory by synthesising urea from ammonium cyanate?","options":["Friedrich Wohler","Buckminster Fuller","John Dalton","Antoine Lavoisier"],"answer":0,"explain":"Friedrich Wohler prepared urea from ammonium cyanate in 1828, showing organic compounds don't need a living 'vital force'."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't silicon match carbon's catenation ability?","options":["Silicon-hydrogen chains longer than 7-8 atoms become very reactive and unstable","Silicon has no valence electrons at all","Silicon cannot bond with hydrogen","Silicon is a noble gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Silicon forms hydrogen chains only up to about 7-8 atoms before becoming too reactive, unlike carbon's strong, stable chains."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"With how many other atoms can a tetravalent carbon atom form bonds?","answer":4,"explain":"Carbon's valency of four lets it bond with up to 4 other carbon atoms or monovalent atoms."}]},{"id":"cc4","title":"Saturated, Unsaturated & Reactions","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"The Refinery of Reactions only lets calm 'saturated' travellers through one gate, while the wild 'unsaturated' gate is guarded by double and triple bonds that react at the slightest touch. Learn to tell them apart, and how each one transforms under hydrogen or sunlight.","teach":[{"title":"Building ethane, step by step","body":"To build a carbon compound, first link the carbon atoms with a bond, then use hydrogen atoms to satisfy the remaining valencies. Linking two carbons with a single bond (C-C) leaves three valencies free on each, so 6 hydrogens attach to give ethane, C2H6.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Saturated compounds","body":"Compounds like methane, ethane and propane, where carbon atoms are linked only by single bonds, are called saturated compounds. These compounds are normally not very reactive.","visual":"⛽"},{"title":"Unsaturated compounds","body":"Ethene (C2H4) has a double bond between its two carbons, and ethyne (C2H2) has a triple bond. Compounds with double or triple bonds between carbon atoms are called unsaturated compounds, and they are more reactive than saturated ones.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Addition reaction - adding hydrogen","body":"Unsaturated hydrocarbons can add hydrogen in the presence of a catalyst like palladium or nickel, becoming saturated. This addition reaction is used industrially to hydrogenate vegetable oils (unsaturated) using a nickel catalyst.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Substitution reaction - swapping hydrogen","body":"Saturated hydrocarbons are fairly unreactive, but in sunlight, chlorine can replace their hydrogen atoms one by one: CH4 + Cl2 -> CH3Cl + HCl. This is called a substitution reaction because one atom takes the place of another.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A compound whose carbon atoms are linked only by single bonds is called:","options":["Saturated","Unsaturated","Ionic","Aromatic"],"answer":0,"explain":"Single bonds only, between all carbon atoms, define a saturated compound."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ethyne (C2H2) has what kind of bond between its two carbon atoms?","options":["Triple bond","Single bond","Double bond","No bond"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ethyne's two carbons are joined by a triple bond, making it unsaturated."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which catalyst is commonly used to add hydrogen to unsaturated vegetable oils?","options":["Nickel","Sodium","Copper","Iron"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nickel catalyses the hydrogenation (addition reaction) of unsaturated vegetable oils."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"CH4 + Cl2 -> CH3Cl + HCl (in sunlight) is an example of which reaction type?","options":["Substitution reaction","Addition reaction","Combustion reaction","Neutralisation reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"A chlorine atom substitutes for a hydrogen atom, so this is a substitution reaction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many hydrogen atoms are in one molecule of ethane, C2H6?","answer":6,"explain":"Ethane's formula, C2H6, contains 6 hydrogen atoms bonded across its two carbons."}]},{"id":"cc5","title":"Chains, Branches & Rings","icon":"⭕","kind":"lesson","story":"The Carbon Cartographer needs a map of every 'skeleton' a chain of carbons can take - one stretched straight, one branched - both leading to rooms with the very same formula. Beyond them lie the circular corridors: rings like cyclohexane and the famous flat hexagon, benzene.","teach":[{"title":"Chains growing one carbon at a time","body":"Carbon chains can grow to any length: methane (1 carbon), ethane (2), propane (3), butane (4), pentane (5), hexane (6), each following the pattern of adding one more carbon and its hydrogens.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Structural isomers - same formula, different skeleton","body":"Butane (C4H10) can be drawn as a straight 4-carbon chain, or as a branched chain with the same formula. Compounds with an identical molecular formula but different structures are called structural isomers.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Rings of carbon","body":"Some carbon compounds form rings instead of open chains - cyclohexane (C6H12) is one example, and benzene (C6H6) is another. Straight chains, branched chains and rings can all be either saturated or unsaturated.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Hydrocarbon families","body":"Compounds made only of carbon and hydrogen are called hydrocarbons. Saturated hydrocarbons are alkanes, unsaturated ones with double bonds are alkenes, and those with triple bonds are alkynes.","visual":"🏷️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What do we call compounds with the same molecular formula but different structures?","options":["Structural isomers","Homologues","Allotropes","Functional isomers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Structural isomers share a molecular formula (like C4H10) but have different arrangements of atoms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Cyclohexane (C6H12) has its carbon atoms arranged in a:","options":["Ring","Straight chain only","Branched chain only","Double helix"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cyclohexane's six carbon atoms are joined in a ring rather than an open chain."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Saturated hydrocarbons are called:","options":["Alkanes","Alkenes","Alkynes","Alcohols"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydrocarbons with only single bonds between carbons are named alkanes."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many carbon atoms does pentane contain?","answer":5,"explain":"Pentane is the 5-carbon member of the alkane chain series."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Unsaturated hydrocarbons containing one or more triple bonds are called:","options":["Alkynes","Alkanes","Alkenes","Esters"],"answer":0,"explain":"A carbon-carbon triple bond places a hydrocarbon in the alkyne family."}]},{"id":"cc6","title":"Functional Groups & Homologous Series","icon":"🏷️","kind":"lesson","story":"Every chain in this realm can wear a 'badge' - swap one hydrogen for a chlorine, an -OH, or a -COOH, and the compound's whole personality changes, no matter how long its carbon tail is. Learn to read these badges to predict how a whole family of compounds behaves.","teach":[{"title":"Heteroatoms and functional groups","body":"An atom other than carbon or hydrogen - like chlorine, oxygen, nitrogen or sulphur - can replace a hydrogen on a carbon chain, and is called a heteroatom. Groups like -OH (alcohol) or -COOH (carboxylic acid) are called functional groups because they give the compound its characteristic properties, regardless of chain length.","visual":"🏷️"},{"title":"Homologous series","body":"A homologous series is a family of compounds where the same functional group substitutes for hydrogen along carbon chains of increasing length - for example CH3OH, C2H5OH, C3H7OH and C4H9OH all share the -OH group and have very similar chemical properties.","visual":"📶"},{"title":"The -CH2- rule","body":"Successive members of a homologous series differ by a -CH2- unit, which adds 14 u to the molecular mass each time - just as CH4 differs from C2H6, and C2H6 differs from C3H8.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Gradation in properties","body":"As molecular mass increases along a homologous series, physical properties like melting point, boiling point and solubility change gradually. But chemical properties, set by the functional group, remain similar throughout the series.","visual":"📈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a homologous series?","options":["A series of compounds with the same functional group, differing by a -CH2- unit along the chain","A series of compounds with completely unrelated functional groups","A group of isotopes of the same element","A list of compounds with an identical molecular formula"],"answer":0,"explain":"A homologous series keeps the same functional group while the carbon chain grows by -CH2- units each time."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"By how many atomic mass units (u) do successive members of a homologous series, like CH4 and C2H6, typically differ?","answer":14,"explain":"They differ by a -CH2- unit: 1 carbon (12u) + 2 hydrogens (2u) = 14u."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An atom that replaces hydrogen in a hydrocarbon chain and confers specific properties is called a:","options":["Heteroatom","Isotope","Isomer","Catalyst"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heteroatoms like Cl, O, N or S replace hydrogen and, as part of a functional group, define the compound's properties."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which property stays similar across all members of a homologous series?","options":["Chemical properties, because the functional group is the same","Boiling point, because mass doesn't matter","Molecular formula, because all members are identical","Colour, because carbon is always black"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the functional group doesn't change along the series, chemical behaviour stays similar even as physical properties shift gradually."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The functional group -OH represents which class of compound?","options":["Alcohol","Aldehyde","Ketone","Carboxylic acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"The -OH functional group is the defining feature of alcohols."}]},{"id":"cc7","title":"Naming Carbon Compounds","icon":"🔢","kind":"lesson","story":"The Namer's Tower only opens its gate for those who can christen a compound correctly - count the carbons, spot the functional group, and choose the right suffix. Get the prefix or suffix wrong, and the tower relabels you a first-year apprentice.","teach":[{"title":"Step 1 - count the carbons","body":"The first step in naming a carbon compound is identifying the number of carbon atoms in the chain. A compound with three carbon atoms is named propane, based on the basic chain name.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Step 2 - mark the functional group","body":"If a functional group is present, it's shown with a prefix (like chloro- for a halogen) or a suffix (like -ol for alcohol, -al for aldehyde, -one for ketone, -oic acid for carboxylic acid).","visual":"🏷️"},{"title":"The vowel rule","body":"If the functional group's suffix begins with a vowel, the final 'e' of the carbon-chain name is dropped before adding the suffix. For example: propane - 'e' + 'one' = propanone.","visual":"✂️"},{"title":"Naming unsaturated chains","body":"If the carbon chain is unsaturated, the final 'ane' in its name is replaced by 'ene' (double bond) or 'yne' (triple bond) - so a three-carbon chain with a double bond is propene, and with a triple bond, propyne.","visual":"🔀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct name for a three-carbon chain with a ketone group?","options":["Propanone","Propanal","Propanol","Propanoic acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Propane - 'e' + 'one' = propanone, following the vowel rule for ketones."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct name for a three-carbon chain with a double bond?","options":["Propene","Propane","Propyne","Propanol"],"answer":0,"explain":"A double bond changes the '-ane' ending to '-ene', giving propene."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which suffix is used to name a carboxylic acid?","options":["-oic acid","-ol","-al","-one"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carboxylic acids take the suffix '-oic acid', as in propanoic acid."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Butanone is a four-carbon compound with which functional group?","options":["Ketone","Carboxylic acid","Aldehyde","Alcohol"],"answer":0,"explain":"The '-one' suffix in butanone marks it as a ketone."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If a functional group's suffix begins with a vowel, what happens to the carbon-chain name before adding it?","options":["Its final 'e' is dropped","An extra 'e' is added","The chain name is reversed","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"explain":"For example, propane loses its final 'e' before '-one' is added, giving propanone."}]},{"id":"cc8","title":"Combustion & Oxidation","icon":"🕯️","kind":"lesson","story":"The Flame Trial burns everything you throw at it - but watch closely. A clean blue flame means you've mastered complete combustion, while a sooty yellow one means your fuel, and maybe your grade, is going up in smoke.","teach":[{"title":"Combustion releases heat and light","body":"Carbon and its compounds burn in oxygen to give carbon dioxide (and water, for hydrocarbons), releasing heat and light: C + O2 -> CO2, CH4 + O2 -> CO2 + H2O, and ethanol (CH3CH2OH) burns similarly.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Clean flame vs sooty flame","body":"Saturated hydrocarbons generally burn with a clean flame, while unsaturated carbon compounds burn with a yellow, sooty flame that deposits soot on a metal plate held above it.","visual":"🕯️"},{"title":"Limited air means incomplete combustion","body":"Even saturated hydrocarbons give a sooty flame if the air supply is limited. This is why blocked air holes on a gas or kerosene stove blacken the bottom of a cooking vessel and waste fuel.","visual":"🍳"},{"title":"Oxidising alcohols to acids","body":"Alcohols such as ethanol can be oxidised to carboxylic acids using oxidising agents like alkaline potassium permanganate or acidified potassium dichromate, which add oxygen to the starting material.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What two main products form when a carbon compound undergoes complete combustion in oxygen?","options":["Carbon dioxide and water, plus heat and light","Only soot and smoke","Carbon monoxide and nitrogen","Salt and hydrogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Complete combustion of carbon compounds in oxygen releases CO2, water (for hydrocarbons), heat, and light."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A blackened vessel bottom on a gas stove usually means:","options":["The air holes are blocked, causing incomplete combustion","The fuel is burning completely","Too much oxygen is reaching the flame","The food itself is burning"],"answer":0,"explain":"Blocked air holes starve the flame of oxygen, causing incomplete combustion and sooty deposits."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an oxidising agent that can convert ethanol to ethanoic acid?","options":["Alkaline potassium permanganate","Dilute hydrochloric acid","Sodium chloride solution","Distilled water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alkaline potassium permanganate (or acidified potassium dichromate) oxidises ethanol to ethanoic acid by adding oxygen."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do unsaturated carbon compounds tend to burn with a yellow, sooty flame?","options":["Their combustion is often incomplete, releasing unburnt carbon as smoke and soot","They contain no carbon at all","They cannot burn in oxygen","They release only water vapour"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unsaturated compounds burn with more smoke and soot compared to the cleaner flame of saturated hydrocarbons."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the reaction C + O2 -> CO2, how many oxygen atoms bond with one carbon atom?","answer":2,"explain":"One molecule of O2 (2 oxygen atoms) combines with each carbon atom to form CO2."}]},{"id":"cc9","title":"Ethanol & Ethanoic Acid","icon":"🍶","kind":"lesson","story":"Two rival potions sit on the alchemist's shelf: a clear, dangerous spirit and a sharp vinegar-smelling acid. Test how each reacts with sodium, with a base, and with each other, and you'll unlock the secret of the sweet-smelling ester chamber.","teach":[{"title":"Ethanol, the common alcohol","body":"Ethanol is a liquid at room temperature, fully soluble in water, and used as a solvent in medicines like tincture iodine and cough syrups. Even a small quantity of pure ethanol (absolute alcohol) can be lethal.","visual":"🍶"},{"title":"Ethanol reacts with sodium","body":"2Na + 2CH3CH2OH -> 2CH3CH2O-Na+ + H2. Sodium reacts with ethanol to release hydrogen gas, with sodium ethoxide as the other product.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Dehydration of ethanol to ethene","body":"Heating ethanol at 443 K with excess concentrated sulphuric acid - which acts as a dehydrating agent - removes water from ethanol and converts it into ethene.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Ethanoic acid, the vinegar acid","body":"Ethanoic acid (acetic acid) is a carboxylic acid; a 5-8% solution in water is called vinegar. Pure ethanoic acid freezes around 290 K in cold winters, earning it the name 'glacial acetic acid'. Unlike fully-ionised mineral acids like HCl, it is a weak acid.","visual":"🍋"},{"title":"Esterification and saponification","body":"Ethanoic acid reacts with ethanol in the presence of an acid catalyst to form a sweet-smelling ester - this is esterification. Treating that ester with sodium hydroxide converts it back to alcohol and the sodium salt of the acid, a reaction called saponification because it is used to make soap.","visual":"🧫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What gas is produced when sodium reacts with ethanol?","options":["Hydrogen","Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Chlorine"],"answer":0,"explain":"2Na + 2CH3CH2OH gives 2CH3CH2O-Na+ + H2, releasing hydrogen gas."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Heating ethanol with excess concentrated sulphuric acid at 443 K produces:","options":["Ethene, by dehydration","Ethane","Ethyne","Ethanoic acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Concentrated sulphuric acid dehydrates ethanol at 443 K, converting it into ethene."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A 5-8% solution of ethanoic acid in water is commonly known as:","options":["Vinegar","Glacial acid","Absolute alcohol","Baking soda"],"answer":0,"explain":"Vinegar is a dilute (5-8%) solution of ethanoic acid used as a preservative."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The reaction between ethanoic acid and ethanol, using an acid catalyst, that produces a sweet-smelling compound is called:","options":["Esterification","Saponification","Substitution","Neutralisation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Esterification combines an acid and alcohol using an acid catalyst to form a sweet-smelling ester."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the conversion of ethanol to ethanoic acid considered an oxidation reaction?","options":["Because oxygen is added to ethanol by an oxidising agent","Because hydrogen is added to ethanol","Because it releases only heat with no chemical change","Because it forms an ionic bond"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oxidising agents like alkaline potassium permanganate add oxygen to ethanol to form ethanoic acid."}]},{"id":"cc10","title":"Soaps & Detergents","icon":"🧼","kind":"lesson","story":"A stubborn grease stain dares you to wash it away with plain water alone - impossible, until you deploy the micelle squad: molecules with an oil-loving tail and a water-loving head that surround every greasy particle and drag it into the rinse.","teach":[{"title":"What soap molecules are","body":"Soaps are sodium or potassium salts of long-chain carboxylic acids. One end of the molecule is the ionic end, which is hydrophilic and interacts with water, while the long hydrocarbon chain is hydrophobic and interacts with oil.","visual":"🧼"},{"title":"Micelles - the cleaning trick","body":"Soap molecules cluster around an oil droplet with their hydrocarbon tails pointing inward toward the oil and their ionic ends facing outward toward the water. This structure, called a micelle, keeps dirt suspended in water so it can be rinsed away.","visual":"🫧"},{"title":"Hard water and scum","body":"Calcium and magnesium salts in hard water react with soap to form an insoluble, curdy white precipitate called scum, which wastes soap and reduces the foam formed.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Detergents work even in hard water","body":"Detergents are generally sodium salts of sulphonic acids or ammonium salts with long hydrocarbon chains. Their charged ends do not form insoluble precipitates with calcium and magnesium ions, so they stay effective even in hard water.","visual":"🧴"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are soap molecules, chemically?","options":["Sodium or potassium salts of long-chain carboxylic acids","Sodium salts of sulphonic acids","Pure hydrocarbons with no charge","Mineral acids"],"answer":0,"explain":"Soaps are the sodium or potassium salts of long-chain carboxylic acids."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a micelle formed during cleaning, which part of the soap molecule points toward the oil droplet?","options":["The hydrocarbon (hydrophobic) tail","The ionic (hydrophilic) end","Both ends equally","Neither end touches the oil"],"answer":0,"explain":"The hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail faces inward toward the oil, while the ionic end faces outward into the water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The curdy white precipitate (scum) formed when soap is used in hard water is caused by soap reacting with:","options":["Calcium and magnesium salts","Sodium chloride","Carbon dioxide","Potassium salts"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hard water's calcium and magnesium salts react with soap to form an insoluble scum."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do detergents work well even in hard water, unlike soaps?","options":["Their charged ends don't form insoluble precipitates with calcium or magnesium ions","They contain no hydrocarbon chain at all","They are completely insoluble in water","They chemically soften hard water permanently"],"answer":0,"explain":"Detergents' charged ends stay soluble even with calcium/magnesium ions present, so they keep cleaning in hard water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the ionic end of a soap molecule interact with?","options":["Water","Oil","Air","Metal"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ionic (hydrophilic) end of a soap molecule interacts with water."}]},{"id":"cc11","title":"Final Trial: The Carbon Colossus","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the realm looms the Carbon Colossus, built from every bond, chain, ring and functional group you've studied. It won't let you pass on luck - only a traveller who truly understands carbon's versatile nature can bring it down.","boss":{"name":"The Carbon Colossus","taunt":"Four bonds, a million compounds, and only ONE of you. Show me you understand catenation and tetravalency - or be absorbed into my chain forever!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything is connected","body":"Carbon's tetravalency and catenation together explain why it forms millions of compounds - from simple methane to complex chains, rings, and the ethanol and ethanoic acid we use every day.","visual":"⬡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Ethane has the molecular formula C2H6. How many total covalent bonds does one molecule of ethane have (counting the C-C bond and all C-H bonds)?","answer":7,"explain":"Ethane has 1 C-C bond plus 6 C-H bonds, giving 7 covalent bonds in total."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"While cooking, if the bottom of the vessel is getting blackened on the outside, it means:","options":["The fuel is not burning completely","The food is not cooked completely","The fuel is wet","The fuel is burning completely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Blackening (soot) on the vessel bottom is a sign of incomplete combustion of the fuel."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these hydrocarbons can undergo an addition reaction: C2H6, C3H8, C3H6, C2H2, CH4?","options":["C3H6 and C2H2","C2H6 and C3H8","CH4 only","All of them equally"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only unsaturated hydrocarbons (with double or triple bonds) like C3H6 and C2H2 can undergo addition reactions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is hydrogenation, and where is it used industrially?","options":["Addition of hydrogen to unsaturated hydrocarbons using a catalyst like nickel; used to convert vegetable oils to fats","Removal of hydrogen from saturated hydrocarbons; used to make fuels","Addition of oxygen to alcohols; used to make vinegar","Removal of water from alcohols; used to make ethene only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydrogenation adds hydrogen to unsaturated compounds using a catalyst such as nickel, and is used industrially to convert vegetable oils into fats."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How would you experimentally distinguish an alcohol from a carboxylic acid?","options":["The carboxylic acid turns blue litmus red and releases CO2 gas with a carbonate; the alcohol does neither","The alcohol turns blue litmus red; the carboxylic acid does not react with litmus","Both react identically with litmus and carbonates","Neither reacts with litmus or carbonates"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carboxylic acids are acidic (turn blue litmus red) and react with carbonates/hydrogencarbonates to release CO2, while alcohols do not show these reactions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two properties of carbon lead to the huge number of carbon compounds we see around us?","options":["Catenation and tetravalency","Radioactivity and low density","High conductivity and catenation","Tetravalency and high melting point"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carbon's ability to chain with itself (catenation) plus its four bonding positions (tetravalency) together explain the vast diversity of carbon compounds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does micelle formation help soap clean oily dirt from clothes?","options":["Soap molecules surround the oil with hydrocarbon tails inward and ionic ends outward, letting the oil disperse into water","Soap molecules dissolve the oil into individual atoms","Soap reacts chemically with oil to destroy it completely","Soap molecules repel water so oil can float away"],"answer":0,"explain":"Micelles trap oil droplets inside a shell of soap molecules with ionic ends facing the water, allowing the emulsified dirt to be rinsed away."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Balance this combustion equation: CH4 + ? O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O. How many O2 molecules are needed?","answer":2,"explain":"The balanced equation is CH4 + 2O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O, needing 2 molecules of oxygen."}]}]},{"id":"lifeprocesses10","index":4,"name":"Life Processes","subtitle":"Science Chapter 5","emoji":"🫀","palette":{"from":"#fb7185","to":"#881337"},"lessons":[{"id":"lp1","title":"The Maintenance Crew","icon":"🔧","kind":"lesson","story":"Your ship's life-support AI has crashed mid-flight, and its systems must be rebooted using the same four maintenance jobs every living body runs, even while asleep. Diagnose the core life processes before the crew wakes up to a dead ship.","teach":[{"title":"Life Processes","body":"Life processes are the maintenance functions a living organism must keep performing even when it appears to do nothing, such as sitting still or sleeping, to prevent its ordered body structures from breaking down.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Why Multicellular Bodies Need Special Systems","body":"In single-celled organisms the whole body surface can exchange food, gases and wastes directly with the environment. In large multicellular organisms not all cells touch the outside world, so simple diffusion alone cannot meet every cell's needs.","visual":"🦠"},{"title":"Four Core Processes","body":"The maintenance processes that keep an organism alive are nutrition (taking in food), respiration (using oxygen to break down food for energy), transportation (carrying food, oxygen and wastes around the body), and excretion (removing harmful wastes).","visual":"🫀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is simple diffusion NOT enough to meet the oxygen needs of multicellular organisms like humans?","options":["Not all cells of a large multicellular body are in direct contact with the surrounding environment","Oxygen molecules are too heavy to diffuse","Human skin is completely impermeable to gases","Diffusion only happens underwater"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since not every cell touches the outside environment in a large body, food and oxygen taken up at one place must be transported to all other cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The process of acquiring oxygen from outside the body and using it to break down food for cellular energy is called:","options":["respiration","nutrition","excretion","transportation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Respiration is the process of acquiring oxygen and using it in the break-down of food sources for cellular needs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is one of the four maintenance processes described as essential for life?","options":["Excretion","Reproduction","Locomotion","Digestion of light"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter describes nutrition, respiration, transportation and excretion as the maintenance processes essential to life."}]},{"id":"lp2","title":"Autotrophic Nutrition","icon":"🌱","kind":"lesson","story":"You crash-land inside an alien biodome where every plant is starving despite plenty of soil and sunlight. Restore photosynthesis before the whole biosphere collapses.","teach":[{"title":"Autotrophs","body":"Autotrophs, including green plants and some bacteria, make their own food from simple inorganic substances like carbon dioxide and water, using an outside energy source such as sunlight.","visual":"☀️"},{"title":"Photosynthesis Steps","body":"Photosynthesis involves three main steps: absorption of light energy by chlorophyll, conversion of that light energy to chemical energy with splitting of water into hydrogen and oxygen, and reduction of carbon dioxide to carbohydrates.","visual":"🍃"},{"title":"Stomata and Gas Exchange","body":"Stomata are tiny pores on the leaf surface, opened and closed by guard cells, through which carbon dioxide enters and gases are exchanged; since water vapour is also lost through them, plants close stomata when they don't need carbon dioxide.","visual":"⚪"},{"title":"Raw Materials from Soil","body":"Besides carbon dioxide and water, plants take up nitrogen, phosphorus, iron and magnesium from the soil through their roots; nitrogen is essential for making proteins and is absorbed as inorganic nitrates or nitrites.","visual":"🌾"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Photosynthesis converts carbon dioxide and water into carbohydrates using energy absorbed by chlorophyll from:","options":["sunlight","moonlight","heat from the soil","friction of the wind"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chlorophyll absorbs light energy from the sun and converts it to chemical energy used to make carbohydrates."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the tiny pores on a leaf's surface that allow gases to move in and out during photosynthesis?","options":["Stomata","Xylem vessels","Nephrons","Alveoli"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stomata are the pores on the leaf surface through which gaseous exchange for photosynthesis takes place."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which cells control the opening and closing of the stomatal pore?","options":["Guard cells","Root hair cells","Companion cells","Bowman's capsule cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Guard cells swell when water flows into them, opening the stomatal pore, and shrink to close it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Carbohydrates made by a plant but not used immediately are stored in the form of:","options":["starch","glycogen","cellulose only","urea"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unused carbohydrates are stored as starch, the plant's internal energy reserve."}]},{"id":"lp3","title":"Heterotrophic Nutrition","icon":"🍽️","kind":"lesson","story":"Aboard the Biology Ark, creatures from three different feeding guilds are fighting over the last food supply. Sort out who eats how before chaos breaks out.","teach":[{"title":"Heterotrophs","body":"Heterotrophs, including animals and fungi, cannot make their own food and depend directly or indirectly on autotrophs, using bio-catalysts called enzymes to break down complex food substances into simpler ones.","visual":"🍄"},{"title":"Saprophytic Nutrition","body":"Organisms such as bread moulds, yeast and mushrooms break down food material outside their body and then absorb it — this is saprophytic nutrition.","visual":"🍞"},{"title":"Parasitic Nutrition","body":"Some organisms, like cuscuta (amar-bel), ticks, lice, leeches and tapeworms, derive nutrition from other living plants or animals without killing them — this is parasitic nutrition.","visual":"🪱"},{"title":"Nutrition in Amoeba and Paramoecium","body":"Amoeba engulfs food using temporary finger-like extensions that fuse around a food particle to form a food-vacuole, where digestion occurs; Paramoecium instead moves food to a fixed spot using cilia that cover its cell surface.","visual":"🔬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bread moulds, yeast and mushrooms digest their food outside the body and then absorb it. This is an example of:","options":["saprophytic nutrition","parasitic nutrition","autotrophic nutrition","excretion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Breaking down food outside the body before absorbing it is saprophytic nutrition, seen in fungi like bread moulds and yeast."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Cuscuta (amar-bel), ticks and tapeworms obtain nutrition from a living host without killing it. This is called:","options":["parasitic nutrition","saprophytic nutrition","autotrophic nutrition","photosynthetic nutrition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deriving nutrition from another living organism without killing it is the parasitic nutritive strategy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Amoeba, food is engulfed using temporary finger-like extensions of the cell surface that fuse to form a:","options":["food-vacuole","nephron","alveolus","stoma"],"answer":0,"explain":"The finger-like extensions fuse over the food particle to form a food-vacuole where digestion occurs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does Paramoecium move food to the specific spot on its cell where it is taken in?","options":["By the movement of cilia covering its cell surface","By finger-like extensions of its cell membrane","By muscular contractions of a stomach","By pumping with a heart-like organ"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cilia covering the entire surface of Paramoecium beat to move food toward the fixed spot where it is ingested."}]},{"id":"lp4","title":"Digestion: Mouth to Stomach","icon":"👅","kind":"lesson","story":"You've shrunk to the size of a breadcrumb and are riding the first bite of lunch down the alimentary canal. Survive the mouth's grinding teeth and the stomach's acid bath.","teach":[{"title":"The Alimentary Canal","body":"The alimentary canal is a long tube running from the mouth to the anus, with different regions specialised to perform different functions in digesting food.","visual":"🛇"},{"title":"Chewing and Saliva","body":"Teeth crush food into small particles, and salivary glands secrete saliva containing the enzyme salivary amylase, which breaks down starch into simple sugar; the muscular tongue mixes food with saliva while chewing.","visual":"🦷"},{"title":"Peristalsis","body":"Rhythmic contractions of the muscles lining the digestive tube, called peristaltic movements, push food forward all along the gut.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Digestion in the Stomach","body":"The stomach's gastric glands release hydrochloric acid, the protein-digesting enzyme pepsin, and mucus; the acid creates the acidic medium pepsin needs to work, while mucus protects the stomach's own lining from that acid.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which enzyme in saliva breaks down starch into simple sugar?","options":["Salivary amylase","Pepsin","Trypsin","Lipase"],"answer":0,"explain":"Saliva contains salivary amylase, which breaks down the complex molecule starch into simple sugar."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the function of the hydrochloric acid released by gastric glands in the stomach?","options":["It creates an acidic medium that facilitates the action of the enzyme pepsin","It digests fats directly","It stores food before digestion","It absorbs digested nutrients"],"answer":0,"explain":"The acidic medium created by hydrochloric acid is necessary for pepsin to act on proteins."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Rhythmic muscular contractions that push food along the digestive tube are called:","options":["peristaltic movements","transpiration","translocation","diffusion currents"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lining of the canal contracts rhythmically as peristaltic movements to push food forward."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What protects the inner lining of the stomach from being damaged by its own acid?","options":["Mucus","Bile","Pepsin","Saliva"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mucus secreted by gastric glands protects the stomach lining from the action of hydrochloric acid."}]},{"id":"lp5","title":"Digestion: Small Intestine Onward","icon":"🧬","kind":"lesson","story":"Your breadcrumb-sized self exits the stomach's sphincter gate into the coiled maze of the small intestine, where liver bile and pancreatic juice wait to finish the job.","teach":[{"title":"Entering the Small Intestine","body":"A sphincter muscle regulates the exit of food from the stomach, releasing it in small amounts into the small intestine — the longest part of the alimentary canal, coiled to fit into a compact space, and the site of complete digestion of carbohydrates, proteins and fats.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Bile from the Liver","body":"Food from the stomach is acidic and must be made alkaline for pancreatic enzymes to act; bile juice from the liver does this, and also breaks large fat globules into smaller ones, increasing the efficiency of enzyme action on fats.","visual":"🟡"},{"title":"Pancreatic and Intestinal Juices","body":"The pancreas secretes pancreatic juice containing trypsin for digesting proteins and lipase for breaking down emulsified fats; intestinal juice from the small intestine's walls finally converts proteins to amino acids, complex carbohydrates to glucose, and fats to fatty acids and glycerol.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Villi and the Large Intestine","body":"Finger-like projections called villi line the small intestine, increasing surface area for absorption, and are richly supplied with blood vessels that carry absorbed food to every cell; unabsorbed food passes to the large intestine, which absorbs water, and the rest leaves via the anus, regulated by the anal sphincter.","visual":"🩸"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bile juice from the liver makes the acidic food from the stomach alkaline and also helps digest:","options":["fats","proteins","starch","nucleic acids"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bile salts break large fat globules into smaller ones, increasing the efficiency of fat digestion by enzymes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which pancreatic enzyme digests proteins in the small intestine?","options":["Trypsin","Lipase","Pepsin","Salivary amylase"],"answer":0,"explain":"The pancreas secretes trypsin for digesting proteins and lipase for breaking down emulsified fats."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the function of villi in the small intestine?","options":["They increase the surface area for absorption of digested food","They produce hydrochloric acid","They store undigested waste","They pump blood to the heart"],"answer":0,"explain":"Villi are finger-like projections that increase the surface area of the small intestine for absorbing digested food."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Herbivores like cows need a longer small intestine than carnivores like tigers mainly because:","options":["Digesting cellulose from grass takes longer than digesting meat","Herbivores eat more often than carnivores","Carnivores naturally have smaller stomachs","Meat contains no digestible nutrients"],"answer":0,"explain":"A longer small intestine allows more time for the cellulose in grass to be digested, while meat is easier to digest."}]},{"id":"lp6","title":"Respiration: Breaking Down Glucose","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"The ship's engine room runs on a fuel called ATP, and you must choose the right chemical pathway, with or without oxygen, to keep the reactor from stalling.","teach":[{"title":"Glycolysis: The First Step","body":"In all organisms, the first step of releasing energy from food is the break-down of glucose, a six-carbon molecule, into a three-carbon molecule called pyruvate; this happens in the cytoplasm.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Anaerobic Respiration","body":"When pyruvate is converted into ethanol and carbon dioxide in the absence of oxygen, as happens in yeast during fermentation, the process is called anaerobic respiration.","visual":"🍺"},{"title":"Aerobic Respiration","body":"When pyruvate is broken down using oxygen in the mitochondria, it yields three molecules of carbon dioxide plus water; this aerobic respiration releases much more energy than the anaerobic pathway.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Lactic Acid and Cramps","body":"When muscle cells lack sufficient oxygen during sudden activity, pyruvate is instead converted into lactic acid, another three-carbon molecule, and its build-up in the muscles causes cramps.","visual":"🦵"},{"title":"ATP — The Energy Currency","body":"Energy released during respiration is used to make ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate; breaking the terminal phosphate bond in ATP using water releases energy that drives the cell's other activities, like muscle contraction and protein synthesis.","visual":"🔋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The first step in the breakdown of glucose, which produces a three-carbon molecule called pyruvate, takes place in the:","options":["cytoplasm","mitochondria","nucleus","chloroplast"],"answer":0,"explain":"Glucose is broken down into pyruvate in the cytoplasm, the first common step in all organisms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In yeast during fermentation, pyruvate is converted into ethanol and carbon dioxide without using oxygen. This process is called:","options":["anaerobic respiration","aerobic respiration","photosynthesis","translocation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because this pyruvate break-down happens without oxygen, it is called anaerobic respiration."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In our muscle cells, a lack of oxygen during sudden activity causes pyruvate to be converted into a substance that causes cramps. That substance is:","options":["lactic acid","ethanol","glucose","glycogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pyruvate is converted to lactic acid when oxygen is insufficient, and its build-up causes muscle cramps."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where does the oxygen-using breakdown of pyruvate into carbon dioxide and water take place?","options":["Mitochondria","Cytoplasm","Ribosomes","Golgi body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aerobic break-down of pyruvate using oxygen takes place in the mitochondria."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Glucose, the molecule broken down at the start of respiration, contains how many carbon atoms?","answer":6,"explain":"Glucose is described as a six-carbon molecule that is broken down into three-carbon pyruvate."}]},{"id":"lp7","title":"Respiration: Breathing in Animals","icon":"🫁","kind":"lesson","story":"You dive into an aquarium tank disguised as a fish, then leap onto dry land as a human, comparing gills to lungs to survive in both worlds.","teach":[{"title":"Aquatic vs Terrestrial Breathing","body":"Because dissolved oxygen in water is far less concentrated than oxygen in air, aquatic animals like fish breathe much faster than terrestrial animals; fish take in water through their mouths and force it past the gills, where dissolved oxygen is taken up by blood.","visual":"🐟"},{"title":"The Human Airway","body":"In humans, air enters through the nostrils, is filtered by fine hairs and mucus, then passes through the throat, kept open by rings of cartilage, into the lungs.","visual":"👃"},{"title":"Alveoli — Sites of Gas Exchange","body":"Inside the lungs, air passages branch into smaller tubes ending in balloon-like alveoli, which provide a surface, richly supplied with blood vessels, where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged.","visual":"🎈"},{"title":"Breathing Mechanics","body":"When we breathe in, the ribs lift and the diaphragm flattens, enlarging the chest cavity so air is sucked into the lungs and fills the expanded alveoli; a residual volume of air always remains in the lungs, giving enough time for gas exchange.","visual":"🌬️"},{"title":"Haemoglobin","body":"Haemoglobin, the respiratory pigment present in red blood corpuscles, has a very high affinity for oxygen and carries it from the lungs to oxygen-deficient tissues; carbon dioxide, being more soluble in water, is mostly transported in dissolved form in the blood.","visual":"🔴"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do fish breathe much faster than terrestrial animals?","options":["The amount of dissolved oxygen in water is much lower than the amount of oxygen in air","Fish have smaller lungs than terrestrial animals","Water is heavier than air","Fish have no haemoglobin"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since dissolved oxygen is fairly low compared to oxygen in air, aquatic organisms must breathe faster."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The balloon-like structures in the lungs where exchange of gases actually takes place are called:","options":["alveoli","nephrons","stomata","villi"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alveoli are the balloon-like structures with a rich blood supply where gas exchange occurs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to the ribs and diaphragm when we breathe in?","options":["Ribs lift up and the diaphragm flattens, enlarging the chest cavity","Ribs drop down and the diaphragm domes upward, shrinking the chest cavity","Ribs stay still while the diaphragm disappears","Nothing moves; air enters passively without any mechanism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lifting the ribs and flattening the diaphragm enlarges the chest cavity, sucking air into the lungs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which pigment in red blood corpuscles has a high affinity for oxygen and carries it to the tissues?","options":["Haemoglobin","Chlorophyll","Bile","Mucus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Haemoglobin is the respiratory pigment in red blood corpuscles that has a very high affinity for oxygen."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Compared to oxygen, carbon dioxide is transported in the blood mostly:","options":["dissolved in the plasma, since it is more soluble in water than oxygen","bound to haemoglobin, exactly like oxygen","as a solid precipitate in the blood","it is not transported by the blood at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carbon dioxide is more soluble in water than oxygen, so it is mostly transported dissolved in the blood."}]},{"id":"lp8","title":"The Human Circulatory System","icon":"❤️","kind":"lesson","story":"You're piloting a mini-submarine through the heart's four chambers, timing every valve and contraction so oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood never mix.","teach":[{"title":"Blood — Plasma and Cells","body":"Blood is a fluid connective tissue: plasma transports food, carbon dioxide and nitrogenous wastes in dissolved form, red blood corpuscles carry oxygen, and platelets help plug leaks by clotting blood at points of injury.","visual":"🩸"},{"title":"Four Chambers of the Heart","body":"The heart has four chambers — the left atrium, left ventricle, right atrium and right ventricle — that keep oxygen-rich blood separate from carbon-dioxide-rich blood; ventricles have thicker muscular walls than atria because they pump blood to distant organs.","visual":"❤️"},{"title":"Double Circulation","body":"Blood passes through the heart twice in one complete cycle around the body — once on its way to the lungs for oxygenation and once on its way to the rest of the body — this is called double circulation, useful for animals with high energy needs like birds and mammals.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Blood Vessels","body":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure and so have thick elastic walls; veins carry blood back to the heart at low pressure and have valves to keep blood flowing one way; capillaries are one-cell-thick vessels where materials are exchanged with surrounding cells.","visual":"🩹"},{"title":"Lymph","body":"Lymph, or tissue fluid, forms when plasma, proteins and cells leak through capillary pores into intercellular spaces; it carries digested fat absorbed from the intestine and drains excess fluid from tissues back into the blood via lymph vessels.","visual":"💧"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do mammals and birds need to keep oxygenated and de-oxygenated blood completely separate in the heart?","options":["It allows a highly efficient oxygen supply, needed by animals that constantly use energy to maintain body temperature","It keeps the heart cooler in hot weather","It makes the heart beat more slowly","It has no real functional benefit; it is only structural"],"answer":0,"explain":"Full separation gives a highly efficient oxygen supply, important for animals with high, constant energy needs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Blood passing through the heart twice during one complete cycle around the body is called:","options":["double circulation","single circulation","translocation","transpiration"],"answer":0,"explain":"In vertebrates other than fish, blood goes through the heart twice per cycle, called double circulation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which blood vessels have thick, elastic walls because blood leaves the heart under high pressure through them?","options":["Arteries","Veins","Capillaries","Lymph vessels"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure, so they need thick, elastic walls."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the function of platelets in the blood?","options":["They help clot the blood and plug leaks at points of injury","They carry oxygen to the tissues","They digest proteins in the small intestine","They produce urine in the kidneys"],"answer":0,"explain":"Platelet cells circulate around the body and help clot blood at points of injury to plug leaks."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many chambers does the human heart have?","answer":4,"explain":"The human heart has four chambers: left atrium, left ventricle, right atrium and right ventricle."}]},{"id":"lp9","title":"Transportation in Plants","icon":"🌳","kind":"lesson","story":"You shrink down to travel inside a giant tree's trunk, riding a water molecule up through the xylem and a sugar molecule back down through the phloem.","teach":[{"title":"Xylem — Water and Mineral Transport","body":"Xylem tissue forms a continuous system of interconnected vessels and tracheids that carries water and minerals absorbed by the roots up through the stem to the leaves.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Root Pressure and Transpiration Pull","body":"Root cells actively take up ions from soil, drawing water into the root by diffusion and creating root pressure; but the main force lifting water to great heights during the day is transpiration pull, created by suction as water evaporates from leaf cells through stomata.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Transpiration's Other Roles","body":"Besides pulling water and dissolved minerals upward from roots to leaves, transpiration — the loss of water vapour from the aerial parts of the plant — also helps regulate the plant's temperature.","visual":"🍃"},{"title":"Phloem — Translocation","body":"Phloem transports the soluble products of photosynthesis, along with amino acids and other substances, from the leaves to storage organs like roots, fruits and seeds and to growing parts of the plant, in a process called translocation.","visual":"🍬"},{"title":"Phloem Uses Energy","body":"Unlike xylem transport, which relies mostly on physical forces, translocation in phloem uses energy from ATP to load sucrose into sieve tubes, raising osmotic pressure so water moves in and pushes material toward tissues that need it, in either direction.","visual":"⚡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which plant tissue transports water and minerals from the roots to the rest of the plant?","options":["Xylem","Phloem","Epidermis","Cambium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Xylem vessels and tracheids form the continuous water-conducting system from roots to leaves."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The loss of water vapour from the aerial parts of a plant, which creates the main pulling force for water movement in xylem, is known as:","options":["transpiration","translocation","respiration","excretion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Transpiration is the loss of water vapour from aerial parts, and it drives the transpiration pull."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Translocation of food in the phloem takes place through which structures, with the help of companion cells?","options":["Sieve tubes","Xylem vessels","Guard cells","Root hairs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Food and other substances are translocated through phloem's sieve tubes, aided by companion cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Unlike the mostly physical movement of water in xylem, translocation of food in phloem requires:","options":["energy from ATP","sunlight directly on the phloem","no energy at all","oxygen absorbed only through stomata"],"answer":0,"explain":"Loading sucrose into phloem tissue uses energy from ATP, which raises osmotic pressure to move material."}]},{"id":"lp10","title":"Excretion: Humans and Plants","icon":"🚽","kind":"lesson","story":"Your final infiltration target is the body's own filtration plant: the kidneys. Trace a drop of blood through a nephron, then see how plants handle their own trash without a single kidney.","teach":[{"title":"The Human Excretory System","body":"The human excretory system consists of a pair of kidneys, a pair of ureters, a urinary bladder and a urethra; urine made in the kidneys travels through the ureters to the bladder, where it is stored until released through the urethra.","visual":"🫘"},{"title":"Nephrons — The Filtration Units","body":"Each kidney contains large numbers of filtration units called nephrons; each nephron's cup-shaped Bowman's capsule collects filtrate from a cluster of thin-walled blood capillaries.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Selective Reabsorption","body":"As urine flows along the nephron tube, useful substances such as glucose, amino acids, salts and much of the water are selectively reabsorbed back into the blood, with the amount of water reabsorbed depending on how much excess water and dissolved waste the body needs to get rid of.","visual":"♻️"},{"title":"Excretion in Plants","body":"Plants use very different strategies for excretion than animals: oxygen and carbon dioxide are handled through diffusion, excess water leaves through transpiration, and other wastes are stored in cellular vacuoles, in leaves that later fall off, or as resins and gums, especially in old xylem, or are excreted into the surrounding soil.","visual":"🌿"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The cup-shaped structure in a nephron that collects filtrate from a cluster of blood capillaries is called the:","options":["Bowman's capsule","alveolus","stoma","atrium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each capillary cluster in the kidney is associated with a cup-shaped Bowman's capsule that collects the filtrate."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which substances are selectively reabsorbed back into the blood as urine flows through the nephron tube?","options":["Glucose, amino acids, salts and most of the water","Only carbon dioxide","Only urea","Only red blood cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Useful substances like glucose, amino acids, salts and a major amount of water are selectively reabsorbed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a strategy plants use to get rid of waste?","options":["Storing wastes in cell vacuoles, in leaves that fall off, or as resins and gums","Filtering wastes through nephrons in a kidney","Pumping wastes through a four-chambered heart","Breathing wastes out through alveoli"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plants store waste in vacuoles or shed parts like leaves, and store some wastes as resins and gums."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The tubes that carry urine from each kidney to the urinary bladder are called:","options":["ureters","urethra","xylem vessels","lymph vessels"],"answer":0,"explain":"Urine formed in each kidney enters the ureter, a long tube connecting the kidney to the urinary bladder."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many kidneys does a normal human excretory system contain?","answer":2,"explain":"The human excretory system includes a pair of kidneys, located one on either side of the backbone."}]},{"id":"lp11","title":"Boss: The Homeostat","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","story":"Deep inside the ship's failing life-support core stands the Homeostat, a guardian built from every maintenance process you've mastered. Prove that nutrition, respiration, transportation and excretion can outlast entropy itself, or the whole system shuts down for good.","boss":{"name":"The Homeostat","taunt":"Order is an illusion, and entropy is patient. Prove your body's maintenance systems can outlast mine!"},"teach":[{"title":"Review: Nutrition","body":"Autotrophs make food using carbon dioxide, water and sunlight via photosynthesis; heterotrophs get complex food from other organisms through saprophytic, parasitic or holozoic strategies; in humans, digestion runs from mouth to anus with help from saliva, gastric juice, bile, pancreatic juice and intestinal juice.","visual":"🍽️"},{"title":"Review: Respiration","body":"Glucose is first broken into pyruvate in the cytoplasm; aerobic respiration in the mitochondria yields much more energy than anaerobic respiration (fermentation or lactic acid formation), and the released energy is stored in ATP.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Review: Transportation","body":"In humans, the heart's four chambers, blood vessels and lymph move oxygen, food and wastes around the body through double circulation; in plants, xylem carries water and minerals upward while phloem translocates food using energy from ATP.","visual":"🫀"},{"title":"Review: Excretion","body":"Human kidneys filter blood through nephrons, reabsorb useful substances, and excrete urine via the ureters, bladder and urethra; plants get rid of waste through diffusion, transpiration, shedding leaves, and storing resins, gums or wastes in the soil.","visual":"🚽"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct order of parts through which food passes in the human alimentary canal?","options":["Mouth to oesophagus to stomach to small intestine to large intestine to anus","Mouth to stomach to oesophagus to large intestine to small intestine to anus","Mouth to small intestine to stomach to oesophagus to anus","Mouth to anus to stomach to small intestine"],"answer":0,"explain":"The alimentary canal runs from the mouth through the oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, to the anus."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which organelle is the site of aerobic breakdown of pyruvate into carbon dioxide and water?","options":["Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Nucleus","Golgi body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aerobic respiration, which breaks down pyruvate using oxygen, takes place in the mitochondria."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do birds and mammals have hearts that fully separate oxygenated and deoxygenated blood?","options":["It allows a highly efficient oxygen supply, needed by animals that constantly expend energy to maintain body temperature","It makes their hearts beat slower","It is needed only for flight, not for other mammals","It has no real advantage over a two- or three-chambered heart"],"answer":0,"explain":"Full separation of blood streams gives an efficient oxygen supply, useful for animals with constant high energy needs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In plants, which tissue carries water and minerals, and which carries the products of photosynthesis?","options":["Xylem carries water and minerals; phloem carries products of photosynthesis","Phloem carries water and minerals; xylem carries products of photosynthesis","Both xylem and phloem carry only water","Both xylem and phloem carry only food"],"answer":0,"explain":"Xylem moves water and minerals from the soil, while phloem transports products of photosynthesis from the leaves."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the basic filtration unit of the kidney, where blood is filtered to eventually form urine?","options":["Nephron","Alveolus","Villus","Sieve tube"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nephron, containing the Bowman's capsule and its capillary cluster, is the kidney's basic filtration unit."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many molecules of carbon dioxide are produced when one three-carbon pyruvate molecule is fully broken down via aerobic respiration in the mitochondria?","answer":3,"explain":"Aerobic breakdown of the three-carbon pyruvate molecule yields three molecules of carbon dioxide, plus water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which blood vessels have walls only one cell thick, allowing exchange of material between blood and surrounding cells?","options":["Capillaries","Arteries","Veins","Lymph vessels"],"answer":0,"explain":"Capillaries are the smallest vessels, with walls one cell thick, where exchange with cells takes place."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is produced when yeast performs anaerobic respiration (fermentation) on sugar?","options":["Ethanol and carbon dioxide","Lactic acid and oxygen","Glucose and water","Urea and uric acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"During fermentation, yeast converts pyruvate into ethanol and carbon dioxide without using oxygen."}]}]},{"id":"controlcoordination10","index":5,"name":"Control and Coordination","subtitle":"Science Chapter 6","emoji":"🧠","palette":{"from":"#a78bfa","to":"#4c1d95"},"lessons":[{"id":"cd1","title":"Signals in the Synapse Maze","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the glowing Neuron Network zone of the Study Atlas, where signals race along glowing threads. To pass through the flickering synapse gates ahead, you must learn how a nerve cell detects a stimulus and passes it on.","teach":[{"title":"Receptors detect the world","body":"All information from our environment is detected by the specialised tips of some nerve cells, called receptors. These are usually located in our sense organs, such as the inner ear, nose, tongue and eyes. For example, gustatory receptors detect taste while olfactory receptors detect smell.","visual":"👃"},{"title":"Inside a neuron","body":"Information is acquired at the dendritic tip of a nerve cell, which sets off a chemical reaction creating an electrical impulse. This impulse travels from the dendrite to the cell body, and then along the axon to its end.","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"Crossing the synapse","body":"At the end of the axon, the electrical impulse causes the release of chemicals. These chemicals cross the gap, or synapse, and start a similar electrical impulse in a dendrite of the next neuron, letting the signal travel onward, even to muscles or glands.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Nervous tissue","body":"Nervous tissue is made up of an organised network of nerve cells, or neurons, and is specialised for conducting information via electrical impulses from one part of the body to another.","visual":"🕸️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of a neuron first acquires information from the environment?","options":["Dendritic tip","Axon","Cell body","Synapse"],"answer":0,"explain":"Information is acquired at the dendritic tip, which starts the electrical impulse."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the gap between two neurons called?","options":["Synapse","Axon","Dendrite","Impulse"],"answer":0,"explain":"The synapse is the gap chemicals cross to pass the signal to the next neuron."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which receptors are responsible for detecting smell?","options":["Olfactory receptors","Gustatory receptors","Motor neurons","Cell body receptors"],"answer":0,"explain":"Olfactory receptors detect smell, while gustatory receptors detect taste."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does a signal actually get across the synapse to the next neuron?","options":["Chemicals released at the axon end cross the gap and trigger a new impulse","The electrical impulse jumps directly across as electricity","The impulse stops permanently at the synapse","Muscle proteins physically carry it across"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chemicals released at the end of the axon cross the synapse and start a similar impulse in the next neuron's dendrite."}]},{"id":"cd2","title":"The Reflex Arc Escape","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"A torch topples toward your hand in the Study Atlas trial room! There's no time to think it through — you must trust your reflex arc to snatch your hand away before the brain even finishes processing the danger.","teach":[{"title":"What is a reflex?","body":"A reflex is a sudden action in response to something in our environment that we do without consciously thinking about it, such as pulling a hand back from a flame or the mouth watering when very hungry.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Why not just think about it?","body":"Thinking is a complex activity involving many interacting nerve impulses from many neurons in the brain, so it takes time. If we had to consciously think about pain before moving our hand from a flame, we might get burnt first.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"The reflex arc","body":"Rather than routing signals all the way to the brain first, the nerve that detects a stimulus is connected to the nerve that moves muscles at the point where they first meet: the spinal cord. This shortcut connection is called a reflex arc, and it lets input and output happen very quickly, even though the information also travels on to the brain.","visual":"🦴"},{"title":"Why reflex arcs evolved","body":"Reflex arcs evolved because the brain's thinking process is not fast enough for urgent, dangerous situations. Many animals have very little or no complex neuron network for thinking, so reflex arcs are an efficient way to respond even without true thought.","visual":"🐾"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where in the body are most reflex arcs formed?","options":["Spinal cord","Fore-brain","Cerebellum","Adrenal gland"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nerves meet in a bundle in the spinal cord, and reflex arcs form there for a quick response."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are reflex actions faster than consciously thought-out responses?","options":["The reflex arc connects input and output nerves directly in the spinal cord, skipping slower brain processing","Reflexes use hormones instead of nerves","Reflexes only involve the fore-brain","Reflexes do not need receptors at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The reflex arc lets signals travel from an input nerve to an output nerve at the spinal cord itself, which is much quicker than routing through the brain's complex thinking networks."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key difference between a reflex action and walking?","options":["A reflex is a sudden, automatic response completed via the spinal cord, while walking is a voluntary action directed by the brain","Both are involuntary actions controlled only by the spinal cord","Walking happens faster than any reflex action","A reflex requires conscious thought, but walking does not"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reflex actions are quick, automatic responses via the spinal cord, whereas walking is a voluntary action that the brain consciously controls."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of a reflex action?","options":["Pulling your hand back the instant it touches a hot flame","Reading a chapter of a book","Riding a bicycle down a straight road","Solving a maths problem in your head"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pulling a hand back from a flame is a classic reflex action, an immediate response that does not wait for conscious thought."}]},{"id":"cd3","title":"Three Kingdoms of the Brain","icon":"🧠","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Skull Fortress, three brain regions guard different powers: thought, involuntary control, and balance. Explore the fore-brain, mid-brain and hind-brain, and find the shock-absorbing vault that keeps them safe.","teach":[{"title":"The central and peripheral nervous systems","body":"The brain and spinal cord together receive information from all parts of the body, integrate it, and constitute the central nervous system. Cranial nerves from the brain and spinal nerves from the spinal cord form the peripheral nervous system, which connects the central nervous system to the rest of the body.","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"The fore-brain: thinking and sensing","body":"The fore-brain is the main thinking part of the brain. It has separate areas specialised for hearing, smell, sight and so on, plus association areas that interpret this sensory information together with information already stored in the brain, before sending decisions to the motor areas that control voluntary muscles. It also contains a centre linked to the sensation of hunger.","visual":"💭"},{"title":"Mid-brain and hind-brain: involuntary control","body":"Many involuntary actions, such as blood pressure, salivation and vomiting, are controlled by the medulla, part of the hind-brain, along with the mid-brain. These happen without our thinking about them, unlike voluntary actions.","visual":"❤️"},{"title":"The cerebellum: balance and precision","body":"The cerebellum, also part of the hind-brain, is responsible for precision of voluntary actions and for maintaining the posture and balance of the body, which is why we can walk in a straight line, ride a bicycle, or pick up a pencil smoothly.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Protecting the nervous tissue","body":"The delicate brain sits inside a bony box, the skull, and is further cushioned by a fluid-filled covering that absorbs shocks. The spinal cord is likewise protected by the vertebral column, or backbone, the hard bumpy structure you can feel running down the middle of your back.","visual":"🛡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of the brain maintains posture and balance of the body?","options":["Cerebellum","Medulla","Fore-brain","Mid-brain"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cerebellum is responsible for precision of voluntary actions and maintaining posture and balance."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which structure controls involuntary actions like blood pressure, salivation and vomiting?","options":["Medulla in the hind-brain","Cerebellum","Fore-brain","Spinal nerves alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"The medulla, part of the hind-brain, controls these involuntary actions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is the brain protected inside the skull?","options":["It sits inside a bony box and is cushioned by a fluid-filled covering for shock absorption","It is protected only by the vertebral column","It has no protective structures at all","It is protected by muscle tissue alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"The brain sits in a bony box and is cushioned by a fluid-filled covering that provides shock absorption."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The brain and spinal cord together make up the:","options":["Central nervous system","Peripheral nervous system","Endocrine system","Reflex arc"],"answer":0,"explain":"The brain and spinal cord constitute the central nervous system."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many major regions does the human brain have (fore-brain, mid-brain, hind-brain)?","answer":3,"explain":"The brain has three major parts: fore-brain, mid-brain and hind-brain."}]},{"id":"cd4","title":"The Muscle Engine Room","icon":"💪","kind":"lesson","story":"A rope bridge is collapsing behind you and your legs must launch you across, now. Duck into the Muscle Engine Room to see exactly how a nerve signal gets turned into a burst of movement.","teach":[{"title":"From decision to action","body":"Nervous tissue collects information, sends it around the body, processes it, makes decisions based on it, and finally conveys those decisions to muscles for action. Muscle tissue does the final job of actually producing the movement.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"How a muscle cell moves","body":"When a nerve impulse reaches a muscle, the muscle fibre must move. Muscle cells have special proteins that change both their shape and their arrangement in the cell in response to the nervous electrical impulse, and this new arrangement gives the muscle cell a shorter form.","visual":"💪"},{"title":"Voluntary and involuntary muscles","body":"Some muscle movements, like moving a chair or clapping, are voluntary actions we consciously decide to make. Others, like the beating of the heart, are involuntary and happen without our thinking about them at all.","visual":"🫀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How do muscle cells actually produce movement at the cellular level?","options":["Special proteins in the cell change shape and arrangement, shortening the muscle cell","Muscle cells split into two smaller cells","Muscle cells absorb water and swell up","Muscle cells generate their own independent electrical impulses"],"answer":0,"explain":"Special proteins change shape and rearrange in response to the nerve impulse, giving the muscle cell a shorter form."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What directly triggers a muscle fibre to move?","options":["A nerve impulse reaching the muscle","A rise in blood sugar only","Growth of the muscle cell over days","A change in air temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"When a nerve impulse reaches the muscle, the muscle fibre moves in response."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Clapping your hands at the end of a programme is an example of:","options":["A voluntary action, consciously decided by the brain","An involuntary action","A reflex action only","Chemotropism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Clapping is a voluntary action based on deciding what to do next, unlike involuntary actions such as heartbeat."}]},{"id":"cd5","title":"The Whispering Garden","icon":"🌿","kind":"lesson","story":"In the Whispering Garden zone of the Study Atlas, a touch-me-not plant folds its leaves the instant your avatar brushes past, while nearby a pea tendril slowly curls around a fence post. Neither plant has nerves or muscles — so how do they move?","teach":[{"title":"Plants respond without nerves or muscles","body":"Plants have neither a nervous system nor muscles, yet they still respond to stimuli. They use electrical-chemical means to convey information from cell to cell, though unlike animals, there is no specialised tissue in plants for conducting this information.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"Immediate response: the sensitive plant","body":"Touching the leaves of the sensitive plant, or chhui-mui, makes them fold up and droop very quickly. This movement does not involve any growth; instead, the plant's cells change shape by changing the amount of water in them, causing swelling or shrinking.","visual":"🤏"},{"title":"Movement due to growth: tendrils","body":"Pea plants climb using tendrils that are sensitive to touch. When a tendril contacts a support, the part touching the object grows more slowly than the part away from it, so the tendril circles around the object and clings to it.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"Two kinds of plant movement","body":"Plants show two different types of movement: one that depends on growth, such as a tendril coiling or a seedling's root and stem emerging, which stops if growth is prevented, and another independent of growth, such as the sensitive plant folding its leaves, which happens quickly because no growth is needed.","visual":"🔀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does the sensitive plant's leaf move so quickly right after being touched?","options":["Its cells change shape by changing the amount of water in them","Its cells contract using muscle proteins like animal cells","Its leaves grow instantly toward the touch","It sends an electrical impulse through nerve cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plant cells in the sensitive plant change shape by changing their water content, causing swelling or shrinking, with no growth involved."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does a pea tendril come to circle around a support?","options":["The side of the tendril touching the support grows more slowly than the side away from it","The tendril contracts like an animal muscle","The whole tendril immediately stops growing","The tendril releases a hormone that dissolves the support"],"answer":0,"explain":"Slower growth on the touching side compared to the far side makes the tendril curl around the support."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these plant movements does NOT involve growth?","options":["Folding of the sensitive plant's leaves when touched","A seedling's root growing downward into the soil","A shoot bending towards a window","A pea tendril coiling around a fence"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sensitive plant's leaf-folding is an immediate response with no growth involved, unlike the other examples."}]},{"id":"cd6","title":"The Tropism Compass","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"The garden's compass needle is spinning wildly — shoots stretch toward sunlight, roots dive down for water, and a pollen tube races toward a distant ovule. Learn the hormones and tropisms that steer where a plant grows.","teach":[{"title":"Tropic movements","body":"Environmental triggers such as light or gravity change the direction in which plant parts grow. These directional, or tropic, movements can be either towards the stimulus or away from it.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Phototropism and geotropism","body":"In phototropism, shoots respond by bending towards light while roots respond by bending away from it. In geotropism, roots always grow downwards and shoots usually grow upwards, in response to the pull of gravity.","visual":"☀️"},{"title":"Hydrotropism and chemotropism","body":"Hydrotropism is directional growth in response to water, and chemotropism is directional growth in response to a chemical stimulus. One example of chemotropism is the growth of pollen tubes towards ovules.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Auxin steers the shoot toward light","body":"Auxin is a hormone synthesised at the shoot tip that helps cells grow longer. When light comes from one side, auxin diffuses towards the shadier side of the shoot, making cells there grow longer, so the shoot appears to bend towards the light.","visual":"🌻"},{"title":"Other plant hormones","body":"Gibberellins, like auxins, help in the growth of the stem. Cytokinins promote cell division and are found in greater concentration in areas of rapid cell division, such as fruits and seeds. Abscisic acid, by contrast, inhibits growth, and its effects include the wilting of leaves.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hormone diffuses to the shadier side of a shoot, making it bend towards light?","options":["Auxin","Gibberellin","Cytokinin","Abscisic acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Auxin diffuses towards the shady side of the shoot and stimulates those cells to grow longer, bending the shoot towards light."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is a plant hormone?","options":["Cytokinin","Insulin","Thyroxin","Oestrogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cytokinin is a plant hormone that promotes cell division; the others are animal hormones."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The growth of a pollen tube towards an ovule is an example of:","options":["Chemotropism, growth in response to a chemical stimulus","Geotropism, growth in response to gravity","Phototropism, growth in response to light","An immediate response with no growth"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chemotropism is directional growth in response to a chemical stimulus, and pollen tube growth towards the ovule is a classic example."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which plant hormone inhibits growth and can cause wilting of leaves?","options":["Abscisic acid","Auxin","Gibberellin","Cytokinin"],"answer":0,"explain":"Abscisic acid inhibits growth, with effects including wilting of leaves."}]},{"id":"cd7","title":"The Adrenaline Alarm","icon":"🩸","kind":"lesson","story":"A sudden roar echoes through the jungle biome. Your heart pounds, your breathing quickens, your muscles tense — that's your endocrine system sounding the fight-or-flight alarm before you've even decided what to do.","teach":[{"title":"Hormones: a second control system","body":"If a chemical signal is released instead of an electrical impulse, it can diffuse through the blood to reach all cells of the body, giving wide-ranging, if slower, coordination. Such hormones are part of the endocrine system, a second way of control and coordination in the body, alongside the nervous system.","visual":"🩸"},{"title":"Adrenaline: the fight-or-flight hormone","body":"In a scary situation, adrenaline is secreted directly into the blood from the adrenal glands. It makes the heart beat faster, supplying more oxygen to muscles, reduces blood flow to the digestive system and skin so blood is diverted to skeletal muscles, and increases the breathing rate.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Thyroxin and iodine","body":"The thyroid gland needs iodine to make the hormone thyroxin, which regulates carbohydrate, protein and fat metabolism for the best balance of growth. If iodine is deficient in the diet, it can cause goitre, one symptom of which is a swollen neck.","visual":"🦋"},{"title":"Growth hormone and the pituitary","body":"Growth hormone, secreted by the pituitary gland, regulates the growth and development of the body. A deficiency of growth hormone during childhood leads to dwarfism.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Hormones of puberty","body":"The dramatic changes in appearance seen around 10 to 12 years of age are due to the secretion of testosterone in males and oestrogen in females.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Insulin and diabetes","body":"Insulin is a hormone produced by the pancreas that helps regulate blood sugar levels. If it is not secreted in proper amounts, the sugar level in the blood rises, causing many harmful effects; people with diabetes may need insulin injections as treatment.","visual":"🍬"},{"title":"Feedback keeps hormones balanced","body":"The timing and amount of hormone released are regulated by feedback mechanisms. For example, when blood sugar levels rise, pancreas cells detect this and produce more insulin, and as blood sugar falls, insulin secretion is reduced again.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which gland secretes adrenaline directly into the blood?","options":["Adrenal gland","Pituitary gland","Thyroid gland","Pancreas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adrenaline is secreted from the adrenal glands into the blood in response to a scary or urgent situation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is it advisable to use iodised salt?","options":["Iodine is needed by the thyroid gland to make thyroxin; a deficiency can cause goitre","Iodine directly regulates blood sugar levels","Iodine is a plant hormone precursor","Iodine controls the reflex arc"],"answer":0,"explain":"Iodine is essential for the thyroid gland to synthesise thyroxin, and its deficiency can cause goitre."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A deficiency of growth hormone during childhood leads to:","options":["Dwarfism","Diabetes","Goitre","Gigantism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Growth hormone regulates growth and development; a childhood deficiency leads to dwarfism."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hormone, produced by the pancreas, regulates blood sugar levels?","options":["Insulin","Adrenaline","Testosterone","Growth hormone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Insulin, produced by the pancreas, regulates blood sugar levels."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hormones are responsible for the body changes seen around puberty?","options":["Testosterone in males and oestrogen in females","Insulin and adrenaline","Auxin and gibberellin","Thyroxin and growth hormone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Testosterone in males and oestrogen in females cause the dramatic changes associated with puberty."}]},{"id":"cd8","title":"Showdown: The Coordination Overlord","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Study Atlas fortress waits the Coordination Overlord, a being made of tangled nerves, drifting hormones and bending stems. It challenges you to prove that you truly understand how nerves, hormones and plants control and coordinate every move.","teach":[{"title":"Two systems of control","body":"Control and coordination in the body are the combined functions of the nervous system, which uses fast electrical impulses through neurons and reflex arcs, and hormones, which diffuse through the blood more slowly but reach every cell.","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"Plants coordinate too","body":"Plants coordinate without any nervous system, muscles, or specialised conducting tissue. They rely on immediate water-driven movements, like the sensitive plant folding, and on slower, hormone-driven tropic growth, like shoots bending towards light.","visual":"🌿"}],"boss":{"name":"The Coordination Overlord","taunt":"Fast reflexes, slow hormones, bending stems — you think you can out-coordinate me, cadet? Answer, before your synapse gates seal shut!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the gap between two neurons called?","options":["Synapse","Axon","Dendrite","Impulse"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chemicals cross the synapse to pass the impulse on to the next neuron."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where are most reflex arcs formed, allowing a quick response to danger?","options":["Spinal cord","Fore-brain","Adrenal gland","Pancreas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reflex arcs form in the spinal cord, letting input and output nerves connect quickly without waiting for the brain."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of the brain is responsible for balance and precision of voluntary movements?","options":["Cerebellum","Medulla","Adrenal gland","Pancreas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cerebellum maintains posture, balance, and the precision of voluntary actions."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many major regions does the human brain have?","answer":3,"explain":"The brain has three major regions: fore-brain, mid-brain and hind-brain."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hormone helps a plant shoot bend towards light?","options":["Auxin","Insulin","Thyroxin","Adrenaline"],"answer":0,"explain":"Auxin diffuses to the shady side of the shoot, making those cells grow longer so the shoot bends towards light."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which gland releases adrenaline to prepare the body for fight or flight?","options":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pituitary gland","Ovary"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adrenaline is secreted from the adrenal glands directly into the blood."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hormone, produced by the pancreas, regulates blood sugar and is used to treat diabetes?","options":["Insulin","Auxin","Testosterone","Growth hormone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Insulin is produced by the pancreas to regulate blood sugar levels."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is the sensitive plant's quick leaf-folding different from a shoot slowly bending towards light?","options":["Leaf-folding is an immediate water-driven response with no growth, while bending towards light is a slower hormone-driven growth response","Both involve identical muscle proteins","Leaf-folding involves growth while bending towards light does not","Neither response involves any plant hormone or water change"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sensitive plant's leaf-folding is a fast, growth-independent response caused by water movement in cells, while phototropic bending is a slower response driven by auxin-controlled growth."}]}]},{"id":"reproduction10","index":6,"name":"How do Organisms Reproduce?","subtitle":"Science Chapter 7","emoji":"🌱","palette":{"from":"#34d399","to":"#065f46"},"lessons":[{"id":"rp1","title":"Why Do Organisms Reproduce?","icon":"🧬","kind":"lesson","story":"Your team's scanner picks up a mysterious signal from a single-celled organism deep in a cave lake. Before you can classify it, you need to understand why organisms bother copying themselves at all — and how those copies are never quite perfect.","teach":[{"title":"Reproduction Isn't About Survival","body":"Unlike nutrition, respiration, or excretion, reproduction is not necessary to keep an individual organism alive. Yet organisms spend a lot of energy creating new individuals like themselves.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Copying the Blueprint","body":"Organisms look similar because the DNA blueprint for body design, held in chromosomes, is copied and passed on. A basic event in reproduction is making a DNA copy along with new cellular apparatus, after which the cell divides into two.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Variation Keeps Species Alive","body":"No biochemical copying reaction is perfectly accurate, so each DNA copy has small variations from the original. Some variations are harmful, but others let a population survive if its niche suddenly changes, such as a rise in water temperature.","visual":"🌡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which life process is reproduction NOT necessary for, unlike nutrition or respiration?","options":["Maintaining the life of an individual organism","Producing offspring","Passing on DNA","Creating variation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reproduction, unlike nutrition or respiration, isn't needed to keep a single organism alive — only to create new individuals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is created inside a reproducing cell before it divides into two?","options":["A copy of the DNA plus additional cellular apparatus","A second nucleus only, with no new apparatus","An identical twin cell instantly, with no copying","Extra mitochondria only"],"answer":0,"explain":"DNA copying is accompanied by the creation of additional cellular apparatus, and then the cell divides."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are the two daughter cells produced by DNA copying not perfectly identical?","options":["No biochemical copying reaction is completely accurate","They always come from different parent species","One cell always mutates on purpose to survive","DNA copying deliberately skips sections each time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copying reactions always have some variation, making daughter cells similar but not identical."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does variation help a population of bacteria survive a rise in water temperature from global warming?","options":["A few heat-resistant variants survive and grow further","All the bacteria instantly become heat resistant","Variation causes the entire population to die","Bacteria stop reproducing to conserve energy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most bacteria would die, but the few variants already resistant to heat would survive and multiply."}]},{"id":"rp2","title":"Fission — Splitting Into New Cells","icon":"🦠","kind":"lesson","story":"A vial of pond water in the lab is teeming with life. Zoom into single cells splitting cleanly in two, and one sneaky parasite dividing into many at once, to map out fission before your report is due.","teach":[{"title":"Binary Fission","body":"Many bacteria and protozoa simply split into two equal halves during cell division. In Amoeba this splitting can occur in any plane, while in Leishmania (which causes kala-azar) it happens in a definite orientation relative to its whip-like structure.","visual":"🦠"},{"title":"Multiple Fission","body":"Some single-celled organisms, like the malarial parasite Plasmodium, divide into many daughter cells simultaneously instead of splitting into just two. This is called multiple fission.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Budding in Yeast","body":"Yeast reproduces by putting out small buds that grow and eventually separate from the parent cell to become new, independent yeast cells.","visual":"🍞"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is binary fission?","options":["Splitting of a cell into two equal halves","Splitting of a cell into many daughter cells at once","Growth of a small bud that later detaches","Breaking of a body into fragments that regrow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Binary fission is division into two equal halves, seen in many bacteria and protozoa."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which organism divides into many daughter cells simultaneously through multiple fission?","options":["Plasmodium","Amoeba","Yeast","Spirogyra"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plasmodium, the malarial parasite, undergoes multiple fission."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does binary fission in Leishmania differ from that in Amoeba?","options":["It occurs in a definite orientation related to its whip-like structure","It never actually divides the cell","It always produces exactly four daughter cells","It happens only outside a living body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Amoeba can split in any plane, but Leishmania's whip-like structure fixes the orientation of its division."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does yeast reproduce, as seen when grown in a sugar solution?","options":["By putting out small buds that separate and grow further","By splitting into two identical halves","By releasing spores from a sporangium","By fragmenting into many pieces"],"answer":0,"explain":"Yeast buds outward; the bud eventually separates to become a new yeast cell."}]},{"id":"rp3","title":"Fragmentation, Regeneration & Budding","icon":"🪱","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the aquarium tanks, a Planaria you accidentally cut in half hasn't died — it's growing into two worms. Time to understand how fragments, cut pieces, and buds can each grow into whole new organisms.","teach":[{"title":"Fragmentation","body":"In simple multi-cellular organisms like Spirogyra, the body simply breaks up into smaller pieces upon maturation, and each fragment grows into a new individual.","visual":"🧵"},{"title":"Regeneration","body":"Many fully differentiated organisms, such as Hydra and Planaria, can be cut into pieces, and many of these pieces grow into complete new organisms through an organised sequence of changes called development. Regeneration itself is not the same as normal reproduction, since most organisms do not depend on being cut up to reproduce.","visual":"🪱"},{"title":"Budding","body":"Hydra uses its regenerative cells for reproduction through budding: a bud grows outward at one specific site from repeated cell division, and when mature, detaches to become a new, independent individual.","visual":"🌸"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't most multi-cellular organisms simply divide cell-by-cell the way unicellular organisms do?","options":["Their cells are organised into tissues and organs at fixed positions","They have too little DNA to divide","They lack a nucleus entirely","They cannot copy DNA at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cells are organised into tissues and organs placed at definite positions, so simple cell-by-cell division would be impractical."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to Spirogyra as it matures?","options":["It breaks up into smaller pieces that each grow into a new individual","It buds off tiny organisms from one site","It splits into two equal halves like bacteria","It forms spores inside a sporangium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Spirogyra simply fragments into pieces upon maturation, and each fragment develops into a new filament."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which animals are given as examples that can regenerate after being cut into pieces?","options":["Hydra and Planaria","Amoeba and Plasmodium","Yeast and Rhizopus","Bacteria and protozoa in general"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydra and Planaria can be cut into pieces, with many pieces growing into complete organisms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is regeneration described as different from normal reproduction?","options":["Most organisms do not normally depend on being cut up in order to reproduce","Regeneration never actually produces a complete organism","Regeneration only happens in plants, never animals","Regeneration always kills the organism afterward"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter notes regeneration isn't the usual way these organisms reproduce, even though it can occur."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does a Hydra reproduce through budding?","options":["A bud grows as an outgrowth from repeated cell division and detaches when mature","It splits into two equal-sized daughter hydras","It releases spores into the surrounding water","It fragments into many small pieces like Spirogyra"],"answer":0,"explain":"Repeated cell division at one specific site forms a bud, which later detaches as a new individual."}]},{"id":"rp4","title":"Vegetative Propagation & Spore Formation","icon":"🌿","kind":"lesson","story":"The school garden club wants to grow new rose bushes and bananas without waiting for seeds. Before you help them, you need to know which plant parts can grow into whole new plants — and how bread mould spreads without seeds at all.","teach":[{"title":"Vegetative Propagation","body":"In many plants, parts like the root, stem, and leaves can develop into new plants under the right conditions. Methods like layering or grafting use this to grow crops such as sugarcane, roses, and grapes, and can propagate plants like banana, orange, and jasmine that have lost the ability to produce seeds.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"Advantages & Bryophyllum","body":"Plants grown by vegetative propagation can flower and fruit earlier than seed-grown ones, and all the new plants are genetically similar enough to the parent to share its characteristics. In Bryophyllum, buds forming in the notches along the leaf margin fall onto soil and develop into new plants.","visual":"🍃"},{"title":"Tissue Culture","body":"In tissue culture, cells taken from a plant's growing tip are grown in an artificial medium to form a callus, which is then given hormones to grow and differentiate into plantlets. This lets many disease-free plants be grown from one parent, and is commonly used for ornamental plants.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Spore Formation","body":"In the bread mould Rhizopus, tiny blob-like structures called sporangia form and contain spores covered by thick protective walls. When a spore contacts a moist surface, it can begin to grow into a new Rhizopus individual.","visual":"🍄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which parts of a plant can develop into new plants through vegetative propagation?","options":["Root, stem, and leaves","Only the flower","Only the seed","Only the fruit"],"answer":0,"explain":"Vegetative propagation uses parts like the root, stem, and leaves to grow new plants."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is vegetative propagation especially useful for plants like banana and jasmine?","options":["They have lost the capacity to produce seeds, so this method can still propagate them","They cannot survive without flowers at all","They only grow through tissue culture","They reproduce solely through spores"],"answer":0,"explain":"Banana, orange, rose, and jasmine have lost seed-producing capacity, so vegetative propagation keeps them going."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In tissue culture, what is the mass of rapidly dividing cells called before it differentiates into plantlets?","options":["Callus","Zygote","Sporangium","Embryo"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cells from the growing tip divide to form a callus, which is later given hormones to differentiate into plantlets."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What structures in Rhizopus contain the spores used for reproduction?","options":["Sporangia","Ovaries","Buds","Fragments"],"answer":0,"explain":"The blob-like sporangia on the hyphae of Rhizopus contain spores that grow into new individuals in moist conditions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What protects Rhizopus spores until they reach a moist surface?","options":["A thick outer wall","A layer of soil around them","The parent hyphae surrounding them","A coating of pollen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Spores are covered by thick walls that protect them until conditions are right for growth."}]},{"id":"rp5","title":"Why Sexual Reproduction?","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"Two rival research teams challenge you: can a species adapt to a changing environment faster by combining DNA from two individuals rather than just copying one? Dive into meiosis and gametes to settle the bet.","teach":[{"title":"Combining Variations","body":"DNA copying errors create variation slowly, one generation at a time. Sexual reproduction speeds this up by combining the different variations already accumulated in two individuals, creating novel combinations in their offspring.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Meiosis Solves the DNA Problem","body":"If offspring simply combined the full DNA of two parents, the amount of DNA would double every generation. Meiosis solves this: it produces germ-cells with half the number of chromosomes, so when two germ-cells combine, the normal chromosome number and DNA content are restored.","visual":"➗"},{"title":"Male and Female Gametes","body":"In very simple organisms the two germ-cells may look similar, but as body designs become more complex, the germ-cells specialise: one is large and stores food — the female gamete — and the other is smaller and motile — the male gamete.","visual":"⚥"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What advantage does sexual reproduction offer over asexual reproduction, according to the chapter?","options":["It combines variations from two individuals, generating novel combinations faster","It requires no DNA copying at all","It always produces offspring identical to one parent","It uses far less cellular energy than asexual reproduction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Combining variations from two individuals speeds up the generation of new, potentially useful variation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What problem would arise if offspring simply combined the full DNA of both parents every generation?","options":["The amount of DNA would double every generation","Offspring would end up with no DNA at all","Cells would stop dividing completely","Offspring would resemble only one parent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without a halving mechanism, DNA content would keep doubling generation after generation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which process produces germ-cells with half the normal number of chromosomes?","options":["Meiosis","Binary fission","Budding","Fragmentation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Meiosis is the cell division that halves the chromosome number in germ-cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which germ-cell is conventionally described as smaller and motile?","options":["The male gamete","The female gamete","The zygote","The spore"],"answer":0,"explain":"The motile, smaller germ-cell is called the male gamete, while the larger, food-storing one is the female gamete."}]},{"id":"rp6","title":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","icon":"🌺","kind":"lesson","story":"You've been handed a hibiscus flower and a magnifying glass for the botany fair. To win the ribbon, trace the journey of a pollen grain from stamen to seed.","teach":[{"title":"Flower Parts","body":"A flower may be unisexual, containing only stamens or only a pistil (as in papaya or watermelon), or bisexual, containing both (as in Hibiscus or mustard). The stamen is the male reproductive part and produces pollen grains; the pistil is the female reproductive part, made of the ovary, style, and stigma.","visual":"🌺"},{"title":"Pollination","body":"Transfer of pollen from the stamen to the stigma of the same flower is self-pollination, while transfer of pollen from one flower to another, carried by agents like wind, water, or animals, is cross-pollination.","visual":"🐝"},{"title":"Fertilisation and Seed Formation","body":"After pollen lands on the stigma, a tube grows from the pollen grain through the style to reach the ovary, where the male germ-cell fuses with the egg cell in the ovule — this fertilisation forms the zygote. The zygote develops into an embryo, the ovule becomes a seed, and the ovary ripens into a fruit.","visual":"🌱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the male reproductive part of a flower, and what does it produce?","options":["The stamen, which produces pollen grains","The pistil, which produces pollen grains","The sepal, which produces ovules","The petal, which produces the zygote"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stamen is the male part of the flower and produces the yellowish pollen grains."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the three parts of the pistil, from bottom to top?","options":["Ovary, style, stigma","Sepal, petal, stamen","Anther, filament, pollen","Zygote, embryo, seed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The pistil consists of the swollen ovary at the bottom, the elongated style in the middle, and the stigma at the tip."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does pollination differ from fertilisation?","options":["Pollination transfers pollen to the stigma; fertilisation is the fusion of male and female germ-cells","Pollination and fertilisation are exactly the same process","Fertilisation always happens before pollination","Pollination only occurs in bisexual flowers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pollination moves pollen to the stigma; fertilisation is the actual fusion of germ-cells that forms the zygote."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Transfer of pollen from one flower to a different flower is called:","options":["Cross-pollination","Self-pollination","Germination","Fragmentation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pollen moving between different flowers is cross-pollination, carried by agents like wind, water, or animals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the ovary develop into after fertilisation?","options":["A fruit","A petal","A stigma","A stamen"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ovary grows rapidly and ripens to form a fruit, while the ovule becomes a seed."}]},{"id":"rp7","title":"Puberty & the Male Reproductive System","icon":"👦","kind":"lesson","story":"The school nurse's poster on 'growing up' has half its labels missing. Before health class starts, map out puberty's changes and trace how the male reproductive system delivers sperm.","teach":[{"title":"Puberty","body":"As general body growth begins to slow down during adolescence, reproductive tissues begin to mature — this period is called puberty. Changes common to both boys and girls include new hair growth in the armpits and genital area, oily skin, and pimples.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Changes Specific to Boys and Girls","body":"In girls, breast size begins to increase and menstruation begins around this time. In boys, new facial hair grows, the voice begins to crack, and the penis occasionally becomes enlarged and erect.","visual":"🧑"},{"title":"Male Reproductive System","body":"Sperm are formed in the testes, located in the scrotum outside the abdominal cavity because sperm formation needs a lower temperature than the rest of the body. Testosterone, secreted by the testes, regulates sperm formation and also causes the body changes seen in boys at puberty. Sperm travel through the vas deferens, joined along the way by fluid from the prostate and seminal vesicles, and leave through the urethra, which also carries urine.","visual":"👦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are the testes located outside the abdominal cavity, in the scrotum?","options":["Sperm formation requires a lower temperature than normal body temperature","To keep sperm away from testosterone","So they can also produce eggs","To make urination easier"],"answer":0,"explain":"The scrotum keeps the testes cooler than the body's core temperature, which sperm formation requires."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Besides regulating sperm formation, what else does testosterone do?","options":["Brings about the physical changes of puberty seen in boys","Controls menstruation in girls","Produces eggs in the ovary","Forms the placenta during pregnancy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Testosterone regulates sperm production and also causes puberty changes such as facial hair growth."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the function of the secretions added by the prostate gland and seminal vesicles?","options":["They make sperm transport easier and provide nutrition","They destroy old, unused sperm","They produce testosterone for the testes","They form the outer wall of the testes"],"answer":0,"explain":"These gland secretions put sperm into a fluid that eases their transport and provides nourishment."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the urethra serve a dual role in the male body?","options":["It forms a common passage for both sperm and urine","It produces both sperm and eggs","It stores sperm permanently","It connects directly to the ovaries"],"answer":0,"explain":"The urethra carries both urine from the bladder and sperm from the vas deferens."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these puberty changes is common to BOTH boys and girls?","options":["New hair growth in the armpits and genital area","Menstruation","Voice cracking","Increase in breast size"],"answer":0,"explain":"New hair growth and skin changes occur in both sexes, while menstruation and voice cracking are sex-specific."}]},{"id":"rp8","title":"Female Reproductive System & Menstruation","icon":"🌷","kind":"lesson","story":"In the embryology wing of the museum, a model uterus lights up month by month. Trace the path of an egg from ovary to embryo, and learn why the uterine lining sheds when no fertilisation occurs.","teach":[{"title":"Female Reproductive System","body":"Eggs are made in the ovaries, which already contain thousands of immature eggs at birth; after puberty, one egg matures and is released roughly every month. The egg travels through the oviduct (fallopian tube) toward the uterus, which opens into the vagina through the cervix.","visual":"🌷"},{"title":"Fertilisation and Pregnancy","body":"Sperm enter through the vagina during intercourse and travel up to the oviduct, where fertilisation may occur. The resulting zygote divides to form an embryo, which implants in the uterine lining. The placenta, embedded in the uterine wall, lets the embryo exchange glucose, oxygen, and waste with the mother's blood over about nine months of development.","visual":"🤰"},{"title":"Menstruation","body":"Each month the uterus lining thickens to prepare for a possible pregnancy. If the egg is not fertilised, it survives for about one day, and the unneeded, thickened lining breaks down and is shed through the vagina as blood and mucous — this is menstruation, usually lasting about two to eight days.","visual":"🩸"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where does fertilisation normally occur in the human female reproductive tract?","options":["In the oviduct (fallopian tube)","In the ovary","In the vagina","In the cervix"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sperm travel up to the oviduct, where they may encounter and fertilise the egg."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the role of the placenta during pregnancy?","options":["It lets glucose, oxygen, and waste pass between mother and embryo","It produces sperm for the developing embryo","It replaces the uterus after implantation","It prevents the embryo from growing further"],"answer":0,"explain":"The placenta provides a large surface area for exchange of nutrients, oxygen, and waste between mother and embryo."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For roughly how many months does the developing child stay inside the mother's body?","answer":9,"explain":"The chapter states development inside the mother's body takes approximately nine months."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does menstruation occur when an egg is not fertilised?","options":["The unneeded, thickened uterine lining breaks down and is shed","The ovary stops producing eggs permanently","The placenta detaches too early","The vagina seals itself off"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without fertilisation, the lining built up to nourish an embryo is no longer needed and sheds as blood and mucous."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"About how long does an unfertilised egg survive before menstruation follows?","options":["About one day","About one week","About one month","About one hour"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states the unfertilised egg lives for about one day."}]},{"id":"rp9","title":"Reproductive Health","icon":"⚖️","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Reproductive Health Gatekeeper","taunt":"Confuse pollination with fertilisation, or a barrier with a hormone, and you'll never pass through my gate!"},"story":"One final test across everything in this world — fission, regeneration, flowering plants, human reproduction, and reproductive health. The Gatekeeper blocks the exit until every question is answered correctly.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which contraceptive method works by changing the body's hormonal balance so eggs are not released?","options":["Oral contraceptive pills","Condoms","Copper-T","Surgical blocking of the vas deferens"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pills work by altering hormonal balance so eggs aren't released and fertilisation can't occur."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the vas deferens is surgically blocked in a male, what is prevented?","options":["Sperm transfer","Egg release","Menstruation","Pollen transfer"],"answer":0,"explain":"Blocking the vas deferens prevents sperm from being transferred, so fertilisation cannot occur."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Does using a copper-T protect a woman from sexually transmitted diseases?","options":["No, it only prevents pregnancy, not disease transmission","Yes, it fully protects against all STDs","Yes, but only against viral infections","No, it directly increases STD risk"],"answer":0,"explain":"The copper-T is a contraceptive device; it does not act as a barrier against disease-causing organisms the way a condom does."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which organism's binary fission occurs in a fixed orientation relative to a whip-like structure?","options":["Leishmania","Yeast","Rhizopus","Hydra"],"answer":0,"explain":"Leishmania's binary fission occurs in a definite orientation related to its whip-like structure."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is formed when the male germ-cell from pollen fuses with the egg cell in the ovule?","options":["The zygote","The sporangium","The callus","The placenta"],"answer":0,"explain":"This fusion, called fertilisation, produces the zygote, which grows into a new plant."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the chapter, what may be a more important cause of poor living standards than population size alone?","options":["Inequality in society","The rate of DNA copying errors","The number of spores produced by fungi","The length of the menstrual cycle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter suggests that if inequality is the main reason for poor living standards, population size is comparatively less important."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a viral sexually transmitted infection mentioned in the chapter?","options":["HIV-AIDS","Gonorrhoea","Syphilis","Malaria"],"answer":0,"explain":"HIV-AIDS and warts are viral infections mentioned; gonorrhoea and syphilis are bacterial."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At the upper end of the range given in the chapter, for about how many days can menstruation last?","answer":8,"explain":"The chapter states menstruation usually lasts about two to eight days."}]}]},{"id":"heredity10","index":7,"name":"Heredity","subtitle":"Science Chapter 8","emoji":"🧬","palette":{"from":"#2dd4bf","to":"#134e4a"},"lessons":[{"id":"hr1","title":"The Variation Vault","icon":"🧫","kind":"lesson","story":"You slip into an old fermentation vault where a single bacterium has been dividing for generations. Comparing its clone-army to the wild diversity outside the vault door sets your first heredity puzzle: why is the vault so boring?","teach":[{"title":"Reproduction Always Creates Variation","body":"Every round of reproduction produces new individuals that are similar to their parents but subtly different. Even asexual reproduction, like a bacterium dividing, creates some variation.","visual":"🧫"},{"title":"Asexual vs Sexual Variation","body":"When a bacterium divides repeatedly, the resulting individuals are very similar, differing only slightly due to small inaccuracies in DNA copying. Sexual reproduction generates much greater diversity than this.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"Variation Helps Survival","body":"Different variants of a species have different chances of surviving in a given environment. For instance, bacteria that can withstand heat survive better during a heat wave, showing how variation aids survival.","visual":"🔥"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which process generates greater diversity among offspring: sexual or asexual reproduction?","options":["Sexual reproduction","Asexual reproduction","Both generate identical diversity","Neither generates diversity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sexual reproduction generates far greater diversity than asexual reproduction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A field of sugarcane (grown asexually) shows very little variation among plants, while sexually reproducing animals show distinct variation between individuals. What explains this difference?","options":["Sexual reproduction creates more diversity than asexual reproduction","Sugarcane plants never mutate","Animals copy DNA less accurately than plants","Asexual reproduction always produces more variation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because asexual reproduction relies on copying, offspring are far more similar, while sexual reproduction maximises variation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If bacteria divide repeatedly and the resulting individuals show only minor differences, what is the source of these small differences?","options":["Small inaccuracies during DNA copying","Random mixing of two parents' genes","Environmental temperature alone","Chromosome pairing errors during fertilisation"],"answer":0,"explain":"In asexual reproduction, minor variations arise from small inaccuracies in DNA copying."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If trait A exists in 10% of a population of an asexually reproducing species and trait B exists in 60% of the same population, which trait most likely arose earlier?","options":["Trait B, since it has had more time to spread through the population","Trait A, since rare traits are always older","Both arose at exactly the same time","Neither trait can be older since the species reproduces asexually"],"answer":0,"explain":"A trait present in a larger fraction of the population has likely had more generations to spread, suggesting it arose earlier."}]},{"id":"hr2","title":"Whispers of the Earlobe","icon":"👂","kind":"lesson","story":"The Study Atlas drops you into a family reunion hall full of look-alike strangers. To unlock the next wing, you must explain why children resemble their parents without being identical copies of them.","teach":[{"title":"What Heredity Means","body":"Heredity is the process by which traits and characteristics are reliably passed from parents to offspring. A child bears all the basic features of a human being, but does not look exactly like its parents.","visual":"👶"},{"title":"Equal Contribution From Both Parents","body":"Both the father and the mother contribute practically equal amounts of genetic material to their child. This means each trait can be influenced by both paternal and maternal DNA, so every child has two versions of each trait.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Earlobes Show Inherited Variation","body":"The earlobe, the lowest part of the ear, is closely attached to the head in some people and free-hanging in others. Free and attached earlobes are two variants found in human populations, inherited from parents.","visual":"👂"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"About how much genetic material does each parent contribute to a child?","options":["Practically equal amounts","The father contributes almost all of it","The mother contributes almost all of it","It varies randomly with no pattern"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both parents contribute practically equal amounts of genetic material to the child."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Because both parents contribute genetic material, how many versions of each trait does a child end up with?","options":["Two, one from each parent","Only one, from whichever parent is dominant","Four, two from each parent","None until the trait is expressed"],"answer":0,"explain":"For each trait, a child has two versions, one inherited from each parent."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Free and attached earlobes in humans are an example of:","options":["An inherited trait variant found in human populations","A trait that changes with age regardless of parents","A trait caused only by environment, not genes","A trait unique to only one family in the world"],"answer":0,"explain":"Earlobe shape is a variant trait inherited from parents, illustrating heredity in action."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why doesn't a child look exactly like either parent?","options":["Because the child inherits a mix of traits from both parents, not an exact copy of either","Because heredity does not really apply to physical appearance","Because only mothers pass on visible traits","Because children copy only the recessive parent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since traits come from both parents, the child shows a combination of inherited features rather than being identical to one parent."}]},{"id":"hr3","title":"Mendel's Pea Garden","icon":"🌱","kind":"lesson","story":"A shimmering portal drops you into a monastery garden in the 1800s, rows of pea plants swaying in neat lines. A monk named Mendel hands you his notebook and asks you to help him count generations.","teach":[{"title":"Crossing Tall and Short Plants","body":"Mendel crossed tall pea plants with short pea plants. All the F1 (first generation) progeny were tall, with no 'medium-height' plants, showing that only one parental trait was expressed, not a blend of both.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"The Hidden Trait Reappears in F2","body":"When the tall F1 plants were self-pollinated, their F2 progeny were not all tall: one quarter of them were short. This showed that both tallness and shortness were inherited by the F1 plants, but only tallness was expressed.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Dominant and Recessive Traits","body":"Mendel proposed that two copies of a factor (now called a gene) control each trait. Both TT and Tt plants are tall, while only tt plants are short: a single copy of the dominant 'T' is enough to make a plant tall (a dominant trait), while 't' is recessive and needs two copies to show.","visual":"🔤"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When Mendel crossed tall pea plants with short pea plants, the F1 progeny were:","options":["All tall","All short","Half tall and half short","All of medium height"],"answer":0,"explain":"There were no medium-height plants; all F1 progeny were tall, showing only one parental trait was expressed."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Out of every 4 F2 plants produced by self-pollinating the tall F1 plants, how many are expected to be short?","answer":1,"explain":"One quarter of the F2 progeny were short, giving a 3 tall : 1 short ratio."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A single copy of the dominant gene 'T' is enough to make a pea plant:","options":["Tall","Short","Sterile","Medium height"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both TT and Tt plants are tall because one dominant copy is sufficient to express the trait."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the genotype ratio of TT : Tt : tt among the F2 plants?","options":["1 : 2 : 1","3 : 1 : 0","1 : 1 : 1","9 : 3 : 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Self-pollinating Tt (F1) plants produces F2 offspring in a 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt genotype ratio."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Traits like tallness, which show up in the F1 generation while masking the other trait, are called:","options":["Dominant traits","Recessive traits","Hybrid traits","Mutant traits"],"answer":0,"explain":"A trait that is expressed even when only one copy of its gene is present is called dominant."}]},{"id":"hr4","title":"The Double Trait Dungeon","icon":"🎲","kind":"lesson","story":"Deeper in the garden portal, you find a dungeon of tangled vines where two traits at once, seed shape and seed colour, must be tracked through two generations to escape.","teach":[{"title":"Crossing Two Traits at Once","body":"When a tall plant with round seeds was crossed with a short plant with wrinkled seeds, the F1 progeny were all tall and had round seeds, showing tallness and round seeds are both dominant traits.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"New Combinations Appear in F2","body":"When F1 progeny were self-pollinated, F2 plants included tall-round and short-wrinkled plants, but also new combinations not seen in either parent: tall-wrinkled and short-round plants. This showed the two traits are inherited independently of each other.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"The 9:3:3:1 Ratio","body":"In Mendel's actual seed cross of 556 F2 seeds, 315 were round & yellow, 108 round & green, 101 wrinkled & yellow, and 32 wrinkled & green, approximating the classic 9:3:3:1 ratio for two independently inherited traits.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Crossing a tall, round-seeded pea plant with a short, wrinkled-seeded pea plant produces F1 progeny that are:","options":["All tall with round seeds","All short with wrinkled seeds","Half tall-round and half short-wrinkled","All medium height with round seeds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tallness and round seeds are both dominant traits, so all F1 progeny show them."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The appearance of new combinations, such as tall plants with wrinkled seeds, among the F2 progeny shows that:","options":["The two traits (height and seed shape) are inherited independently of each other","The two traits are always linked together","Mendel made an error in his experiment","Only one trait can be inherited at a time"],"answer":0,"explain":"New trait combinations in F2 progeny demonstrate that seed shape and plant height assort independently."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Mendel's dihybrid seed cross, the F2 seeds approximated which ratio of round-yellow : round-green : wrinkled-yellow : wrinkled-green?","options":["9 : 3 : 3 : 1","3 : 1 : 1 : 3","1 : 2 : 2 : 1","1 : 1 : 1 : 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mendel's data of 315:108:101:32 approximates the classic 9:3:3:1 dihybrid ratio."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the 9:3:3:1 dihybrid ratio, what number represents the rarest combination, wrinkled seeds that are also green?","answer":1,"explain":"The wrinkled, green combination is the rarest, corresponding to the '1' portion of the 9:3:3:1 ratio."}]},{"id":"hr5","title":"Gene Machines","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"A shrinking beam zaps you down to the size of a cell. Inside, you must trace how a strand of DNA quietly decides whether a pea plant grows tall or stays short.","teach":[{"title":"A Gene Makes a Protein","body":"Cellular DNA is the information source for making proteins. A section of DNA that provides the information for one protein is called the gene for that protein.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Genes Control Traits Through Proteins","body":"An efficient enzyme can make plenty of a plant growth hormone, producing a tall plant. If a gene alteration makes that enzyme less efficient, less hormone is made and the plant is short, showing how genes control traits.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Two Gene Sets, Split Into Chromosomes","body":"Each pea plant has two sets of all genes, one inherited from each parent, organised as separate independent pieces called chromosomes. Each germ cell (sperm or egg) takes just one chromosome from each pair, so combining two germ cells restores the normal chromosome number.","visual":"🧫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A gene is best described as:","options":["A section of DNA that provides information for making one protein","An entire chromosome","A visible trait like tallness itself","A type of plant hormone"],"answer":0,"explain":"A gene is the section of DNA carrying the information needed to make one particular protein."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the tallness example, what happens if a gene alteration makes the relevant enzyme less efficient?","options":["Less growth hormone is made, and the plant is short","More growth hormone is made, and the plant grows taller","The plant produces no seeds at all","The change has no effect on plant height"],"answer":0,"explain":"A less efficient enzyme produces less hormone, resulting in a shorter plant, showing how genes influence traits."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Each germ cell (sperm or egg) contains:","options":["One chromosome from each pair, i.e. a single gene set","Two chromosomes from each pair","No chromosomes at all","Only chromosomes inherited from the mother"],"answer":0,"explain":"Germ cells carry only one chromosome from each pair, ensuring each parent contributes one gene set."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When two germ cells combine during fertilisation, the result is that:","options":["The normal chromosome number is restored in the progeny","The chromosome number doubles every generation","All chromosomes become identical","Genes are destroyed and replaced"],"answer":0,"explain":"Combining two germ cells restores the normal two-copies-per-chromosome state, keeping the species' DNA stable."}]},{"id":"hr6","title":"Boy or Girl? The Chromosome Choice","icon":"🚻","kind":"lesson","story":"In the final chamber before the boss, a mysterious cradle asks you to predict its next occupant's sex using nothing but chromosomes, no coin flips allowed.","teach":[{"title":"Different Species, Different Strategies","body":"Sex determination strategies vary across species. In some reptiles, the temperature at which fertilised eggs are kept determines whether the offspring is male or female, while in snails individuals can change sex, so it is not genetically fixed there.","visual":"🥚"},{"title":"Human Sex Chromosomes","body":"In humans, sex is largely genetically determined. Women have a matched pair of sex chromosomes, both called X, while men have a mismatched pair: one normal-sized X and one short one called Y. So women are XX and men are XY.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Father's Chromosome Decides","body":"Every child inherits an X chromosome from the mother, regardless of sex. Whether the child is a boy or a girl depends on what is inherited from the father: an X chromosome gives a girl, and a Y chromosome gives a boy.","visual":"👨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In some reptiles, what determines whether developing offspring will be male or female?","options":["The temperature at which the fertilised eggs are kept","The size of the egg","The number of eggs laid together","The colour of the eggshell"],"answer":0,"explain":"In certain reptiles, egg incubation temperature, not genes, determines the offspring's sex."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Human women have which sex chromosomes?","options":["XX","XY","YY","A single X only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Women have a matched pair of X chromosomes (XX)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Whether a human child is born a boy or a girl depends on which chromosome is inherited from:","options":["The father, either X or Y","The mother, either X or Y","Both parents equally contributing a Z chromosome","Neither parent, it is random each time"],"answer":0,"explain":"All children get an X from the mother; the father's contribution (X or Y) determines the child's sex."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which chromosome does every human child inherit from the mother, regardless of whether they are a boy or a girl?","options":["X","Y","Both X and Y","Neither X nor Y"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mothers only carry X chromosomes, so every child receives an X chromosome from the mother."}]},{"id":"hr_boss","title":"The Heredity Overlord","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"The Overlord towers before you, cloaked in stitched pea pods and chromosome strands. Every wrong answer lets it self-pollinate another wave of minions; answer sharply to end its reign.","boss":{"name":"The Heredity Overlord","taunt":"Two copies, one dominant... think you can out-inherit ME?"},"teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A trait that is expressed even when only one copy of its gene is present is called:","options":["Dominant","Recessive","Hybrid","Mutant"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dominant traits show up with just a single copy of the gene, masking the recessive trait."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a monohybrid cross between two F1 hybrids (Tt x Tt), the F2 generation shows tall and short plants in a ratio of X : 1. What is X?","answer":3,"explain":"F2 progeny of a monohybrid cross appear in a 3 (dominant) : 1 (recessive) phenotypic ratio."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The F2 generation of a dihybrid cross (two independently inherited traits) shows phenotypes in the ratio:","options":["9 : 3 : 3 : 1","3 : 1","1 : 2 : 1","1 : 1 : 1 : 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two independently inherited traits produce a 9:3:3:1 phenotypic ratio in F2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In humans, a boy is born when the child inherits which chromosome from the father?","options":["Y","X","Both X and Y","Neither"],"answer":0,"explain":"Inheriting a Y chromosome from the father results in a boy; inheriting an X results in a girl."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A section of DNA that carries the information for making one protein is called a:","options":["Gene","Chromosome","Enzyme","Hormone"],"answer":0,"explain":"A gene is the DNA segment coding for a single protein, and proteins ultimately control traits."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A man with blood group A marries a woman with blood group O, and their daughter has blood group O. Based on this single family alone, can we conclude which trait, blood group A or O, is dominant?","options":["No, one such case is not enough evidence to determine dominance","Yes, this proves blood group O is dominant","Yes, this proves blood group A is dominant","Yes, this proves both are co-dominant"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single family's outcome isn't sufficient evidence; determining dominance requires studying many crosses and generations."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A Mendelian cross breeds tall violet-flowered pea plants with short white-flowered plants. All progeny are violet, but almost half are short. What is the genetic make-up of the tall parent?","options":["TtWW","TTWW","TTww","TtWw"],"answer":0,"explain":"Half the progeny being short (Tt x tt gives 1:1) means the tall parent is Tt, while all progeny being violet means the tall parent is homozygous WW."}]}]},{"id":"lightreflection10","index":8,"name":"Light: Reflection and Refraction","subtitle":"Science Chapter 9","emoji":"🔍","palette":{"from":"#fbbf24","to":"#78350f"},"lessons":[{"id":"lr1","title":"Mirror, Mirror","icon":"🪞","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Hall of a Thousand Mirrors in a forgotten temple. The only way through is to prove you understand how every reflecting surface bends light back at you.","teach":[{"title":"Laws of Reflection","body":"The angle of incidence always equals the angle of reflection. The incident ray, the normal at the point of incidence, and the reflected ray all lie in the same plane. These laws hold for every reflecting surface, flat or curved.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Plane Mirror Images","body":"A plane mirror always forms a virtual, erect image the same size as the object. The image sits as far behind the mirror as the object is in front of it, and it is laterally inverted.","visual":"🪞"},{"title":"Naming the Spherical Mirror","body":"The pole (P) is the centre of the mirror's reflecting surface. The centre of curvature (C) is the centre of the sphere the mirror is cut from, and it lies outside the mirror. The radius of curvature (R) is the distance PC, and the principal axis is the line through P and C.","visual":"⚪"},{"title":"Concave vs Convex","body":"A mirror whose reflecting surface curves inward, facing the centre of the sphere, is a concave mirror. One that curves outward is a convex mirror — like the inside and outside of a shining spoon.","visual":"🥄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ray of light strikes a plane mirror at 30° to the normal. At what angle does it reflect?","options":["30° to the normal","15° to the normal","60° to the normal","0°, straight back along its path"],"answer":0,"explain":"The angle of reflection always equals the angle of incidence."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which best describes the image formed by a plane mirror?","options":["Virtual, erect, and the same size as the object","Real, inverted, and smaller than the object","Virtual, inverted, and magnified","Real, erect, and magnified"],"answer":0,"explain":"A plane mirror always gives a virtual, erect, laterally-inverted image equal in size to the object."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The radius of curvature of a spherical mirror is 20 cm. What is its focal length, in cm?","answer":10,"explain":"Focal length f = R/2 = 20/2 = 10 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A shining spoon's scooped (inward-curving) surface behaves like which type of mirror?","options":["Concave mirror","Convex mirror","Plane mirror","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"A surface curving inward, toward the centre of the sphere, is concave."}]},{"id":"lr2","title":"Focus of the Storm","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"Lost without a firestarter, you aim a curved shard of mirror at the sun. Somewhere along the principal axis is a single point hot enough to save you — if you can find it.","teach":[{"title":"Principal Focus","body":"Rays parallel to the principal axis, after reflecting off a concave mirror, all meet at one point on the axis — the principal focus. For a convex mirror, the reflected rays only appear to diverge from a point behind the mirror, which is its principal focus.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Focal Length and R = 2f","body":"The distance from the pole to the principal focus is the focal length (f). For a spherical mirror of small aperture, the radius of curvature is twice the focal length: R = 2f, so the focus lies midway between the pole and the centre of curvature.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Finding f Experimentally","body":"A concave mirror pointed at the sun focuses sunlight to a tiny, bright, real image of the sun — hot enough to burn paper. The distance from the mirror to this bright spot is the mirror's approximate focal length.","visual":"☀️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the principal focus of a concave mirror?","options":["The point on the principal axis where rays parallel to the axis meet after reflection","The point exactly midway between the object and the mirror","The centre of the sphere the mirror is cut from","The point where the object itself is placed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Parallel rays converge at the principal focus after reflecting off a concave mirror."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the focal length, in cm, of a convex mirror whose radius of curvature is 32 cm.","answer":16,"explain":"f = R/2 = 32/2 = 16 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a convex mirror, rays parallel to the principal axis, after reflection, ___","options":["appear to diverge from the principal focus behind the mirror","converge at the principal focus in front of the mirror","travel on forever, staying parallel","scatter randomly in all directions"],"answer":0,"explain":"A convex mirror's reflected rays only appear to spread out from a focus point behind it."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A spherical mirror has a focal length of 7.5 cm. What is its radius of curvature, in cm?","answer":15,"explain":"R = 2f = 2 × 7.5 = 15 cm."}]},{"id":"lr3","title":"Concave Mirror Chronicles","icon":"🕯","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in a candle-lit cave, you slide a lantern back and forth in front of a concave mirror, watching its image flicker, flip, and grow. Every position tells a different story about where the treasure's reflection truly lies.","teach":[{"title":"Drawing the Rays","body":"To locate an image, pick any two of these rays: one parallel to the axis (reflects through F), one through F (reflects parallel to the axis), one through C (reflects straight back), or one hitting the pole (reflects at an equal angle to the axis). Where two reflected rays cross is the image.","visual":"✏️"},{"title":"Concave Mirror Image Table","body":"Object at infinity → image at F, point-sized, real, inverted. Beyond C → between F and C, diminished, real, inverted. At C → at C, same size, real, inverted. Between C and F → beyond C, enlarged, real, inverted. At F → at infinity, no image forms. Between P and F → behind the mirror, enlarged, virtual, erect.","visual":"🕯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An object placed between the pole and the focus of a concave mirror produces an image that is:","options":["Virtual, erect, and enlarged, behind the mirror","Real, inverted, and diminished, in front of the mirror","Real, inverted, and the same size, at C","No image forms at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Between P and F, a concave mirror gives an enlarged virtual, erect image behind the mirror — like a shaving mirror."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When the object is placed exactly at the centre of curvature C of a concave mirror, the image forms:","options":["At C, real, inverted, and the same size as the object","At F, real and diminished","Behind the mirror, virtual and enlarged","At infinity"],"answer":0,"explain":"At C, the image also forms at C — real, inverted, same size."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An object placed exactly at the principal focus F of a concave mirror produces an image:","options":["At infinity — no sharp image can be caught on a screen","At the pole P","At C, same size as the object","Behind the mirror, virtual"],"answer":0,"explain":"At F, reflected rays become parallel and never converge, so the image forms at infinity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A concave mirror produces a three-times-magnified real image of an object placed 10 cm in front of it. At what distance from the mirror, in cm, does the image form?","answer":30,"explain":"A real image has negative magnification: m = -v/u = -3, u = -10 cm, so v = 3 × 10 = 30 cm in front of the mirror."}]},{"id":"lr4","title":"Bulging Out: Convex Mirror","icon":"🚗","kind":"lesson","story":"A chase down a winding mountain road! Your only warning of what's behind you comes from a small, bulging mirror bolted to your side door — trust it, and you'll see everything coming.","teach":[{"title":"Convex Mirror Images","body":"No matter where the object is, a convex mirror always forms a virtual, erect, diminished image behind the mirror — shrinking toward a point as the object moves toward infinity.","visual":"🚙"},{"title":"Why Convex for Rear-View","body":"Convex mirrors are used as rear/side-view mirrors because they always give an erect image and, being curved outward, cover a much wider field of view than a plane mirror of the same size.","visual":"🔎"},{"title":"Uses of Concave Mirrors","body":"Concave mirrors give powerful parallel beams when the source sits near the focus — used in torches, search-lights, and vehicle headlights. Their magnified image also makes them useful as shaving mirrors, for dentists examining teeth, and in solar furnaces to concentrate sunlight.","visual":"🪒"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are convex mirrors preferred as rear-view mirrors in vehicles?","options":["They give an erect image and a wider field of view than a plane mirror","They give a real, magnified image of what's behind","They reverse the direction of light entirely","They are simply cheaper, with no optical advantage"],"answer":0,"explain":"Convex mirrors' curvature gives a wider view and always shows an erect (right-way-up) image."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The image formed by a convex mirror, no matter where the object is placed, is always:","options":["Virtual, erect, and diminished","Real, inverted, and enlarged","Virtual, inverted, and the same size","Real, erect, and diminished"],"answer":0,"explain":"A convex mirror never forms a real image — it is always virtual, erect and smaller than the object."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Dentists use which type of mirror to see an enlarged image of a patient's teeth?","options":["Concave mirror","Convex mirror","Plane mirror","A flat glass slab"],"answer":0,"explain":"Placed close to the teeth, a concave mirror gives an enlarged, virtual, erect image."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Concave mirrors are used in headlights and search-lights because they can:","options":["Produce a powerful, parallel beam from a source placed near the focus","Diverge light widely for the broadest possible view","Only ever produce virtual images","Reduce the intensity of the light source"],"answer":0,"explain":"A source at the focus of a concave mirror sends out light as a strong parallel beam."}]},{"id":"lr5","title":"Sign Language of Mirrors","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"A gatekeeper spirit blocks the vault, speaking only in plus and minus signs. To pass, you must master the New Cartesian Sign Convention — the language every mirror formula is written in.","teach":[{"title":"New Cartesian Sign Convention","body":"The pole of the mirror is the origin, and the principal axis is the x-axis. The object is always placed to the left, so light falls on the mirror from the left. Distances measured to the right of the pole are positive, to the left are negative. Heights measured upward (above the axis) are positive, downward are negative.","visual":"➕➖"},{"title":"The Mirror Formula","body":"The mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f relates the image distance (v), object distance (u), and focal length (f), and holds for every spherical mirror in every situation, as long as you apply the sign convention correctly.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Magnification","body":"Magnification m = h'/h = -v/u compares image height to object height. A negative m means the image is real and inverted; a positive m means the image is virtual and erect.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the New Cartesian Sign Convention for mirrors, where is the object always placed?","options":["To the left of the mirror, so light falls on it from the left","To the right of the mirror","Directly above the pole","It can be placed anywhere, signs don't matter"],"answer":0,"explain":"The convention always places the object to the left, with light travelling left to right."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The mirror formula relating object distance (u), image distance (v), and focal length (f) is:","options":["1/v + 1/u = 1/f","1/v − 1/u = 1/f","v + u = f","v/u = f"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mirror formula is 1/v + 1/u = 1/f."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the magnification produced by a mirror comes out negative, the image is:","options":["Real and inverted","Virtual and erect","Always the same size as the object","Impossible — magnification can't be negative"],"answer":0,"explain":"A negative sign in magnification signals a real, inverted image."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A convex mirror has a radius of curvature of 3.00 m. What is its focal length, in m?","answer":1.5,"explain":"f = R/2 = 3.00/2 = 1.50 m."}]},{"id":"lr6","title":"Bus in the Mirror","icon":"🚌","kind":"lesson","story":"\"Objects in this mirror are closer than they appear\" — or are they? Time to run the numbers on the bus rumbling up behind you and the candle flame guttering in front of a cave mirror.","teach":[{"title":"Worked Example: The Rear-View Bus","body":"A convex mirror with R = 3.00 m (so f = +1.50 m) has a bus at u = -5.00 m. The mirror formula gives image distance v = +1.15 m — the bus's image forms 1.15 m behind the mirror.","visual":"🚌"},{"title":"Its Magnification","body":"For that bus, magnification m = -v/u = +0.23. The positive sign confirms the image is virtual and erect, and it is smaller than the real bus by a factor of 0.23.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Worked Example: The Candle","body":"A 4.0 cm candle flame sits at u = -25.0 cm in front of a concave mirror of f = -15.0 cm. The mirror formula gives v = -37.5 cm, and the image height works out to h' = -6.0 cm — real, inverted, and enlarged.","visual":"🕯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the rear-view mirror example (R = 3.00 m, bus at 5.00 m), at what distance behind the mirror does the bus's image form, in m?","answer":1.15,"explain":"Using 1/v = 1/f − 1/u with f = 1.50 m and u = -5.00 m gives v = +1.15 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In that same rear-view mirror example, what is the magnification (to 2 decimal places)?","answer":0.23,"explain":"m = -v/u = -(1.15)/(-5.00) = +0.23, a small, positive, virtual, erect image."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 4.0 cm object is placed 25.0 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15.0 cm. At what distance from the mirror, in cm, should the screen be placed for a sharp image?","answer":37.5,"explain":"1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/(-15.0) − 1/(-25.0) gives v = -37.5 cm, so the screen goes 37.5 cm in front of the mirror."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the candle-and-concave-mirror example above, the image is:","options":["Real, inverted, and enlarged (6.0 cm tall)","Virtual, erect, and diminished","Real, erect, and the same size","Virtual, inverted, and enlarged"],"answer":0,"explain":"The image height comes out to -6.0 cm: real, inverted, and bigger than the 4.0 cm object."}]},{"id":"lr7","title":"Bending Light: Refraction","icon":"🌈","kind":"lesson","story":"A coin glints at the bottom of a pool, but every time you reach for it, your hand misses — the coin isn't quite where it looks like it is. Light bends at the water's surface, and you need to learn its rules before you can trust your eyes again.","teach":[{"title":"Laws of Refraction","body":"When light passes obliquely from one transparent medium into another, it changes direction — this is refraction. The incident ray, refracted ray, and normal all lie in the same plane, and sin(i)/sin(r) is a constant for a given pair of media and colour of light — this is Snell's law.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Refractive Index","body":"The refractive index of a medium compares the speed of light in air/vacuum to its speed in that medium: n = c/v. A higher refractive index means light travels slower in that medium — it is optically denser. Diamond (n = 2.42) is far denser optically than air (n ≈ 1.0003).","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Which Way Does It Bend?","body":"Going from a rarer to a denser medium, light slows down and bends towards the normal. Going from denser to rarer, it speeds up and bends away from the normal. Kerosene (n = 1.44) is optically denser than water (n = 1.33) even though kerosene's mass density is lower — optical density and mass density are different things.","visual":"↩️"},{"title":"Through a Glass Slab","body":"Entering a rectangular glass slab, the ray bends toward the normal (air to glass); leaving it, the ray bends away from the normal by an equal amount (glass to air). The two bendings cancel out, so the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray, just shifted sideways.","visual":"🧊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Snell's law, for a given pair of media and colour of light, sin(i)/sin(r) is:","options":["A constant","Always equal to 1","Equal to the angle of incidence itself","Different for every angle of incidence"],"answer":0,"explain":"Snell's law states sin(i)/sin(r) is constant — the refractive index."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When light travels from air into water (a denser medium), it:","options":["Slows down and bends towards the normal","Speeds up and bends away from the normal","Travels at the same speed, without bending","Stops completely at the surface"],"answer":0,"explain":"Entering a denser medium, light slows and bends towards the normal."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Light enters glass of refractive index 1.50 from air, where it travels at 3×10^8 m/s. What is its speed in the glass, in units of 10^8 m/s?","answer":2,"explain":"v = c/n = 3/1.50 = 2 (×10^8 m/s)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using typical refractive index values, which medium is optically the densest?","options":["Diamond","Water","Ice","Air"],"answer":0,"explain":"Diamond has a refractive index of 2.42, far higher than water, ice or air."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Kerosene (n = 1.44) is optically denser than water (n = 1.33), even though kerosene's mass density is lower. What does 'optically denser' mean here?","options":["Light travels more slowly and bends more in kerosene than in water","Kerosene weighs more per litre than water","Kerosene is more transparent than water","Optical density and mass density always mean the same thing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Optical density tracks how much a medium slows and bends light, not its mass per volume."}]},{"id":"lr8","title":"Lenses: Convex and Concave","icon":"🔎","kind":"lesson","story":"A watchmaker's dusty workshop holds a magnifying glass and a strange thinner lens. Both bend light, but in opposite ways — learn to tell them apart before you try to read the tiny inscription hidden inside the pocket watch.","teach":[{"title":"Convex vs Concave Lens","body":"A lens is bound by at least one spherical surface. A double convex lens is thicker at the middle and converges light rays — a converging lens. A double concave lens is thicker at the edges and diverges light rays — a diverging lens.","visual":"🔎"},{"title":"Principal Focus of a Lens","body":"Rays parallel to the principal axis converge at the principal focus F2 after passing through a convex lens, or appear to diverge from focus F1 on a concave lens. A ray passing through the optical centre O of any lens carries straight on, without any deviation.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Convex Lens Images","body":"Object beyond 2F1 → diminished, real, inverted image between F2 and 2F2. At 2F1 → same-size, real, inverted image at 2F2. Between F1 and 2F1 → enlarged, real, inverted image beyond 2F2. At F1 → no image (forms at infinity). Between F1 and O → enlarged, virtual, erect image on the same side as the object.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Concave Lens Images","body":"A concave lens always forms a virtual, erect, diminished image on the same side as the object, no matter where the object is placed — it never forms a real image.","visual":"🔍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A double convex lens, thicker at the middle than at the edges, is also called a:","options":["Converging lens","Diverging lens","Plane lens","Prism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Convex lenses bring parallel rays together, so they are called converging lenses."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ray of light passing through the optical centre of a lens:","options":["Passes straight through without any deviation","Always bends towards the principal focus","Reflects back along the same path","Splits into two separate rays"],"answer":0,"explain":"The optical centre is the one point a ray crosses undeviated."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When an object is placed at 2F1 in front of a convex lens, the image formed is:","options":["Real, inverted, and the same size, at 2F2","Virtual, erect, and enlarged","Real, inverted, and highly diminished, at F2","No image forms at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"At 2F1, the convex lens forms a same-size, real, inverted image at 2F2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A concave lens, no matter where the object is placed, always forms an image that is:","options":["Virtual, erect, and diminished","Real, inverted, and enlarged","Real, erect, and the same size","Virtual, inverted, and diminished"],"answer":0,"explain":"A concave lens can never form a real image — it is always virtual, erect and smaller."}]},{"id":"lr9","title":"Power Play: Lens Formula","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"An old optician's spirit needs one last lens ground correctly to restore a wizard's failing eyesight. Get the formula and the power right, or the enchantment fails.","teach":[{"title":"Lens Formula and Magnification","body":"The lens formula 1/v − 1/u = 1/f relates object distance (u), image distance (v), and focal length (f) for any spherical lens. Magnification m = h'/h = v/u.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Sign Convention for Lenses","body":"Lenses use the same sign convention as mirrors, but all distances are measured from the optical centre. A convex lens has a positive focal length; a concave lens has a negative focal length.","visual":"➕➖"},{"title":"Power of a Lens","body":"Power P = 1/f (with f in metres) measures how strongly a lens converges or diverges light. Its SI unit is the dioptre (D); 1 D is the power of a lens with a 1 m focal length. A convex lens has positive power, a concave lens negative power, and lenses in contact add their powers: P = P1 + P2 + P3 + …","visual":"⚡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A concave lens of focal length 15 cm forms a virtual image 10 cm from the lens. Using the lens formula, at what distance from the lens, in cm, is the object placed?","answer":30,"explain":"1/u = 1/v − 1/f = 1/(-10) − 1/(-15) gives u = -30 cm, so the object sits 30 cm from the lens."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same concave lens, what is the magnification produced (to 2 decimal places)?","answer":0.33,"explain":"m = v/u = (-10)/(-30) = +0.33 — virtual, erect, one-third the object's size."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 2.0 cm tall object is placed 15 cm from a convex lens of focal length 10 cm. At what distance from the lens, in cm, does the real image form?","answer":30,"explain":"1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/10 + 1/(-15) gives v = +30 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In that same convex lens example, what is the height of the image, in cm (magnitude)?","answer":4,"explain":"h' = h × (v/u) = 2.0 × (30/-15) = -4.0 cm — a real, inverted image twice the object's size."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The power of a lens is the reciprocal of its focal length (in metres), measured in units of:","options":["Dioptre (D)","Watt (W)","Newton (N)","Metre (m)"],"answer":0,"explain":"P = 1/f, with f in metres, gives power in dioptres."}]},{"id":"lr10","title":"The Optics Overlord","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"Atop the Reflection Spire, the Optics Overlord guards the Lantern of Clarity behind a maze of mirrors and lenses. Every formula, every sign, every table you've learned is about to be tested at once.","boss":{"name":"The Optics Overlord","taunt":"You've bent a few rays and burned a little paper — but can you survive my whole Hall of Reflection and Refraction?"},"teach":[{"title":"Two Formulas, One Sign Apart","body":"Mirror formula: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f. Lens formula: 1/v − 1/u = 1/f. Mix them up and every answer flips.","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"Magnification, Mirror vs Lens","body":"For mirrors, m = -v/u. For lenses, m = v/u. In both cases, negative means real and inverted; positive means virtual and erect.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"The Always-Diminished Pair","body":"Convex mirrors and concave lenses always give virtual, erect, diminished images, whatever the object's position. Concave mirrors and convex lenses can give either real or virtual images, real or diminished or enlarged, depending on where the object sits.","visual":"🔮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An object is placed 10 cm in front of a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm. At what distance behind the mirror, in cm, does the image form?","answer":6,"explain":"1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/15 − 1/(-10) = 1/6, so v = +6 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An object 7.0 cm tall is placed 27 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 18 cm. At what distance from the mirror, in cm, should the screen be placed for a sharp image?","answer":54,"explain":"1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/(-18) − 1/(-27) = -1/54, so v = -54 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An object 5 cm tall is held 25 cm from a convex lens of focal length 10 cm. At what distance from the lens, in cm (to 1 decimal place), does the image form?","answer":16.7,"explain":"1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/10 + 1/(-25) = 3/50, so v ≈ 16.7 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the focal length, in cm, of a lens whose power is -2.0 D.","answer":50,"explain":"f = 1/P = 1/(-2.0) = -0.5 m = -50 cm; the magnitude is 50 cm, and it is a concave lens."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A doctor prescribes a corrective lens of power +1.5 D. What is its focal length, in cm (to 1 decimal place)?","answer":66.7,"explain":"f = 1/P = 1/1.5 ≈ 0.667 m ≈ 66.7 cm, a converging (convex) lens."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A spherical mirror and a thin spherical lens each have a focal length of -15 cm. What are they most likely to be?","options":["The mirror is concave and the lens is concave","The mirror is concave and the lens is convex","The mirror is convex and the lens is concave","Both are convex"],"answer":0,"explain":"A negative focal length means a concave mirror by the mirror sign convention, and a concave lens by the lens sign convention."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"You want an erect, enlarged, virtual image of an object using a spherical mirror. Which mirror, and where should the object go?","options":["A concave mirror, with the object between the pole and the focus","A convex mirror, with the object anywhere","A concave mirror, with the object beyond the centre of curvature","A plane mirror, placed close to the object"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only a concave mirror, with the object between P and F, gives an enlarged virtual, erect image."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do vehicles use convex mirrors for rear/side views instead of plane or concave mirrors?","options":["Convex mirrors give a wider field of view and always show an erect image","Concave mirrors are simply too expensive to fit","Plane mirrors always show inverted images","Convex mirrors magnify distant objects for extra clarity"],"answer":0,"explain":"The outward curve of a convex mirror widens the field of view while always keeping the image erect."}]}]},{"id":"humaneye10","index":9,"name":"The Human Eye and the Colourful World","subtitle":"Science Chapter 10","emoji":"👁️","palette":{"from":"#60a5fa","to":"#1e3a8a"},"lessons":[{"id":"he1","title":"The Eye as a Camera","icon":"📷","kind":"lesson","story":"You shrink down to the size of a photon and ride a beam of light straight into a stranger's eye. Your mission: map the incredible optical machine before the pupil closes on you.","teach":[{"title":"Cornea","body":"Light first enters the eye through the cornea, a thin transparent membrane forming the front bulge of the eyeball. Most of the refraction of light entering the eye happens right here, at the outer surface of the cornea.","visual":"👁️"},{"title":"Iris and Pupil","body":"Behind the cornea lies the iris, a dark muscular diaphragm that controls the size of the pupil. The pupil regulates and controls the amount of light entering the eye.","visual":"⚫"},{"title":"The Crystalline Lens","body":"The eye lens (crystalline lens) merely provides the finer adjustment of focal length needed to focus objects at different distances onto the retina.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Retina and Optic Nerve","body":"The eye lens forms an inverted real image of the object on the retina, a delicate membrane packed with light-sensitive cells. These cells generate electrical signals that travel to the brain via the optic nerve, which interprets them so we perceive objects as they are.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of the eye is responsible for most of the refraction of light entering the eye?","options":["The cornea","The iris","The crystalline lens","The retina"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most refraction happens at the outer surface of the cornea; the eye lens only fine-tunes the focal length."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the function of the iris?","options":["It controls the size of the pupil, regulating the amount of light entering the eye","It generates electrical signals for the brain","It bends light the most among all eye parts","It stores the image before it reaches the retina"],"answer":0,"explain":"The iris is a dark muscular diaphragm that controls how much light enters through the pupil."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The human eyeball is approximately spherical in shape with a diameter of about how many cm?","answer":2.3,"explain":"The chapter states the eyeball's diameter is about 2.3 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"On which structure does the eye lens form a real image of an object?","options":["Retina","Cornea","Iris","Pupil"],"answer":0,"explain":"The eye lens forms an inverted real image of the object on the retina, whose light-sensitive cells send signals to the brain."}]},{"id":"he2","title":"Power of Accommodation","icon":"💪","kind":"lesson","story":"The ciliary muscles are your engine room now. Flex them to reshape the eye's lens and pull faraway objects into focus before your vision blurs for good.","teach":[{"title":"What is Accommodation?","body":"The eye lens is a fibrous, jelly-like material whose curvature can be modified by the ciliary muscles. This ability of the eye lens to adjust its focal length is called accommodation.","visual":"💪"},{"title":"Relaxed vs Contracted Muscles","body":"When the ciliary muscles relax, the lens becomes thin and its focal length increases, letting us see distant objects clearly. When they contract, the lens thickens and its focal length decreases, letting us see nearby objects clearly.","visual":"👀"},{"title":"Near Point and Far Point","body":"The minimum distance at which objects can be seen most distinctly without strain is called the near point, or least distance of distinct vision, about 25 cm for a young adult. The far point is the farthest distance up to which the eye sees clearly, infinity for a normal eye.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Cataract","body":"At old age, the crystalline lens of some people becomes milky and cloudy, a condition called cataract, causing partial or complete loss of vision. Vision can be restored through cataract surgery.","visual":"🌫️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the near point (least distance of distinct vision), in cm, for a young adult with normal vision?","answer":25,"explain":"The near point of a normal young adult eye is about 25 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The far point of a normal eye is:","options":["Infinity","25 cm","2.5 m","1 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"A normal eye can see clearly all objects between 25 cm (near point) and infinity (far point)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The ability of the eye lens to adjust its focal length to focus on objects at different distances is called:","options":["Accommodation","Presbyopia","Dispersion","Scattering"],"answer":0,"explain":"This adjustment ability of the eye lens is called accommodation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A milky, cloudy crystalline lens in old age, causing loss of vision, is called:","options":["Cataract","Myopia","Hypermetropia","Presbyopia"],"answer":0,"explain":"This condition is called cataract and can be treated with cataract surgery."}]},{"id":"he3","title":"Myopia and Hypermetropia","icon":"👓","kind":"lesson","story":"Two villagers approach the Study Atlas: one squints at a distant mountain, the other struggles to read a scroll held close. Diagnose both eyes and forge the correct lens before nightfall.","teach":[{"title":"Myopia (Near-sightedness)","body":"In myopia, a person can see nearby objects clearly but not distant ones. The far point is nearer than infinity, and the image of a distant object forms in front of the retina. It is corrected using a concave lens of suitable power.","visual":"🔻"},{"title":"Causes of Myopia","body":"Myopia can arise due to excessive curvature of the eye lens, or elongation of the eyeball.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Hypermetropia (Far-sightedness)","body":"In hypermetropia, a person can see distant objects clearly but not nearby ones distinctly. The near point is farther away than the normal 25 cm, since light from a close object focuses behind the retina. It is corrected using a convex lens of appropriate power.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Causes of Hypermetropia","body":"Hypermetropia arises either because the focal length of the eye lens is too long, or because the eyeball has become too small.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a myopic eye, the image of a distant object forms:","options":["In front of the retina","Behind the retina","Exactly on the retina","On the cornea"],"answer":0,"explain":"In myopia the image of a distant object forms in front of the retina instead of on it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which lens corrects myopia?","options":["A concave lens of suitable power","A convex lens of suitable power","A cylindrical lens","A bifocal lens"],"answer":0,"explain":"A concave lens of suitable power brings the image back onto the retina, correcting myopia."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which lens corrects hypermetropia?","options":["A convex lens of appropriate power","A concave lens of suitable power","A cylindrical lens","A bifocal lens"],"answer":0,"explain":"A convex lens provides the extra focussing power needed to form the image on the retina."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The chapter's exercises describe a myopic person whose far point is 80 cm in front of the eye. What focal length (in cm, magnitude) must the corrective concave lens have?","answer":80,"explain":"For myopia, the concave lens's focal length must equal the far point distance (80 cm) so parallel rays from infinity appear to diverge from that point."}]},{"id":"he4","title":"Presbyopia and Bifocal Lenses","icon":"👓","kind":"lesson","story":"Years pass in the Atlas timeline. An elder approaches, holding her book at arm's length to read it. Master the third defect and craft a lens that serves both near and far.","teach":[{"title":"Presbyopia","body":"The power of accommodation usually decreases with ageing. In presbyopia, the near point gradually recedes away, making it difficult to see nearby objects comfortably without corrective eye-glasses. It arises due to gradual weakening of the ciliary muscles and diminishing flexibility of the eye lens.","visual":"👵"},{"title":"Bifocal Lenses","body":"Some people suffer from both myopia and hypermetropia and need bifocal lenses. A common type has an upper portion of concave lens for distant vision and a lower portion of convex lens for near vision.","visual":"👓"},{"title":"Modern Corrections","body":"Today, refractive defects of vision can also be corrected using contact lenses or through surgical interventions.","visual":"🔬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Presbyopia occurs due to:","options":["Gradual weakening of the ciliary muscles and diminishing flexibility of the eye lens with age","Excessive curvature of the eye lens","Elongation of the eyeball","Clouding of the cornea"],"answer":0,"explain":"Presbyopia is caused by ageing effects on the ciliary muscles and the eye lens's flexibility."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a common bifocal lens, which portion facilitates near vision?","options":["The lower, convex portion","The upper, concave portion","The lower, concave portion","The upper, convex portion"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lower convex part of a bifocal lens facilitates near vision; the upper concave part facilitates distant vision."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Besides spectacles, refractive defects of vision can also be corrected using:","options":["Contact lenses or surgical interventions","Only cataract surgery","Only bifocal lenses","Eye donation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter notes that contact lenses or surgery can also correct refractive defects."}]},{"id":"he5","title":"Prism Play and Dispersion","icon":"🌈","kind":"lesson","story":"A beam of captured sunlight sits inert in a chamber, guarded by a triangular crystal gate. Bend the light through the prism and unlock the seven-coloured seal, VIBGYOR, to proceed.","teach":[{"title":"Refraction Through a Prism","body":"A triangular glass prism has two triangular bases and three rectangular lateral surfaces; the angle between its two lateral faces is called the angle of the prism. Light bends towards the normal entering the glass and away from the normal leaving it, and the overall bending of the emergent ray from the incident ray's direction is called the angle of deviation.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Dispersion of White Light","body":"When white light passes through a prism, it splits into a band of colours called a spectrum: Violet, Indigo, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange and Red, remembered by the acronym VIBGYOR. This splitting of light into its component colours is called dispersion.","visual":"🌈"},{"title":"Why the Colours Separate","body":"Different colours of light bend through different angles as they pass through a prism. Red bends the least while violet bends the most, so each colour's ray emerges along a different path, making the colours distinct.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Newton's Recombination","body":"Isaac Newton passed the spectrum of white light through a second, identical prism held inverted, and recovered a beam of white light on the other side. This showed him that sunlight (white light) is made up of seven colours.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"The Rainbow","body":"A rainbow is a natural spectrum formed by dispersion of sunlight by tiny water droplets in the atmosphere. The droplets refract and disperse the sunlight, reflect it internally, and refract it again as it leaves the droplet, so different colours reach the observer's eye. A rainbow always forms in a direction opposite to the Sun.","visual":"🌦️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which acronym helps remember the sequence of colours in the spectrum of white light?","options":["VIBGYOR","RIGBYVO","VIGROBY","BYVIGRO"],"answer":0,"explain":"VIBGYOR stands for Violet, Indigo, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange, Red."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which colour bends the most when passing through a prism?","options":["Violet","Red","Green","Yellow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Violet light bends the most, while red bends the least, when passing through a prism."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The splitting of white light into its component colours is called:","options":["Dispersion","Deviation","Refraction","Reflection"],"answer":0,"explain":"This splitting of white light into its seven colours is called dispersion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A rainbow is formed due to:","options":["Dispersion of sunlight by tiny water droplets in the atmosphere, followed by internal reflection","Scattering of light by air molecules","Reflection of sunlight from clouds","Absorption of sunlight by water vapour"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water droplets refract and disperse sunlight, reflect it internally, and refract it again, sending different colours to the eye."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newton demonstrated that sunlight is made of seven colours by:","options":["Passing the spectrum through a second, inverted identical prism to recombine it into white light","Measuring the angle of deviation of each colour","Using a diffraction grating","Photographing a rainbow"],"answer":0,"explain":"The second inverted prism recombined the spectrum back into white light, proving sunlight contains all seven colours."}]},{"id":"he6","title":"Atmospheric Refraction","icon":"⭐","kind":"lesson","story":"Night falls over the Atlas. The stars above shimmer and dance. Chase down why they twinkle while the planets nearby hold perfectly still.","teach":[{"title":"Wavering Near a Flame","body":"Air just above a fire or radiator is hotter and less dense, with a slightly lower refractive index than the cooler air above it. Since these physical conditions keep changing, the apparent position of an object seen through this turbulent air fluctuates, causing wavering or flickering. This is atmospheric refraction on a small, local scale.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Twinkling of Stars","body":"Starlight bends continuously as it passes through the earth's atmosphere, a medium of gradually changing refractive index, and is bent towards the normal. Because atmospheric conditions keep changing, the star's apparent position and the amount of starlight reaching the eye keep fluctuating, producing the twinkling effect.","visual":"⭐"},{"title":"Why Planets Don't Twinkle","body":"Planets are much closer to Earth and are seen as extended sources, like a collection of many point-sized light sources. The light variations from all these individual points average out to zero, nullifying the twinkling effect.","visual":"🪐"},{"title":"Advance Sunrise, Delayed Sunset","body":"Due to atmospheric refraction, the Sun becomes visible about 2 minutes before actual sunrise, and remains visible about 2 minutes after actual sunset. The same effect also causes the apparent flattening of the Sun's disc at sunrise and sunset.","visual":"🌅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Stars twinkle mainly because of:","options":["Atmospheric refraction of starlight through air of continuously changing refractive index","Their own light output changing rapidly","Scattering of starlight by dust","Reflection of starlight off the Moon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Continuously changing atmospheric refraction makes the star's apparent position and brightness fluctuate."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why don't planets twinkle even though seen through the same atmosphere?","options":["Planets are seen as extended sources, and light variations from their many points average out to zero","Planets have their own steady light source","Planets are too close for refraction to occur","Planets are far larger than stars"],"answer":0,"explain":"Treating a planet as many point sources, the fluctuations from each point cancel out on average."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The Sun becomes visible to us about how many minutes before actual sunrise, due to atmospheric refraction?","answer":2,"explain":"Atmospheric refraction makes the Sun visible about 2 minutes before actual sunrise and 2 minutes after actual sunset."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The apparent flattening of the Sun's disc at sunrise and sunset is caused by:","options":["Atmospheric refraction","Scattering of light","Dispersion of light","Total internal reflection"],"answer":0,"explain":"The same atmospheric refraction that delays sunset also flattens the Sun's apparent disc near the horizon."}]},{"id":"he7","title":"Scattering of Light","icon":"🌌","kind":"lesson","story":"The sky above the Atlas glows an impossible blue, and the horizon burns orange at dusk. Uncover the scattering secrets hidden in the air itself before the colours fade.","teach":[{"title":"The Tyndall Effect","body":"The atmosphere is a mixture of fine particles: smoke, tiny water droplets, dust and air molecules. When light strikes such particles, it is scattered diffusely and its path becomes visible. This is the Tyndall effect, seen when sunlight enters a smoke-filled room through a small hole, or passes through a forest canopy where mist droplets scatter it.","visual":"💡"},{"title":"Particle Size Matters","body":"The colour of scattered light depends on the size of the scattering particles. Very fine particles scatter mainly blue light, particles of larger size scatter light of longer wavelengths, and sufficiently large particles scatter light of all wavelengths so it appears white.","visual":"🔎"},{"title":"Why the Sky is Blue","body":"Air molecules and fine particles are smaller than the wavelength of visible light, and are far more effective at scattering shorter wavelengths at the blue end than longer wavelengths at the red end (red light's wavelength is about 1.8 times that of blue). This scattered blue light reaching our eyes makes the clear sky look blue.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Dark Sky at High Altitude","body":"If the earth had no atmosphere, there would be no scattering and the sky would look dark. This is why the sky appears dark to passengers flying at very high altitudes, where scattering is not prominent.","visual":"🌌"},{"title":"Red Danger Signals","body":"Red light is scattered the least by fog or smoke, so a red 'danger' signal can still be seen in the same colour from a distance.","visual":"🚦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The clear sky appears blue mainly because:","options":["Fine particles in the atmosphere scatter shorter (blue) wavelengths of sunlight more strongly than longer (red) wavelengths","The sky reflects the blue oceans below","Blue light travels fastest through air","Air molecules absorb all colours except blue"],"answer":0,"explain":"Air molecules scatter blue light far more effectively than red light, and this scattered blue light reaches our eyes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The scattering of light by colloidal-sized particles, which makes a light beam's path visible, is called:","options":["The Tyndall effect","Dispersion","Atmospheric refraction","Accommodation"],"answer":0,"explain":"This scattering phenomenon is called the Tyndall effect."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Red light has a wavelength about how many times greater than blue light, according to the chapter?","answer":1.8,"explain":"The chapter states red light's wavelength is about 1.8 times that of blue light."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"'Danger' signal lights are red in colour because:","options":["Red light is the least scattered by fog or smoke, so it remains visible at a distance","Red is the brightest colour to the human eye","Red light travels fastest","Red pigment is the cheapest to produce"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since red is scattered least, a red signal stays visible in the same colour even through fog or smoke."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the sky appear dark instead of blue to passengers flying at very high altitudes?","options":["Because scattering of light is not prominent at such heights, where the atmosphere is thin","Because there is no sunlight at high altitude","Because clouds block the view","Because oxygen absorbs blue light"],"answer":0,"explain":"With very little atmosphere to scatter light at high altitude, the sky looks dark rather than blue."}]},{"id":"he8","title":"Boss: The Spectrum Sentinel","icon":"👁️🌈","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Atlas, a shifting guardian woven from scattered light and refracted shadow blocks the final gate. It shifts colour faster than the eye can follow. Only mastery of the whole chapter can stop it.","boss":{"name":"The Spectrum Sentinel","taunt":"Your pupils will never adjust fast enough to catch me!"},"teach":[{"title":"Chapter Recap","body":"From the cornea and retina, to the defects myopia, hypermetropia and presbyopia, to prisms splitting light into VIBGYOR, to twinkling stars and the blue sky, this chapter connects the eye's structure to the colourful phenomena of the world outside it.","visual":"👁️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of the eye directly controls the amount of light entering it?","options":["The pupil, regulated by the iris","The cornea","The retina","The optic nerve"],"answer":0,"explain":"The iris controls the size of the pupil, which regulates the amount of light entering the eye."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the least distance of distinct vision (near point), in cm, for a normal young adult?","answer":25,"explain":"The near point for a young adult with normal vision is about 25 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A person can see distant objects clearly but not nearby ones, since the near point has receded beyond 25 cm. This defect is:","options":["Hypermetropia","Myopia","Presbyopia","Cataract"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hypermetropia is far-sightedness, where the near point moves farther away than 25 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Myopia is corrected using a:","options":["Concave lens","Convex lens","Cylindrical lens","Bifocal lens with two convex parts"],"answer":0,"explain":"A concave lens of suitable power brings the image of distant objects back onto the retina."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The splitting of white light into VIBGYOR by a prism is called:","options":["Dispersion","Deviation","Scattering","Accommodation"],"answer":0,"explain":"This splitting of white light into its seven component colours is called dispersion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Stars twinkle but planets do not, mainly because:","options":["Planets are extended sources, so light fluctuations from their many points average out, unlike point-sized stars","Planets don't emit or reflect any light at all","Stars are actually closer to Earth than planets","Planets are unaffected by atmospheric refraction"],"answer":0,"explain":"A planet's light comes from many points that average out any twinkling; a star is effectively a single point source."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The sky looks blue mainly because air molecules:","options":["Scatter shorter (blue) wavelengths of sunlight more strongly than longer (red) wavelengths","Reflect blue light from the oceans below","Absorb red and yellow light completely","Emit blue light themselves"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fine particles in the air scatter blue light much more than red light, so scattered blue light fills the sky."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The chapter's exercises describe a myopic person whose far point is 80 cm in front of the eye. What focal length (in cm, magnitude) should the corrective concave lens have?","answer":80,"explain":"The concave lens's focal length must equal the far point distance of 80 cm to correct this myopia."}]}]},{"id":"electricity10","index":10,"name":"Electricity","subtitle":"Science Chapter 11","emoji":"⚡","palette":{"from":"#facc15","to":"#713f12"},"lessons":[{"id":"el1","title":"The Torch in the Dark","icon":"🔌","kind":"lesson","story":"Your torch has gone dark in a tunnel, and the only way to relight it is to understand what actually flows through its wire. You trace the path from the cell to the bulb and back, and realise you're chasing a stream of charge.","teach":[{"title":"Electric current","body":"If electric charge flows through a conductor, we say there is an electric current in it. Current is the rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t. In metallic wires, electrons carry the charge, but conventionally the direction of current is taken opposite to the direction of electron flow.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Units of charge and current","body":"The SI unit of charge is the coulomb (C), equal to the charge on about 6 × 10¹⁸ electrons. Current is measured in amperes (A): 1 A = 1 C flowing per second. Small currents are measured in milliampere (10⁻³ A) or microampere (10⁻⁶ A).","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"The ammeter and the circuit","body":"An ammeter measures current and is always connected in series in the circuit. A continuous, closed path along which current flows is called an electric circuit; breaking it anywhere (like opening a switch) stops the current.","visual":"📟"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which instrument measures electric current, and how must it be connected in a circuit?","options":["Ammeter, connected in series","Voltmeter, connected in series","Ammeter, connected in parallel","Galvanometer, connected in parallel"],"answer":0,"explain":"An ammeter measures current and must be connected in series so the same current passes through it."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A current of 0.5 A is drawn by a bulb filament for 10 minutes (600 s). How many coulombs of charge flow through the circuit? (Use Q = It)","answer":300,"explain":"Q = It = 0.5 A × 600 s = 300 C."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a metallic wire, the conventional direction of electric current is taken to be opposite to the direction of flow of","options":["electrons","protons","positive ions","neutrons"],"answer":0,"explain":"Current was defined as the flow of positive charge before electrons were discovered, so it points opposite to actual electron flow."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One coulomb of charge is approximately equal to the charge on how many electrons?","options":["6 × 10¹⁸","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹","6 × 10²³","1 × 10⁶"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each electron carries 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, so about 6 × 10¹⁸ electrons make up 1 C."}]},{"id":"el2","title":"Pressure of the Unseen River","icon":"🔋","kind":"lesson","story":"You find a still copper wire that refuses to carry any charge, just like water sitting in a perfectly level pipe. Only when you connect it to a cell — a tank held at a higher 'electric pressure' — does the current begin to flow.","teach":[{"title":"Potential difference","body":"Charges flow through a conductor only when there is a difference of electric pressure, called potential difference, along it — just as water flows only when there's a height difference. A cell or battery, powered by chemical energy, maintains this potential difference and keeps the current flowing.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Defining and measuring it","body":"Potential difference (V) between two points is the work done (W) to move a unit charge (Q) from one point to the other: V = W/Q. Its SI unit is the volt (V); 1 volt = 1 joule/1 coulomb.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"The voltmeter","body":"Potential difference is measured with a voltmeter, which is always connected in parallel across the two points being measured — unlike the ammeter, which goes in series.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does a voltmeter measure, and how is it connected in a circuit?","options":["Potential difference; connected in parallel","Current; connected in series","Potential difference; connected in series","Resistance; connected in parallel"],"answer":0,"explain":"A voltmeter measures potential difference and is always placed in parallel across the two points of interest."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How much work (in joules) is done in moving a charge of 2 C between two points with a potential difference of 12 V? (W = VQ)","answer":24,"explain":"W = VQ = 12 V × 2 C = 24 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How much energy (in joules) is given to each coulomb of charge passing through a 6 V battery?","answer":6,"explain":"By definition, 1 V = 1 J per coulomb, so a 6 V battery gives 6 J to each coulomb of charge."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What sets up the potential difference that drives charges around a circuit?","options":["A cell or battery, using its stored chemical energy","Gravity acting directly on electrons","The ammeter in the circuit","The switch, even when open"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chemical action inside a cell generates the potential difference across its terminals, expending chemical energy to keep current flowing."}]},{"id":"el3","title":"Sigils of the Circuit Map","icon":"🗺️","kind":"lesson","story":"A vault door is etched with strange sigils — a long line, a short line, a zigzag, a circle with a needle. To open it, you must read this treasure map for what it really is: a schematic circuit diagram.","teach":[{"title":"Why we use circuit symbols","body":"It's convenient to draw a schematic diagram in which each component — cell, battery, switch, resistor, bulb, ammeter, voltmeter — is shown using a standard, universally recognised symbol, rather than a realistic picture.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Cell vs battery","body":"A single electric cell is drawn with one long and one short parallel line. A battery is a combination of two or more cells joined together, and is drawn as a series of these long-short line pairs.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Reading the map correctly","body":"An ammeter symbol always sits in series in the loop; a voltmeter symbol always sits in parallel across a component. A rheostat (variable resistance) is used to regulate the current in a circuit without changing the voltage source.","visual":"🎛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A rheostat in a circuit is used to","options":["change resistance and regulate current without changing the voltage source","measure current directly","measure potential difference directly","store chemical energy like a cell"],"answer":0,"explain":"A rheostat is a variable resistance used to adjust current while the source voltage stays fixed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For current to flow at all, a circuit diagram must show","options":["a complete, unbroken conducting loop from one battery terminal back to the other","any loop, even if broken by an open switch","a voltmeter placed in series with the bulb","only wires crossing without joining"],"answer":0,"explain":"A continuous, closed path is called an electric circuit; if it is broken anywhere, current stops flowing."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a schematic circuit diagram, the battery symbol represents","options":["a combination of two or more electric cells","always exactly one single cell","a resistor of fixed value","a rheostat"],"answer":0,"explain":"A battery is a combination of cells, distinct from the single-cell symbol."}]},{"id":"el4","title":"Ohm's Reckoning","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"An old scale in the tunnel balances 'pressure' against 'flow'. Adding more cells raises the reading on one side, and the current on the other side rises in exact step — you've stumbled onto the law that governs every circuit ahead.","teach":[{"title":"Ohm's law","body":"The potential difference V across the ends of a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the current I flowing through it, provided its temperature stays the same: V ∝ I, or V = IR, where R is the resistance.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Resistance","body":"Resistance R = V/I is the property of a conductor that resists the flow of charge through it. Its SI unit is the ohm (Ω): 1 Ω = 1 volt / 1 ampere.","visual":"🚧"},{"title":"The inverse relationship","body":"From I = V/R, the current through a resistor is inversely proportional to its resistance — if resistance doubles, current halves for the same voltage. This is why a rheostat can be used to control current in a circuit.","visual":"🔀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electric bulb filament has resistance 1200 Ω and is connected to a 220 V source. What current (in A, to 2 decimals) does it draw? (I = V/R)","answer":0.18,"explain":"I = 220 V / 1200 Ω ≈ 0.18 A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A heater coil of resistance 100 Ω is connected to the same 220 V source. What current (in A) does it draw?","answer":2.2,"explain":"I = 220 V / 100 Ω = 2.2 A — much more than the bulb, since its resistance is much lower."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A heater draws 4 A at 60 V, so its resistance is 15 Ω. If the potential difference is increased to 120 V, what current (in A) will it now draw, assuming resistance is unchanged?","answer":8,"explain":"I = V/R = 120 V / 15 Ω = 8 A."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Ohm's law, if a conductor's resistance is doubled while the voltage across it stays the same, the current through it will","options":["become half","become double","stay the same","become four times as large"],"answer":0,"explain":"I = V/R, so doubling R halves I when V is unchanged."}]},{"id":"el5","title":"Forging the Right Wire","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"The blacksmith hands you spools of wire — thick, thin, long, short, copper, nichrome — and challenges you to forge exactly the resistor a device needs. Choose wrong, and the toaster in the corner either stays cold or bursts into flame.","teach":[{"title":"What resistance depends on","body":"The resistance of a uniform conductor depends on its length (l), its area of cross-section (A), and the material it's made of: R = ρl/A, where ρ (rho), the resistivity, is a constant for a given material. Resistance is directly proportional to length and inversely proportional to area of cross-section.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Resistivity ranges","body":"Resistivity's SI unit is Ω m. Metals and alloys have very low resistivity (10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁶ Ω m), making them good conductors; insulators like glass and rubber have resistivity as high as 10¹² to 10¹⁷ Ω m. Both resistance and resistivity change with temperature.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Choosing materials wisely","body":"Alloys like nichrome have higher resistivity than their constituent metals and don't oxidise readily at high temperatures, so they're used in electric irons and toasters. Tungsten, with its very high melting point, is used for bulb filaments, while low-resistivity copper and aluminium are used for transmission lines.","visual":"💡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are the heating coils of toasters and electric irons made of an alloy like nichrome rather than a pure metal?","options":["Alloys have higher resistivity and do not oxidise (burn) readily at high temperatures","Alloys are always cheaper than pure metals","Alloys conduct electricity better than any pure metal","Alloys have lower melting points than pure metals"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alloys resist oxidation at high temperature and have suitably high resistivity for heating elements."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If a wire's length is doubled while its area of cross-section and material stay the same, its resistance will","options":["double","halve","stay the same","quadruple"],"answer":0,"explain":"R is directly proportional to length (R = ρl/A), so doubling length doubles resistance."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A wire of resistance 4 Ω has length l and area of cross-section A. What is the resistance (in Ω) of another wire of the same material, with length l/2 and area of cross-section 2A?","answer":1,"explain":"R2 = ρ(l/2)/(2A) = (1/4)ρl/A = (1/4)R1 = (1/4)(4 Ω) = 1 Ω."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A 1 m metal wire of diameter 0.3 mm has resistance 26 Ω, giving a resistivity of 1.84 × 10⁻⁶ Ω m. Using the resistivity table, this metal is","options":["Manganese","Copper","Nichrome","Mercury"],"answer":0,"explain":"Manganese has a resistivity of 1.84 × 10⁻⁶ Ω m, matching this wire exactly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Copper and aluminium wires are commonly used for electricity transmission lines because they","options":["have low resistivity and are good conductors","are natural insulators","have extremely high resistivity","oxidise readily and are cheap to replace"],"answer":0,"explain":"Their low resistivity means less energy is wasted as heat while transmitting current over long distances."}]},{"id":"el6","title":"The Single-File Bridge","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"A narrow bridge of resistors forces every traveller to cross one after another in a single file — the same current must pass through each one. One collapsed plank, and the whole crossing shuts down.","teach":[{"title":"Same current everywhere","body":"When resistors are joined end to end, they are said to be connected in series. In a series combination, the current is the same through every resistor and every part of the circuit, no matter where the ammeter is placed.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Voltages add up","body":"The total potential difference across a series combination equals the sum of the potential differences across each resistor: V = V1 + V2 + V3. This gives an equivalent resistance Rs = R1 + R2 + R3, which is greater than any individual resistance.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"The catch with series circuits","body":"If one component in a series circuit fails or is removed, the whole circuit breaks and nothing works — like a string of fairy lights going dark from one dead bulb. It's also impractical to put appliances needing very different currents (like a bulb and a heater) in series.","visual":"💥"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electric lamp of resistance 20 Ω is connected in series with a 4 Ω conductor to a 6 V battery. What is the total resistance (in Ω) of the circuit?","answer":24,"explain":"Rs = R1 + R2 = 20 Ω + 4 Ω = 24 Ω."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same 6 V battery and 24 Ω total resistance, what current (in A) flows through the circuit?","answer":0.25,"explain":"I = V/Rs = 6 V / 24 Ω = 0.25 A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"With that 0.25 A current flowing, what is the potential difference (in V) across the 20 Ω lamp alone?","answer":5,"explain":"V1 = I × R1 = 0.25 A × 20 Ω = 5 V."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A major disadvantage of connecting electrical appliances in series is that","options":["if one component fails or is removed, the whole circuit breaks and nothing works","the total resistance of the circuit decreases","each device automatically gets the exact current it needs","voltmeters cannot be used anywhere in the circuit"],"answer":0,"explain":"A broken link anywhere in a series circuit stops current everywhere in that loop."}]},{"id":"el7","title":"The Branching Canals","icon":"🌐","kind":"lesson","story":"Instead of one bridge, you find a network of canals branching off the same source and rejoining downstream. Each canal carries its own share of the current, yet every branch feels the very same push of potential difference.","teach":[{"title":"Same voltage, split current","body":"When resistors are connected between the same two points, they are said to be connected in parallel. Each resistor then has the same potential difference V across it (equal to the source voltage), while the total current I splits into I1, I2, I3 through each branch: I = I1 + I2 + I3.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Combining resistances","body":"For resistors in parallel, the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance equals the sum of the reciprocals of each resistance: 1/Rp = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3. This means Rp is always smaller than the smallest individual resistor.","visual":"➗"},{"title":"Why we use parallel wiring at home","body":"In a parallel circuit, each appliance gets the full source voltage and draws only the current it needs, and one appliance failing doesn't switch off the others. This is why household circuits use parallel connections rather than series.","visual":"✅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Resistors R1 = 5 Ω, R2 = 10 Ω, R3 = 30 Ω are connected in parallel across a 12 V battery. What current (in A) flows through R1?","answer":2.4,"explain":"I1 = V/R1 = 12 V / 5 Ω = 2.4 A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In that same circuit, what current (in A) flows through R2 = 10 Ω?","answer":1.2,"explain":"I2 = V/R2 = 12 V / 10 Ω = 1.2 A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the total current (in A) drawn from the 12 V battery, summing the currents through all three resistors (2.4 A + 1.2 A + 0.4 A)?","answer":4,"explain":"I = I1 + I2 + I3 = 2.4 + 1.2 + 0.4 = 4 A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the total (equivalent) resistance (in Ω) of this parallel combination of 5 Ω, 10 Ω and 30 Ω?","answer":3,"explain":"1/Rp = 1/5 + 1/10 + 1/30 = 1/3, so Rp = 3 Ω — smaller than the smallest individual resistor."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"R1=10 Ω and R2=40 Ω are in parallel (giving 8 Ω), in series with R3=30 Ω, R4=20 Ω and R5=60 Ω in parallel (giving 10 Ω), across a 12 V battery. Total resistance is 18 Ω. What current (in A, to 2 decimals) flows in the circuit?","answer":0.67,"explain":"I = V/R = 12 V / 18 Ω ≈ 0.67 A."}]},{"id":"el8","title":"The Forge's Fury","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"The blacksmith's forge glows red-hot, powered purely by current fighting its way through resistance. A frayed wire nearby melts and snaps before the flames can spread — you've just watched a fuse do its one heroic job.","teach":[{"title":"The heating effect","body":"In a purely resistive circuit, all the electrical energy supplied by the source is dissipated as heat. If a current I flows through resistance R for time t at potential difference V, the source supplies energy VIt, all of which becomes heat: H = VIt.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Joule's law of heating","body":"Applying Ohm's law gives Joule's law: H = I²Rt. Heat produced is directly proportional to the square of the current, directly proportional to the resistance, and directly proportional to the time for which current flows.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Putting heat to work — and guarding against it","body":"Heating devices like irons, toasters and heaters use Joule heating directly, while bulb filaments (made of high-melting-point tungsten) glow because they retain heat. A fuse is a wire of an appropriate melting point placed in series with a circuit; if current exceeds a safe value, the fuse wire heats up, melts, and breaks the circuit, protecting the appliance.","visual":"🧯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electric iron consumes 840 W at 220 V when heating at the maximum rate. What current (in A, to 2 decimals) does it draw? (I = P/V)","answer":3.82,"explain":"I = 840 W / 220 V ≈ 3.82 A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using that current of 3.82 A at 220 V, what is the resistance (in Ω, to 1 decimal) of the iron at maximum heating?","answer":57.6,"explain":"R = V/I = 220 V / 3.82 A ≈ 57.6 Ω."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"100 J of heat is produced each second (t = 1 s) in a 4 Ω resistor. What is the potential difference (in V) across the resistor? (First find I = √(H/Rt), then V = IR)","answer":20,"explain":"I = √(100/(4×1)) = 5 A, so V = IR = 5 A × 4 Ω = 20 V."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is a fuse wire designed to melt and break the circuit when current exceeds a safe value?","options":["It has an appropriate melting point and is placed in series, so it melts first and protects the rest of the appliance","It is a perfect insulator that blocks all current permanently","It has zero resistance and never heats up","It boosts the current to a safer, higher value"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fuse wire's melting point is chosen so it heats up and breaks the circuit before other components are damaged."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Joule's law of heating, H = I²Rt, shows that heat produced in a resistor is directly proportional to","options":["the square of the current, the resistance, and the time","the current only, with no dependence on resistance","the square of the resistance only","the inverse of the time for which current flows"],"answer":0,"explain":"H = I²Rt depends on I squared, on R, and on t, all directly."}]},{"id":"el9","title":"The Kingdom's Power Bill","icon":"💡","kind":"lesson","story":"The royal treasurer hands you a stack of glowing meters from every corner of the kingdom's grid and asks you to work out exactly what the electricity is costing, appliance by appliance, before the treasury runs dry.","teach":[{"title":"Electric power","body":"Power is the rate of consuming energy. Electric power is given by P = VI, which can also be written P = I²R = V²/R. The SI unit of power is the watt (W): 1 W = 1 volt × 1 ampere.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"The commercial unit of energy","body":"Electrical energy is power multiplied by time. Since the watt is small, the commercial unit used for billing is the kilowatt hour (kWh), commonly called a 'unit': 1 kWh is the energy used by a 1000 W device running for 1 hour, equal to 3.6 × 10⁶ J.","visual":"🔌"},{"title":"Calculating the bill","body":"To find the cost of running an appliance, convert its total energy use into kWh, then multiply by the rate charged per kWh by the electricity provider.","visual":"💰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electric bulb connected to a 220 V source draws a current of 0.50 A. What is its power (in W)?","answer":110,"explain":"P = VI = 220 V × 0.50 A = 110 W."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 400 W refrigerator runs 8 hours a day for 30 days, using 96 kWh in total. What is the cost (in rupees) of this energy at Rs 3.00 per kWh?","answer":288,"explain":"Cost = 96 kWh × Rs 3.00/kWh = Rs 288."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electric motor takes 5 A from a 220 V line, giving a power of 1100 W. How much energy (in kWh) does it consume in 2 hours?","answer":2.2,"explain":"Energy = P × t = 1100 W × 2 h = 2200 Wh = 2.2 kWh."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An electric iron consuming 1 kW at 220 V draws about 4.54 A. Which standard fuse rating should be used for it?","options":["5 A","1 A","2 A","10 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the iron draws 4.54 A, the next higher standard fuse rating, 5 A, must be used."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One kilowatt hour (kWh) is equal to how many joules?","options":["3.6 × 10⁶ J","1000 J","3600 J","3.6 × 10³ J"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J."}]},{"id":"el10","title":"The Overload Golem's Gate","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Overload Golem","taunt":"Current, resistance, series, parallel, watts and coulombs — I am built from every circuit you've ever crossed. Resist ME if you can!"},"story":"At the final gate stands the Overload Golem, its body a tangle of every wire, resistor and fuse you've studied on this journey. It will only power down if you can out-calculate it, one circuit law at a time.","teach":[{"title":"Everything you've learned","body":"Current I = Q/t flows because of a potential difference V maintained by a cell, and Ohm's law V = IR links them through resistance R = ρl/A. Series resistors add directly (Rs = R1+R2+...), parallel resistors add as reciprocals (1/Rp = 1/R1+1/R2+...), and the heating effect H = I²Rt and power P = VI = I²R = V²/R govern every appliance in the kingdom's grid.","visual":"👑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 12 V battery is connected across an unknown resistor, giving a current of 2.5 mA (0.0025 A) in the circuit. What is the resistance (in Ω)?","answer":4800,"explain":"R = V/I = 12 V / 0.0025 A = 4800 Ω."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A wire of resistance R is cut into five equal parts, which are then connected in parallel. What is the ratio R/R' of the original resistance to this new equivalent resistance R'?","options":["25","5","1/5","1/25"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each part has resistance R/5; five of these in parallel give R' = R/25, so R/R' = 25."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following expressions does NOT correctly represent electrical power in a circuit?","options":["IR²","I²R","VI","V²/R"],"answer":0,"explain":"Power is P = VI = I²R = V²/R; the expression IR² is not one of the valid power formulas."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A bulb rated 220 V, 100 W (so its resistance is 484 Ω) is instead operated on 110 V. What power (in W) does it now consume? (P = V²/R)","answer":25,"explain":"P = 110² / 484 = 12100/484 = 25 W."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two identical wires are first connected in series, then in parallel, across the same potential difference. What is the ratio of heat produced in the series case to the parallel case, in the same time?","options":["1:4","4:1","1:2","2:1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Series doubles resistance and halves current, giving H ∝ V²t/(2R); parallel halves resistance, giving H ∝ 2V²t/R — a ratio of 1:4."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 9 V battery is connected in series with resistors of 0.2 Ω, 0.3 Ω, 0.4 Ω, 0.5 Ω and 12 Ω (total 13.4 Ω). What current (in A, to 2 decimals) flows through the 12 Ω resistor?","answer":0.67,"explain":"In series, the same current flows everywhere: I = 9 V / 13.4 Ω ≈ 0.67 A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many 176 Ω resistors, connected in parallel, are needed to draw 5 A from a 220 V line?","answer":4,"explain":"Required total resistance = V/I = 220/5 = 44 Ω. Since 176/44 = 4, four 176 Ω resistors in parallel are needed."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 100 W and a 60 W lamp, both rated at 220 V, are connected in parallel to a 220 V line. What total current (in A, to 2 decimals) is drawn from the line?","answer":0.73,"explain":"I = (100+60)/220 = 160/220 ≈ 0.73 A, since currents in parallel branches add up."}]}]},{"id":"magneticeffects10","index":11,"name":"Magnetic Effects of Electric Current","subtitle":"Science Chapter 12","emoji":"🧲","palette":{"from":"#f472b6","to":"#831843"},"lessons":[{"id":"me1","title":"Oersted's Spark","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Circuit Caverns, your compass suddenly spins wildly near a humming copper wire, as if some invisible hand were pushing it. Channel the spirit of Hans Christian Oersted and uncover the hidden link between electricity and magnetism before the cavern's ancient relics short out.","teach":[{"title":"Current makes magnetism","body":"When electric current flows through a metallic conductor, it produces a magnetic effect around it — an electric current-carrying wire behaves like a magnet. This was shown by a simple activity: a compass needle placed near a current-carrying copper wire gets deflected.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Oersted's accidental discovery","body":"In 1820, Hans Christian Oersted accidentally discovered that a compass needle got deflected when an electric current passed through a metallic wire placed nearby. This showed that electricity and magnetism are related phenomena.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"The compass needle is a tiny magnet","body":"A compass needle is itself a small bar magnet. The end that points towards north is called the north pole (north-seeking) and the end pointing towards south is the south pole (south-seeking). Like poles repel each other, while unlike poles attract.","visual":"🧲"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 1820, which scientist discovered that a compass needle deflects near a current-carrying wire?","options":["Hans Christian Oersted","Andre Marie Ampere","Michael Faraday","Isaac Newton"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oersted's accidental 1820 discovery revealed the link between electricity and magnetism."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to a compass needle placed near a wire when current is passed through the wire?","options":["It gets deflected","It melts","It stops working permanently","Nothing happens"],"answer":0,"explain":"The current through the wire produces a magnetic effect that deflects the compass needle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two magnetic poles that are alike (both north or both south) will:","options":["repel each other","attract each other","have no effect on each other","cancel out completely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Like poles repel, while unlike poles of magnets attract each other."}]},{"id":"me2","title":"The Field Line Grotto","icon":"🧲","kind":"lesson","story":"The Field Line Grotto is scattered with iron filings that swirl into ghostly curved patterns around a buried bar magnet. Trace the invisible field lines to find the safe path through the grotto — step on a spot where two lines would cross, and the compass loses its mind.","teach":[{"title":"What is a magnetic field?","body":"The region surrounding a magnet, in which the force of the magnet can be detected, is said to have a magnetic field. Iron filings sprinkled around a bar magnet arrange themselves along field lines, revealing the shape of this field.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Direction of field lines","body":"The direction of the magnetic field at a point is the direction a north pole of a compass needle would move there. By convention, field lines emerge from the north pole and merge at the south pole outside the magnet; inside the magnet they run from south pole to north pole, making the field lines closed curves.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Crowded lines, stronger field","body":"The relative strength of a magnetic field is shown by how close together the field lines are — the field is stronger where the lines are more crowded. No two field lines ever cross, because at a crossing point a compass needle would have to point in two directions at once, which is impossible.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The region around a magnet where its magnetic force can be detected is called the:","options":["magnetic field","electric field","gravitational field","current field"],"answer":0,"explain":"A magnetic field is the region surrounding a magnet in which its force can be detected."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Outside a bar magnet, magnetic field lines run from:","options":["north pole to south pole","south pole to north pole","east to west","centre outward only"],"answer":0,"explain":"By convention, field lines emerge from the north pole and merge at the south pole outside the magnet."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do magnetic field lines never cross each other?","options":["A compass at the crossing point would have to point in two directions, which is impossible","They repel each other like poles","They are too weak to cross","Field lines only exist near the poles"],"answer":0,"explain":"At an intersection the needle would need to point two ways at once, which cannot happen."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where field lines are drawn closer together, the magnetic field is:","options":["stronger","weaker","exactly zero","reversed in direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"The degree of closeness of field lines shows the relative strength of the magnetic field."}]},{"id":"me3","title":"Thumb Rule Tunnels","icon":"➡️","kind":"lesson","story":"A maze of straight copper wires snakes through the Thumb Rule Tunnels, each one hiding an unseen current. Curl your right hand around each wire like an ancient key to reveal which way its magnetic field wraps — get it backwards and the tunnel doors slam shut.","teach":[{"title":"Concentric circles around a wire","body":"A current-carrying straight conductor has a magnetic field around it shaped like concentric circles, centred on the wire and lying in a plane perpendicular to it. These circles get larger and larger as you move away from the wire.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Current and distance change the field","body":"The magnitude of the magnetic field produced at a point increases as the current through the wire increases, and decreases as the distance of the point from the wire increases. Reversing the direction of current also reverses the direction of the magnetic field.","visual":"🔼"},{"title":"The right-hand thumb rule","body":"Imagine holding a current-carrying straight conductor in your right hand so that your thumb points in the direction of the current. Your fingers then wrap around the conductor in the direction of the magnetic field lines. This is the right-hand thumb rule, also called Maxwell's corkscrew rule.","visual":"👍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The magnetic field lines around a long straight current-carrying wire form:","options":["concentric circles centred on the wire","straight lines parallel to the wire","radial lines pointing away from the wire","random scattered curves"],"answer":0,"explain":"The field consists of concentric circles centred on the wire, in a plane perpendicular to it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the right-hand thumb rule, if the thumb points in the direction of current, the curled fingers show the direction of the:","options":["magnetic field lines","current flow","force on the conductor","resistance"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fingers wrap around the conductor in the direction of the magnetic field lines."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As the current through a straight wire is increased, the magnetic field produced at a nearby point:","options":["increases","decreases","stays exactly the same","becomes zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"The magnitude of the magnetic field increases as the current through the wire increases."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As you move farther away from a current-carrying straight wire, the magnetic field:","options":["decreases","increases","stays the same","reverses direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"The field produced by a given current decreases as the distance from the wire increases."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The right-hand thumb rule is also known as:","options":["Maxwell's corkscrew rule","Fleming's left-hand rule","Oersted's rule","Ampere's swimmer rule"],"answer":0,"explain":"If a corkscrew is driven in the direction of the current, its rotation gives the field direction."}]},{"id":"me4","title":"The Ring Chamber","icon":"⭕","kind":"lesson","story":"At the heart of the Ring Chamber glows a circular loop of wire, its field lines curling into a single straight beam at the very centre. Count the coil's turns correctly to power the chamber's gate — too few turns, and the beam is too weak to open it.","teach":[{"title":"From straight wire to circular loop","body":"If a current-carrying straight wire is bent into a circular loop, the concentric circles of its field become larger and larger away from the wire, just as with a straight conductor. By the time you reach the centre of the loop, the arcs of these large circles appear as straight lines.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Every part of the loop adds up","body":"Applying the right-hand rule shows that every section of the current-carrying circular loop contributes to the magnetic field in the same direction within the loop, so the field at the centre is the combined effect of the whole loop.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"More turns, stronger field","body":"The magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire at a point depends directly on the current through it. So a circular coil with n turns produces a field n times as large as a single turn, because the field due to each turn adds up in the same direction.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the centre of a current-carrying circular loop, the magnetic field lines appear as:","options":["straight lines","tight concentric circles","random scattered curves","zero field"],"answer":0,"explain":"The arcs of the large circles around the loop appear as straight lines at its centre."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the magnetic field due to each turn of a circular coil add up?","options":["Because the current in each turn flows in the same direction","Because the turns are made of different materials","Because the turns cancel each other's field","Because the wire gets hotter with more turns"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the current in each turn has the same direction, the field due to each turn adds up."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A single-turn circular coil produces a magnetic field of strength 2 units at its centre. If the same coil is rewound with 5 turns carrying the same current, what is the new field strength (in the same units)?","answer":10,"explain":"A coil with n turns produces a field n times as large as a single turn, so 2 x 5 = 10."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A circular coil with 1 turn is rewound into a coil with 20 turns, carrying the same current. The magnetic field at its centre becomes:","options":["20 times as large","the same as before","half as large","reduced to zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"The field produced by a coil of n turns is n times as large as that of a single turn."}]},{"id":"me5","title":"The Solenoid Forge","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"The Solenoid Forge hums with a coil wound tight as a spring, strong enough to turn ordinary iron into a magnet on command. Master the electromagnet's secret and forge yourself a key of pure magnetism to unlock the next passage.","teach":[{"title":"What is a solenoid?","body":"A coil of many circular turns of insulated copper wire, wrapped closely in the shape of a cylinder, is called a solenoid.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"A solenoid behaves like a bar magnet","body":"The pattern of magnetic field lines around a current-carrying solenoid closely resembles the pattern around a bar magnet. One end of the solenoid behaves as a magnetic north pole, while the other behaves as a south pole.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"A uniform field inside","body":"Inside the solenoid, the field lines are parallel straight lines, which shows that the magnetic field is the same, or uniform, at all points inside it.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Making an electromagnet","body":"A strong magnetic field produced inside a solenoid can be used to magnetise a piece of magnetic material, like soft iron, placed inside the coil. The magnet formed this way is called an electromagnet.","visual":"🔩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A coil of many circular turns of insulated copper wire wound closely like a cylinder is called a:","options":["solenoid","rheostat","ammeter","resistor"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the definition of a solenoid."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Inside a long current-carrying solenoid, the magnetic field is:","options":["the same (uniform) at all points","zero everywhere","strongest only near the ends","increasing towards the ends"],"answer":0,"explain":"The parallel straight field lines inside a solenoid show the field is uniform there."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Placing a piece of soft iron inside a current-carrying solenoid creates a:","options":["electromagnet","permanent magnet needing no current","compass","electric fuse"],"answer":0,"explain":"The strong field inside the solenoid magnetises the soft iron, forming an electromagnet."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The magnetic field pattern around a current-carrying solenoid closely resembles that of a:","options":["bar magnet","single circular loop only","straight wire only","battery"],"answer":0,"explain":"One end of the solenoid acts like a north pole and the other like a south pole, just like a bar magnet."}]},{"id":"me6","title":"The Force Chamber","icon":"✋","kind":"lesson","story":"An aluminium rod hangs trembling between the poles of a horseshoe magnet in the Force Chamber — one jolt of current and it swings like a pendulum. Use Fleming's left hand to predict which way it will fly before the chamber's walls close in.","teach":[{"title":"A magnet pushes back on current","body":"We know a current-carrying conductor produces a magnetic field that exerts a force on a nearby magnet. Andre Marie Ampere suggested that the magnet must also exert an equal and opposite force on the current-carrying conductor.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"The suspended rod experiment","body":"In an activity, an aluminium rod carrying current between the poles of a horseshoe magnet gets displaced. Reversing the direction of current through the rod, or reversing the direction of the magnetic field, reverses the direction of this displacement.","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"When the force is largest","body":"The displacement of the rod, and hence the magnitude of the force, is largest when the direction of the current is at right angles to the direction of the magnetic field.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Fleming's left-hand rule","body":"Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of your left hand so they are mutually perpendicular. If the forefinger points in the direction of the magnetic field and the middle finger in the direction of the current, the thumb points in the direction of the force, or motion, of the conductor.","visual":"✋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Fleming's left-hand rule is used to find the direction of the:","options":["force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field","magnetic field around a straight wire","current through a solenoid","resistance of a conductor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fleming's left-hand rule gives the direction of the force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Fleming's left-hand rule, the forefinger points in the direction of the:","options":["magnetic field","current","force","resistance"],"answer":0,"explain":"The forefinger of the left hand represents the direction of the magnetic field."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Fleming's left-hand rule, the middle finger points in the direction of the:","options":["current","magnetic field","force","voltage"],"answer":0,"explain":"The middle finger of the left hand represents the direction of the current."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The force on a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field is largest when the current direction and the field direction are:","options":["at right angles (perpendicular) to each other","parallel to each other","anti-parallel to each other","at 45 degrees to each other"],"answer":0,"explain":"Experiments show the displacement (and thus the force) is largest when current and field are mutually perpendicular."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the suspended-rod activity, reversing the direction of current through the rod causes its displacement to:","options":["reverse direction","double in size","stop completely","stay exactly the same"],"answer":0,"explain":"The direction of the force, and so the displacement, reverses when the current direction is reversed."}]},{"id":"me7","title":"The Haunted Wiring Closet","icon":"🔌","kind":"lesson","story":"The old manor's Wiring Closet is a tangle of live, neutral, and earth wires waiting to short-circuit at the worst possible moment. Rewire it safely, respect the fuse, and keep the manor's lights burning without getting zapped.","teach":[{"title":"Live, neutral, and earth wires","body":"Domestic supply reaches homes through a live wire (red insulation) and a neutral wire (black insulation), with a potential difference of 220 V between them. A third wire, the earth wire (green insulation), is connected to a metal plate buried deep in the earth near the house, as a safety measure.","visual":"🔌"},{"title":"Two circuits, two ratings","body":"Homes often use two separate circuits: one of 15 A current rating for higher-power appliances such as geysers and air coolers, and another of 5 A current rating for bulbs and fans. Appliances are connected in parallel so each gets the same potential difference.","visual":"💡"},{"title":"Why we earth appliances","body":"The metallic body of an appliance is connected to the earth wire, which provides a low-resistance path for current. If current leaks to the metallic body, it keeps the body at earth's potential, so the user may not get a severe electric shock.","visual":"🛡️"},{"title":"Short circuits and fuses","body":"A short circuit happens when the live wire and neutral wire come into direct contact, causing the current in the circuit to abruptly increase. An electric fuse prevents damage from this overloading: the Joule heating in the fuse melts it, breaking the circuit before the appliance or wiring is harmed.","visual":"🔥"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the potential difference between the live and neutral wires in Indian domestic supply?","options":["220 V","110 V","440 V","12 V"],"answer":0,"explain":"In domestic circuits in India, the potential difference between live and neutral wires is 220 V."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The earth wire in a domestic circuit has insulation of which colour?","options":["green","red","black","blue"],"answer":0,"explain":"The earth wire has green insulation and is connected to a metal plate deep in the earth."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A short circuit occurs when:","options":["the live and neutral wires come into direct contact","a fuse melts safely as designed","too few appliances are connected to a socket","the earth wire is disconnected"],"answer":0,"explain":"Direct contact between the live and neutral wires causes the current to abruptly increase, causing a short circuit."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The main function of an electric fuse is to:","options":["break the circuit by melting when current becomes too high, preventing damage","increase the voltage supplied to appliances","store electrical energy for later use","reduce the frequency of the AC supply"],"answer":0,"explain":"Joule heating in the fuse melts it and breaks the circuit, protecting against overloading or short-circuiting."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A domestic circuit is rated for 5 A at 220 V. Using Power = Voltage x Current, what is the maximum power (in watts) that can safely be drawn from this circuit?","answer":1100,"explain":"220 V x 5 A = 1100 W is the maximum safe power on this circuit."}]},{"id":"me8","title":"Magnetron's Last Stand","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"At the core of the Circuit Caverns waits Magnetron, Warden of the Field Lines, wreathed in crackling solenoids and spinning compasses stolen from every chamber you crossed. Only a true master of magnetism and current can silence its roar and seal the caverns for good.","boss":{"name":"Magnetron, Warden of the Field Lines","taunt":"Your compass will spin in circles long after I'm done with you, little apprentice!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything you've learned","body":"You've traced field lines around bar magnets, curled your right hand around straight wires and loops, forged an electromagnet inside a solenoid, pointed Fleming's left hand at a force, and wired a home safely with live, neutral, and earth wires. Magnetron has studied all of it too.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which rule gives the direction of the magnetic field around a straight current-carrying conductor?","options":["Right-hand thumb rule","Fleming's left-hand rule","Lenz's law","Ohm's law"],"answer":0,"explain":"The right-hand thumb rule (Maxwell's corkscrew rule) gives the field direction around a straight conductor."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which rule gives the direction of the force on a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field?","options":["Fleming's left-hand rule","Right-hand thumb rule","Maxwell's corkscrew rule","Oersted's rule"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fleming's left-hand rule gives the direction of the force on the conductor."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Inside a bar magnet, the direction of the field lines runs from:","options":["south pole to north pole","north pole to south pole","east to west","there are no field lines inside a magnet"],"answer":0,"explain":"Field lines are closed curves: outside they go north to south, and inside the magnet, south to north."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The magnetic field pattern of a current-carrying solenoid resembles that of a:","options":["bar magnet","single straight wire","battery","electric fuse"],"answer":0,"explain":"One end of a solenoid behaves as a north pole and the other as a south pole, just like a bar magnet."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the time of a short circuit, the current in the circuit:","options":["increases heavily","reduces substantially","does not change","varies continuously with no pattern"],"answer":0,"explain":"When live and neutral wires touch directly, the current abruptly increases heavily, which is a short circuit."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The earth wire's purpose in a domestic circuit is to:","options":["provide a low-resistance path so leakage current does not shock the user","increase the voltage delivered to appliances","reduce electricity bills","increase the frequency of the AC supply"],"answer":0,"explain":"The earth wire keeps a metallic appliance body at earth's potential, protecting the user from severe shock."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A circular coil with 1 turn produces a magnetic field of strength 3 units at its centre. What is the field strength (in the same units) if the coil is rewound with 8 turns carrying the same current?","answer":24,"explain":"A coil of n turns gives n times the field of one turn, so 3 x 8 = 24."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two magnetic field lines can never cross each other because:","options":["a compass at the intersection point would have to point in two directions at once","field lines always travel in pairs","field lines are always exactly parallel","a magnet can only have one field line"],"answer":0,"explain":"At a crossing point, the field would have to point two ways simultaneously, which is impossible."}]}]},{"id":"ourenvironment10","index":12,"name":"Our Environment","subtitle":"Science Chapter 13","emoji":"🌍","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"oe1","title":"Ecosystem: Components of Life","icon":"🌳","kind":"lesson","story":"You crash-land in the Whispering Garden, a world buzzing with frogs, insects, and birds. To survive here, you must learn what makes this place a living system before the storm season arrives.","teach":[{"title":"What Is an Ecosystem?","body":"All the interacting organisms in an area, together with the non-living parts of their surroundings, form an ecosystem. A garden, forest, pond, or lake are all ecosystems where living things and physical factors interact and maintain a balance.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Biotic vs Abiotic","body":"Biotic components are the living organisms - plants, animals, microorganisms, and humans. Abiotic components are the non-living physical factors such as temperature, rainfall, wind, soil, and minerals that affect how organisms grow and behave.","visual":"🌦"},{"title":"Natural vs Human-made Ecosystems","body":"Forests, ponds, and lakes are natural ecosystems. Gardens, crop-fields, and even an aquarium are human-made (artificial) ecosystems - an aquarium can become self-sustaining once aquatic plants and animals are added.","visual":"🐠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these best defines an ecosystem?","options":["All the interacting organisms in an area together with the non-living components of their environment","Only the plants growing in one place","Only the animals living in a forest","A place with no living organisms"],"answer":0,"explain":"An ecosystem includes both biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) components interacting together."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is an abiotic component of an ecosystem?","options":["Soil minerals","Frogs","Insects","Birds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Abiotic components are physical factors like temperature, rainfall, wind, soil, and minerals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a human-made (artificial) ecosystem?","options":["An aquarium","A forest","A pond","A lake"],"answer":0,"explain":"Forests, ponds, and lakes are natural ecosystems; gardens, crop-fields, and aquariums are human-made."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What must be added to an aquarium to help it become self-sustaining?","options":["Aquatic plants and animals","Only fish food","Only an oxygen pump","Nothing, it sustains itself automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adding aquatic plants and animals along with oxygen and food can make an aquarium a self-sustaining ecosystem."}]},{"id":"oe2","title":"Producers, Consumers, Decomposers","icon":"🍄","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Whispering Garden you meet three guilds fighting for survival: the Sun-Feeders, the Hunters, and the Cleaners. Only by understanding their roles can you keep the garden from collapsing.","teach":[{"title":"Producers - the Energy Makers","body":"Green plants and certain bacteria that make organic compounds like sugar and starch from inorganic substances using sunlight and chlorophyll are called producers. They are the base of every food chain.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"Consumers - the Energy Users","body":"Organisms that depend on producers, directly or indirectly, for food are consumers. They are classed as herbivores, carnivores, omnivores, and parasites depending on what they eat.","visual":"🦌"},{"title":"Decomposers - the Recyclers","body":"Bacteria and fungi that break down the dead remains and waste products of organisms are decomposers. They convert complex organic substances into simple inorganic substances that return to the soil for plants to reuse.","visual":"🍄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which organisms are called producers?","options":["Green plants and certain bacteria that make food by photosynthesis","Only carnivorous animals","Only bacteria that decompose waste","Only herbivorous animals"],"answer":0,"explain":"Producers make organic food from inorganic substances using sunlight and chlorophyll, mainly through photosynthesis."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An organism that eats both plants and animals is called a:","options":["Omnivore","Herbivore","Carnivore","Decomposer"],"answer":0,"explain":"Omnivores consume both plant and animal matter, unlike herbivores (plants only) or carnivores (animals only)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main role of decomposers like bacteria and fungi?","options":["Breaking down dead remains and waste into simple substances that return to the soil","Making food using sunlight","Hunting other animals for food","Producing oxygen for the atmosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"Decomposers break down complex organic matter into simple inorganic substances used again by plants."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Without decomposers, what would most likely happen in an ecosystem?","options":["Dead matter and waste would pile up and nutrients would not return to the soil","Nothing would change at all","Producers would grow faster than ever","Consumers would stop needing food"],"answer":0,"explain":"Decomposers are essential for recycling nutrients back into the soil for producers to use again."}]},{"id":"oe3","title":"Food Chains and Trophic Levels","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"The Garden Council asks you to map who eats whom, step by step, so they can predict what happens if one link vanishes.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Food Chain?","body":"A food chain is a series of organisms feeding on one another, in a fixed order, transferring energy from one to the next - for example grass to goat to human.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Trophic Levels","body":"Each step in a food chain is a trophic level. Producers (autotrophs) form the first trophic level. Herbivores (primary consumers) are the second, small carnivores (secondary consumers) the third, and larger carnivores (tertiary consumers) the fourth.","visual":"🪜"},{"title":"Energy Enters at the Base","body":"Producers fix solar energy and make it available to heterotrophs. This energy then flows onward through herbivores, carnivores, and decomposers.","visual":"☀️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a trophic level?","options":["Each step or level of a food chain through which energy is transferred","The total number of species in an ecosystem","Only the level occupied by decomposers","The rate at which plants grow"],"answer":0,"explain":"A trophic level is each feeding step in a food chain."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the food chain grass -> goat -> human, which organism is the primary consumer?","options":["Goat","Grass","Human","None of them"],"answer":0,"explain":"The goat, a herbivore that eats grass directly, is the primary consumer (second trophic level)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which trophic level do producers occupy in a food chain?","options":["First","Second","Third","Fourth"],"answer":0,"explain":"Producers, or autotrophs, are always at the first trophic level, fixing solar energy."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the chain grass -> goat -> human, at which trophic level number does the human sit?","answer":3,"explain":"Grass is trophic level 1, goat (herbivore) is level 2, and human is level 3 in this chain."}]},{"id":"oe4","title":"Food Webs and Energy Flow","icon":"🕸","kind":"lesson","story":"A single straight path is too fragile for the Garden Council's plans - you must chart the whole web of who-eats-whom, and learn why energy fades as it climbs.","teach":[{"title":"From Chains to Webs","body":"Each organism is usually eaten by two or more other kinds of organisms, which in turn are eaten by several others. So instead of one straight line, the relationships form a branching network called a food web.","visual":"🕸"},{"title":"The 10% Rule","body":"Green plants capture only about 1% of the sunlight energy falling on their leaves and convert it to food energy. When one trophic level eats another, on average only about 10% of that food energy is turned into body mass and made available to the next level - the rest is lost mainly as heat.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Energy Flow Is One-Way","body":"Energy captured by autotrophs does not flow back to sunlight, and energy passed to herbivores does not return to autotrophs. Because so little usable energy remains after a few transfers, food chains generally have only three or four trophic levels, and there are usually far more individuals at the lower trophic levels.","visual":"➡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do food chains usually have only three or four trophic levels?","options":["Because so much energy is lost at each step that very little usable energy remains after that many transfers","Because there are not enough species on Earth","Because producers run out of sunlight after four levels","Because decomposers stop the chain after four levels"],"answer":0,"explain":"Roughly 90% of energy is lost at each trophic level, leaving too little energy to support many more levels."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"On average, about what percentage of the food energy consumed at one trophic level is passed on to the next trophic level?","answer":10,"explain":"About 10% of the energy an organism eats is converted into its own body mass and made available to the next consumer level."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"About what percentage of the sunlight energy falling on a terrestrial plant's leaves does it capture and convert into food energy?","answer":1,"explain":"Green plants capture roughly 1% of the solar energy that reaches their leaves."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement about the direction of energy flow in an ecosystem is correct?","options":["Energy flows unidirectionally from producers to consumers and does not return to earlier levels","Energy flows equally in both directions between all trophic levels","Energy flows only from consumers back to producers","Energy does not flow between trophic levels at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Once energy passes to a higher trophic level, it does not revert back to the previous level or to sunlight."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a food web give a more accurate picture of feeding relationships than a single food chain?","options":["Because each organism is typically eaten by more than one kind of organism, creating branching connections","Because a food web has no producers","Because a food web only shows decomposers","Because a food web ignores energy flow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Real feeding relationships branch out since organisms are eaten by, and eat, multiple other species, forming a web rather than a single line."}]},{"id":"oe5","title":"Biological Magnification","icon":"☠️","kind":"lesson","story":"Something is wrong in the Garden's ponds - a hidden poison is climbing invisibly up the food chain, growing stronger with every step. Trace it before it reaches the top.","teach":[{"title":"How Chemicals Enter the Food Chain","body":"Pesticides and other chemicals used to protect crops are washed into soil and water bodies. From there they are absorbed by plants along with water and minerals, or taken up by aquatic plants and animals, entering the food chain.","visual":"☠️"},{"title":"Accumulation at Each Level","body":"Because these chemicals are not degradable, they accumulate progressively at each trophic level as they pass up the food chain.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Biological Magnification","body":"Since human beings occupy the top level in most food chains, the maximum concentration of these harmful chemicals builds up in our bodies. This phenomenon is called biological magnification, and it is why foods like wheat, rice, vegetables, fruits, and meat contain pesticide residues that cannot always be removed by washing.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is biological magnification?","options":["The progressive increase in concentration of non-degradable chemicals as they pass up through trophic levels","The process by which plants make food using sunlight","The breakdown of dead organisms by decomposers","The natural growth of an animal's body size over its lifetime"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-degradable chemicals accumulate more and more at each successive trophic level, a process called biological magnification."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do human beings tend to accumulate the highest concentration of harmful chemicals like pesticides?","options":["Because humans usually occupy the top level of the food chain, where accumulated chemicals concentrate the most","Because humans eat only producers","Because humans have no consumers above them and this has no effect","Because pesticides only affect animals, never humans"],"answer":0,"explain":"As chemicals accumulate at each step up the food chain, the top consumer - often a human - ends up with the maximum concentration."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How do pesticides typically first enter a terrestrial food chain?","options":["They are washed into soil and absorbed by plants along with water and minerals","They are produced naturally by herbivores","They form spontaneously inside carnivores","They only appear inside decomposers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pesticides washed into soil are absorbed by plant roots along with water and minerals, entering the food chain at the producer level."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't pesticide residues in food always be removed by washing?","options":["Because the chemicals are not easily degradable and have already accumulated inside the tissues of the organism","Because washing destroys all nutrients in food","Because pesticides evaporate before reaching food","Because pesticides only stick to the outside packaging"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-degradable chemicals accumulate inside the organism's body tissues, so surface washing cannot fully remove them."}]},{"id":"oe6","title":"The Ozone Shield","icon":"🛡","kind":"lesson","story":"High above the Garden, an invisible shield is thinning. Master its chemistry before ultraviolet fire scorches every living thing below.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Ozone?","body":"Ozone (O3) is a molecule made of three oxygen atoms. Unlike the O2 we breathe, ozone is a deadly poison at ground level - but high in the atmosphere it performs an essential job: shielding Earth's surface from the Sun's ultraviolet (UV) radiation, which is known to cause skin cancer.","visual":"🛡"},{"title":"How Ozone Forms","body":"High-energy UV radiation splits some molecular oxygen (O2) into free oxygen atoms. These atoms then combine with molecular oxygen (O2) to form ozone (O3).","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"CFCs and the Hole in the Shield","body":"The amount of atmospheric ozone began dropping sharply in the 1980s, linked to synthetic chemicals called chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), used in refrigerants and fire extinguishers. In 1987 the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) forged an agreement to freeze CFC production at 1986 levels, and CFC-free refrigerators are now mandatory worldwide.","visual":"🧊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many oxygen atoms make up one molecule of ozone?","answer":3,"explain":"Ozone, O3, is composed of three oxygen atoms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What essential function does ozone perform high in the atmosphere?","options":["It shields the Earth's surface from harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiation from the Sun","It provides the oxygen we breathe","It causes rainfall","It traps heat to keep Earth warm as its primary role"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ozone in the upper atmosphere absorbs and blocks damaging UV radiation from reaching the surface."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which chemicals were linked to the sharp depletion of the ozone layer beginning in the 1980s?","options":["Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)","Carbon dioxide only","Oxygen molecules","Pesticides"],"answer":0,"explain":"CFCs, used as refrigerants and in fire extinguishers, were identified as the main cause of ozone depletion."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In what year did UNEP forge an agreement to freeze CFC production?","answer":1987,"explain":"The agreement to freeze CFC production was reached in 1987 under the United Nations Environment Programme."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is UV radiation from the Sun dangerous to living organisms?","options":["It is known to cause skin cancer in human beings","It directly causes plants to overproduce oxygen","It has no known effect on any organism","It only affects organisms living underwater"],"answer":0,"explain":"UV radiation is highly damaging and is known to cause skin cancer in humans."}]},{"id":"oe7","title":"Biodegradable vs Non-biodegradable Waste","icon":"🗑","kind":"lesson","story":"The Garden's paths are choking under mountains of trash. Learn what nature can break down - and what will haunt it for generations - before the whole ecosystem is buried.","teach":[{"title":"Why Some Waste Persists","body":"Enzymes that break down substances are specific in their action, so many human-made materials like plastics cannot be broken down by the enzymes of bacteria or other saprophytes. Under normal environmental conditions, such materials persist for a long time.","visual":"🧫"},{"title":"Biodegradable vs Non-biodegradable","body":"Substances broken down by biological processes are biodegradable. Substances that are not broken down this way are non-biodegradable - they may simply persist in the environment for a long time, or may harm members of the ecosystem.","visual":"🗑"},{"title":"Rising Waste, Rising Problems","body":"Improvements in lifestyle, more disposable products, and changes in packaging have led to greater amounts of waste, much of it non-biodegradable, causing serious environmental problems in towns, cities, and tourist spots.","visual":"🏙"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What makes a substance biodegradable?","options":["It can be broken down by biological processes such as the action of bacteria and other organisms","It dissolves instantly in water","It is made of plastic","It never decomposes under any condition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Biodegradable substances are those that biological processes can break down."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't bacteria break down materials like plastic?","options":["Because enzymes are specific in their action and the right enzymes for breaking down plastic are not available to these organisms","Because plastic contains no atoms","Because bacteria only eat producers","Because plastic is a biotic component"],"answer":0,"explain":"Enzymes act on specific substances, and bacteria lack enzymes suited to breaking down materials like plastics."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is most likely to be non-biodegradable?","options":["Plastic wrapper","Vegetable peel","Used tea leaves","Fruit peel"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plastic is a human-made material that persists in the environment because it is not broken down by biological processes, unlike organic kitchen waste."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What has contributed to the increase in non-biodegradable waste in recent times?","options":["Greater use of disposable products and changes in packaging","A decrease in human population","A ban on all plastic production worldwide","A reduction in daily waste generation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lifestyle changes toward more disposable items and different packaging have increased non-biodegradable waste."}]},{"id":"oe8","title":"The Final Reckoning: Our Environment","icon":"🌍","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the world lies the Warden of Balance, a colossal being built from every trophic level, every molecule of ozone, and every gram of discarded waste you've encountered. This is your last stand in the Science curriculum: prove that you understand how life, energy, and human choices hold the whole environment together.","teach":[{"title":"The Web of Life, Reviewed","body":"Every ecosystem is a balance of biotic and abiotic components, linked by food chains and webs where energy flows one-way from producers through consumers, losing about 90% of it at each step.","visual":"🌐"},{"title":"Human Impact, Reviewed","body":"Our choices - from pesticide use that causes biological magnification, to CFCs that thin the ozone shield, to mounting non-biodegradable waste - ripple through every trophic level of the environment.","visual":"⚠️"}],"boss":{"name":"Warden of the Balance","taunt":"Every trophic level you master feeds mine - show me you understand the whole chain, or watch this world's balance collapse."},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What forms an ecosystem?","options":["The interacting biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) components of an area","Only the plants in an area","Only weather patterns","Only the animals in an area"],"answer":0,"explain":"An ecosystem combines living organisms with non-living physical factors that interact together."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which trophic level always comes first in a food chain?","options":["Producers (autotrophs)","Primary consumers","Secondary consumers","Decomposers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Producers fix solar energy first, forming the base of every food chain."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"About what percentage of energy is transferred on average from one trophic level to the next?","answer":10,"explain":"Roughly 10% of the energy consumed is passed on to the next trophic level; the rest is lost mostly as heat."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the term for the progressive build-up of non-degradable chemicals as they move up the food chain?","options":["Biological magnification","Photosynthesis","Decomposition","Trophic cycling"],"answer":0,"explain":"Biological magnification describes chemicals like pesticides accumulating more at each higher trophic level."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What essential role does the ozone layer play for life on Earth?","options":["It shields the Earth's surface from harmful ultraviolet radiation from the Sun","It supplies breathable oxygen directly to organisms","It produces rainfall for ecosystems","It has no significant role in protecting life"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ozone high in the atmosphere blocks damaging UV radiation that can cause skin cancer."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which chemicals were responsible for the sharp depletion of the ozone layer starting in the 1980s?","options":["Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)","Pesticides","Decomposer enzymes","Molecular oxygen"],"answer":0,"explain":"CFCs used in refrigerants and fire extinguishers were identified as the key cause of ozone depletion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is non-biodegradable waste a serious environmental problem?","options":["It cannot be broken down by biological processes and persists in or harms the environment for a long time","It disappears within a day naturally","It always converts instantly into soil nutrients","It has no effect on ecosystems at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-biodegradable waste resists breakdown by bacteria and other decomposers, so it accumulates and can harm ecosystem members."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What would likely happen if all organisms in one middle trophic level (such as herbivores) were removed from an ecosystem?","options":["Both the producers below and the consumers above that level would be affected, disrupting the flow of energy through the food chain","Nothing would change since trophic levels are independent","Only the decomposers would be affected","The ozone layer would immediately be repaired"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing an entire trophic level breaks the flow of energy in the food chain, impacting levels both below and above it."}]}]}];